        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01HcjCpYKcEqTUE5R76mFmUf",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "453\t## Building\n454\t\n455\tRequires Go 1.26+ and curl 7.70+ (curl is the execution engine; `--write-out '%{json}'` is\n456\trequired).\n457\t\n458\t```sh\n459\tgo build -o talaria ./cmd/talaria\n460\t./talaria version\n461\t```\n462\t\n463\tThe version string defaults to `dev` and is stamped at release time:\n464\t\n465\t```sh\n466\tgo build -ldflags \"-X main.version=v0.1.0\" -o talaria ./cmd/talaria\n467\t```\n468\t\n469\tRun the tests with `go test ./...`.\n470\t\n471\t`internal/canary` is the leak suite: it builds the binary, drives every auth mechanism —\n472\tbearer, basic, and an API key in a header, a query parameter and a cookie — through every\n473\tcommand and every output format, and greps every byte talaria emits or writes for the injected\n474\tsecret, raw, percent-encoded and base64. It is the check that gates a release, so a failure\n475\tthere is a redaction regression, not a flaky test. Run it alone with\n476\t`go test ./internal/canary/...`.\n477\t\n478\t## Driving talaria from an agent\n479\t\n480\t[AGENT.md](AGENT.md) is the operating manual for the LLM on the other end: the\n481\tlist→search→describe→dry-run→call loop, the exit codes and what to do about each, and — the part\n482\tno other API client has to answer — how to name a credential you cannot see. Hand it to the model"
      }
    ]
  },
  "parent_tool_use_id": null,
  "session_id": "cb9f556c-0e08-449b-8b7f-cd6679344d44",
