    "content": [
      {
        "type": "tool_use",
        "id": "toolu_01Py9UwDgCdQhtiEQZu47p1x",
        "name": "Agent",
        "input": {
          "subagent_type": "general-purpose",
          "description": "Security review of branch",
          "run_in_background": false,
          "prompt": "You are the SECURITY reviewer for a code review of the git branch `ralph/design` in /home/casper/projects/talaria (Go CLI, cobra). Base branch is `main`. Review ONLY the branch diff: `git diff main...HEAD`. Do not report pre-existing issues outside the diff.\n\nThe project is `talaria` — an OpenAPI-driven CLI (\"Postman for agents\"). Read `docs/design/DESIGN.md` FIRST, especially §3 principle 0, §5 (Auth), and §5a (The credential firewall). **The credential firewall IS the product.** The core invariant: \"no consumer of stdout or stderr ever observes a credential value.\" Secrets must:\n- never appear on stdout/stderr, in dry-runs, emitted curl, error messages, history, corpus, JUnit reports, or debug logs\n- never be an argv element to curl (must go via `curl -K -` config document on curl's stdin, because /proc/*/cmdline is world-readable)\n- be structurally represented as `SecretRef{EnvVar: ...}` everywhere except `internal/curl` at exec time\n- be redacted at WRITE time for history/corpus, not read time\n- temp-file fallback for large bodies must be mode 0600 and deleted immediately after exec\n\nKey files to scrutinize: internal/secret/*.go, internal/curl/config.go, internal/curl/exec.go, internal/curl/render.go, internal/config/auth.go, internal/config/config.go, internal/corpus/store.go, internal/corpus/entry.go, cmd/talaria/call.go, cmd/talaria/auth.go, cmd/talaria/history.go, cmd/talaria/run.go, internal/output/junit.go, internal/clierr/clierr.go, internal/canary/surfaces.go, internal/spec/source.go.\n\nYour checklist:\n- **Credential leakage** (highest priority): trace every path a resolved secret value can take. Look for a resolved value reaching any output/serialization/error path. Check the redaction header matcher for gaps (case sensitivity, wildcard patterns like `*api*key*`/`*token*`/`*secret*`, Proxy-Authorization, Set-Cookie). Check query-string API keys are `<redacted>` in output URL fields. Check the canary suite in internal/canary actually covers every surface DESIGN.md §5a lists (json, pretty, tsv, dry-run, errors, junit, history store, corpus files, debug logs) — a surface it silently skips is a real finding.\n- **Injection**: argv construction via os/exec (never shell strings); the curl config document escaping (`data = \"...\"`, header values containing quotes/newlines/backslashes — can a crafted header or body value inject an arbitrary curl directive into the config document? e.g. a newline in a `--header` value adding `-o /etc/passwd` or `--upload-file`).\n- **Path traversal**: `--body @file`, `--fixtures dir/`, spec file paths, the local spec cache (internal/spec/source.go — is a URL-derived cache filename sanitized? can a crafted URL write outside the cache dir?), corpus paths.\n- **File permissions**: config file (design says mode 0600), history/corpus store files, temp files.\n- **SSRF / URL handling**: spec fetch by URL, `--base-url`.\n- **Secrets committed** in the diff (testdata, fixtures, CI workflow).\n- **CI workflow** (.github/workflows/ci.yml): unsafe use of untrusted PR input, overly broad permissions, script injection via `${{ }}` interpolation.\n\nVerify each candidate finding by actually reading the code and, where feasible, writing a tiny Go test or running the built binary (`go build ./... && ./talaria ...`) to confirm the failure. Do NOT report speculative issues you could not substantiate.\n\nReturn a list of findings. For each: title, severity (CRIT for exploitable leak/vuln or broken security functionality; WARN for missing hardening/validation; INFO for style), exact `path/to/file.go:LINE`, a description of what is wrong and why it matters, and a concrete suggested fix. If you confirmed it by running something, say what you ran and what you saw. Report nothing you did not verify."
        },
        "caller": {
          "type": "direct"
        }
      }
    ],
