          "description": "Concurrency review",
          "run_in_background": false,
          "prompt": "You are the CONCURRENCY reviewer for a code review of the git branch `ralph/design` in /home/casper/projects/talaria (Go CLI, cobra). Base branch is `main`. Review ONLY the branch diff: `git diff main...HEAD`. Do not report pre-existing issues outside the diff.\n\n`talaria` is an OpenAPI-driven CLI that shells out to system `curl` via os/exec. Read `docs/design/DESIGN.md` §5a for the subprocess/stdin ownership contract:\n> \"The Go process owns the real stdin. curl's stdin is always a fresh pipe that Go writes.\"\n`--body -` is read to EOF by the Go process from ITS stdin; curl is then spawned with a NEW pipe on its stdin carrying the `-K -` config document.\n\nKey files: internal/curl/exec.go, internal/curl/config.go, internal/curl/version.go, internal/corpus/store.go, cmd/talaria/run.go (smoke-test runner — check whether it runs operations concurrently), cmd/talaria/call.go, cmd/talaria/history.go, internal/spec/source.go (URL fetch + local cache).\n\nYour checklist:\n- **Subprocess handling in internal/curl/exec.go**: this is the highest-value area. Is the stdin pipe written and closed correctly — can a write to curl's stdin block forever if curl exits early (broken pipe / deadlock)? Are stdout and stderr drained concurrently with Wait, or can a large response deadlock on a full OS pipe buffer (the classic `cmd.StdoutPipe` + `Wait` bug)? Is `cmd.Wait` always called exactly once on every path including error paths? Are there goroutines writing to the stdin pipe that can leak if exec fails?\n- **Context cancellation**: is a context honoured for the curl subprocess (timeout / Ctrl-C)? If `exec.CommandContext` is used, is the process actually killed and reaped? If no timeout exists at all for a network call, judge whether that is a real defect for this tool.\n- **Goroutine leaks**: every spawned goroutine must be guaranteed to exit. Check any use of `go func`, `sync.WaitGroup`, channels, `errgroup`, and `time.After` in loops.\n- **Data races on shared state**: unsynchronized map/slice writes from multiple goroutines. If `cmd/talaria/run.go` executes operations in parallel, check the results collection, the corpus writer, and any shared spec/index model for races. NOTE: `pb33f/libopenapi` document models are generally NOT safe for concurrent mutation — check whether a shared model is touched from multiple goroutines.\n- **File/store concurrency**: internal/corpus/store.go — concurrent appends from multiple processes or goroutines; is writing atomic (temp file + rename) or can a crash/interleave corrupt the store? Is there a lock, and is it released on every path (defer)? Same question for the spec cache in internal/spec/source.go — two concurrent talaria processes fetching the same spec URL.\n- **Temp files**: created, permissioned, and removed on ALL paths including panics and early returns (defer).\n- **Errors swallowed** instead of propagated — especially ignored return values from Close/Write/Wait and `_ =` assignments that discard a real failure.\n- **Resource cleanup**: file handles, HTTP response bodies (`defer resp.Body.Close()`), pipes.\n\nVerify by reading the code and by RUNNING the race detector: `go test -race ./... 2>&1 | tail -40` and, if `run.go` is concurrent, `go test -race -count=5 ./cmd/... ./internal/corpus/...`. Also consider writing a small throwaway test in /tmp to prove a suspected deadlock (e.g. a large response body through the executor). Report what you ran and what you saw.\n\nDo NOT report speculative issues you could not substantiate. Sequential code that merely *could* be parallelized is not a finding.\n\nReturn a list of findings. For each: title, severity (CRIT for a race, deadlock, leak, or swallowed error that breaks functionality; WARN for a robustness/cleanup gap; INFO for style), exact `path/to/file.go:LINE`, description of the failure mode, and a concrete suggested fix."
        },
