          "description": "Integration review",
          "run_in_background": false,
          "prompt": "You are the INTEGRATION reviewer for a code review of the git branch `ralph/design` in /home/casper/projects/talaria (Go CLI, cobra). Base branch is `main`. Review ONLY the branch diff: `git diff main...HEAD`. Do not report pre-existing issues outside the diff.\n\n`talaria` is an OpenAPI-driven CLI. The full loop is: load spec (file/URL/cache, Swagger 2.0 converted to OpenAPI 3.x) → build operation model + index → bind params/body into a symbolic `Request` (secrets as `SecretRef`) → render curl / build a `curl -K -` config document → exec system curl → parse response → redact → validate against spec → write to the corpus/history store → render output (json/pretty/tsv/junit). Read `docs/design/DESIGN.md` (esp. §5 Architecture, §5a) for the intended data flow.\n\nYour job is to verify data actually FLOWS correctly between components — do not assume, trace and run it.\n\nYour checklist:\n- **Does data produced by one component actually reach the next, intact?** Trace concrete end-to-end paths and check for fields that are populated on one side but dropped, renamed, or ignored on the other. High-value seams:\n  - `internal/spec` (incl. the Swagger 2.0 → 3.x conversion in convert.go) → `internal/operation` extraction: do converted 2.0 specs produce the same operation model shape as native 3.x? Do security schemes, params (esp. `formData`/`body` params which have no 3.x equivalent), consumes/produces, and response contracts survive conversion?\n  - `internal/operation` → `internal/request` binding: required params, path/query/header/cookie param locations, request body content-type selection.\n  - `internal/request` → `internal/curl/config.go` and `render.go`: does the config document ACTUALLY carry every field the symbolic Request holds (method, url, all headers, query, body, auth)? Does the rendered/dry-run curl match what the executor would really send? A dry-run that differs from the real call is a serious finding — verify by comparing the two code paths for the same Request.\n  - `internal/curl/exec.go` response parsing (curl `--write-out '%{json}'`) → the output envelope: status, headers, timing_ms, body.\n  - response → `internal/validate` → the `validation` block in output, and → `internal/corpus` history entries.\n  - `internal/gen` + `internal/gen/fixtures.go` → `cmd/talaria/run.go`: does the documented test-data priority chain (spec example/examples → `--fixtures` by operationId → generated) actually take effect at runtime, in that order?\n  - `cmd/talaria/run.go` → `internal/output/junit.go`: do failures/errors actually land in the JUnit XML with correct counts, and is the XML well-formed and escaped for bodies containing `<`, `&`, control characters?\n  - `history replay` (cmd/talaria/history.go): does a stored entry round-trip back into a real re-issued request identically (params, body, headers, base-url, auth refs re-resolved rather than replayed as redacted placeholders)? Replaying a `<redacted:...>` literal as a real header value would be a serious bug — check it.\n- **Environment parity**: does the code behave the same in tests as at runtime? Look for test-only hooks, injected fakes, or stubbed curl paths that mean the tested path is NOT the production path — especially in internal/curl (is the real `curl` binary exercised anywhere, or is it always stubbed?) and in the e2e tests (internal/e2e/). Check whether TTY detection, env-var reading, and HOME/XDG path resolution behave the same under test as in a real shell.\n- **Chain completeness**: does every step that should fire actually fire? E.g. is a history entry written for EVERY call (including failures, 4xx/5xx, and dry-runs — and should it be?); is the query-string-API-key warning emitted once as DESIGN.md §5a says; is the curl version preflight actually invoked before execution from every command that executes (call, run, history replay).\n- **CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml)**: does the workflow actually run the full suite the design requires (build, lint, test, and specifically the canary leak suite that DESIGN.md §5a says \"gates every release\")? A CI job that silently skips a package or whose lint step cannot fail is a real finding — note `gofmt -l .` exits 0 even when files are unformatted.\n\nVerify by BUILDING and RUNNING: `go build -o /tmp/talaria ./cmd/talaria`, then exercise real commands against `cmd/talaria/testdata/*.yaml` and `internal/e2e/testdata/*` (you can stand up a local HTTP server in Go or python3 to act as the API for real `call`/`run` invocations — real curl is available, version 8.14.1). Compare `--dry-run` output against real execution. Report exactly what you ran and what you saw.\n\nDo NOT report speculative issues you could not substantiate.\n\nReturn a list of findings. For each: title, severity (CRIT for data that is silently dropped/corrupted between components or a broken chain; WARN for parity/robustness gaps; INFO for style), exact `path/to/file.go:LINE`, description of the failure mode with the concrete inputs that trigger it, and a concrete suggested fix."
        },
