  "output_file": "/tmp/claude-1000/-home-casper-projects-talaria/40743458-5293-43fb-a819-2616e10ba7ee/tasks/a8b1ce58630c308e8.output",
  "summary": "I reviewed the branch diff, read the design doc, and verified each candidate by building and running the binary. Findings below; everything reported was reproduced.\n\n---\n\n## CRIT 1 — Any spec can make talaria send arbitrary bytes to an arbitrary TCP host/port (curl protocol smuggling)\n\n**`internal/request/build.go:141`**\n\n`binder.baseURL` validates only that the URL parses and has a non-empty scheme and host. The error message claims \"is not an absolute http(s) URL\", but the scheme is never checked. The value flows into `document.build`'s `url = \"...\"` directive, and `internal/curl/config.go` sets no `proto`/`proto-redir` restriction, so system curl will speak whatever protocol the scheme names — `gopher`, `dict`, `file`, `ftp`, `smb`, `telnet`, `ldap`.\n\nThe candidate list includes `firstServer(b.in.Doc)` — the spec's own `servers[0].url`. The product's premise is that an agent points this at *any* OpenAPI doc it found, so that field is untrusted input. It also completely bypasses the `--allow-mutations` safety rail, since the rail gates the HTTP *method* and gopher has none.\n\n**Verified.** I wrote a spec with `servers: [{url: \"gopher://127.0.0.1:18201\"}]` and a path of `/_SET%20talaria%20pwned`, pointed a raw TCP listener at that port, and ran `talaria call /tmp/evilspec3.yaml getStats --output json` — no `--base-url`, no `--allow-mutations`. The listener received exactly:\n\n```\nb'SET talaria pwned\\r\\n'\n```\n\nThat is the classic Redis/memcached SSRF gadget with an attacker-controlled payload. I also confirmed `file://localhost/etc` is accepted and curl reads the file (the content does not reach stdout, because `readResponse` rejects `http_code == 0` first, but the read happens).\n\n**Fix:** in `baseURL`, reject anything whose scheme is not `http` or `https` — make the check match what the message already promises. Defence in depth: add `proto = \"=http,https\"` and `proto-redir = \"=http,https\"` directives in `document.build`. `cmd/talaria/history.go:494` rebuilds `BaseURL` from a stored URL with the same absent check and should get the same guard.\n\n---\n\n## CRIT 2 — A credential in a query parameter is written unredacted to stdout, the emitted curl, and the permanent history store\n\n**`internal/request/build.go:76`**\n\n`Build` applies `hide()` — the §5a built-in name matcher — to `req.Headers` (line 77) and `req.Cookies` (line 78), but not to `req.Query` (line 76). `hide`'s own doc comment says the rule applies \"whether the value came from a spec's security scheme, a profile, a bound parameter or the flag\"; query is the one location where it silently does not. DESIGN §5a's leak-channel table is explicit that query-string API keys must be `<redacted>` in output URL fields.\n\n**Verified.** Dry run:\n\n```\n$ talaria call canary.yaml getPublic --base-url http://127.0.0.1:9 \\\n    --query api_key=SUPERSECRET123 --header X-Api-Key=SUPERSECRET456 --output json --dry-run\n{\"request\":{\"curl\":\"curl -s -H 'X-Api-Key: <redacted>' 'http://127.0.0.1:9/public?api_key=SUPERSECRET123'\",\n \"url\":\"http://127.0.0.1:9/public?api_key=SUPERSECRET123\",\"headers\":{\"X-Api-Key\":\"<redacted>\"}}}\n```\n\nThe header is redacted; the identically-named query key is not — in the `curl` field and the `url` field. Then a real call against a local server, with `XDG_STATE_HOME` isolated:\n\n```\n$ cat $XDG_STATE_HOME/talaria/history.jsonl\n{\"timestamp\":\"...\",\"source\":\"call\",\"operation_id\":\"getPublic\",\"method\":\"GET\",\n \"url\":\"http://127.0.0.1:18099/public?api_key=SUPERSECRET123\",...}\n```\n\nThe value lands verbatim in the on-disk store that §5a calls \"a permanent artifact — the highest-risk surface in the tool\", where redaction is supposed to happen at write time. The same value also reaches `validate.Input.URL` via `curl.URL(req)`, so a validation error message can quote it too.\n\nThe canary suite does not catch this: `TestAUserSuppliedHeaderIsRedactedLikeASpecCredential` covers `--header` in three spellings and has no `--query` counterpart.\n\n**Fix:** `req.Query = hide(append(b.located(bound, inQuery), b.pairs(in.Query, \"--query\")...))`. Note the display consequence — `urlWord`/`QueryString` branch on `IsSecret()`, not `IsSensitive()`, so a hidden literal currently renders as `%3Credacted%3E`; `urlWord` (`internal/curl/render.go:139`) and `Request.QueryString` (`internal/request/request.go:238`) should skip `url.QueryEscape` for a sensitive value. Add a `--query` case to the canary header test so it stays fixed.\n\n---\n\n## WARN 3 — libopenapi writes structured logs directly to `os.Stdout`, outside talaria's renderer and envelope\n\n**`internal/spec/load.go:49`**\n\n`libopenapi.NewDocument(data)` leaves `d.config == nil`, so `BuildV3Model` falls back to `datamodel.NewDocumentConfiguration()`, whose default `Logger` is `slog.New(slog.NewJSONHandler(os.Stdout, ...))` (confirmed at `libopenapi@v0.38.7/datamodel/document_config.go:233`). That writer is the real process stdout — it bypasses `cmd.OutOrStdout()`, the `talaria/v1` envelope, and every redaction path.\n\n**Verified.** A spec with an unresolvable remote `$ref`:\n\n```\n$ talaria list /tmp/reftest.yaml --output json\n{\"time\":\"...\",\"level\":\"ERROR\",\"msg\":\"unable to open the rolodex file, ...\",\"file\":\"https://example.invalid/schema.json\",\"error\":\"...\"}\n{\"time\":\"...\",\"level\":\"ERROR\",\"msg\":\"error building path item: schema build failed: reference ... at line 14, col 23\"}\n{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":3,\"message\":\"...\"}}\n```\n\nThree JSON documents on stdout where the contract promises one, plus the structured error which is supposed to be on stderr. An agent parsing stdout breaks. It is also an unaudited third-party output channel in a tool whose whole invariant is about what reaches stdout.\n\n(Good news confirmed while checking this: `AllowFileReferences` and `AllowRemoteReferences` both default to false, so a hostile spec's `$ref` cannot read local files or fetch remote URLs.)\n\n**Fix:** use `libopenapi.NewDocumentWithConfiguration` with a config whose `Logger` writes to `io.Discard` or to stderr.\n\n---\n\n## WARN 4 — The canary suite still skips the response-validation error path on a stale premise\n\n**`internal/canary/canary_test.go:424`**\n\n```go\n// A response-validation failure is not here because response validation is\n// not built yet (plan tasks 26 and 27). Whoever adds --fail-on-error adds\n// the case; the stages that exist are all covered.\n```\n\n`--fail-on-error` shipped on this same branch (`cmd/talaria/call.go:213`, `cmd/talaria/run.go:147`), `internal/validate` exists, and `internal/e2e/e2e_test.go` exercises the flag in four places. The comment is now false, and `TestErrorPathsDoNotLeakTheCredential` still has no exit-4 stage — leaving DESIGN §5a's named surface \"validation errors quoting the request\" uncovered by the gate that is supposed to fail the build. A comment that documents a deliberate gap is fine; one that documents a gap on grounds that no longer hold is how a gate quietly stops covering something.\n\nI traced the path and believe it is currently clean — `validateResponse` runs on the redacted `responseView` and on `curl.URL(req)` — *except* for the query-parameter case in finding 2, which this stage would have caught.\n\n**Fix:** add a `{name: \"response validation\", args: ..., code: 4}` stage driving `call --fail-on-error` against a server returning a schema-violating body, and delete the stale comment.\n\n---\n\n## INFO 5 — `authorization` is an exact-match pattern, so `X-Authorization` and `Authentication` are not redacted\n\n**`internal/secret/redact.go:20`**\n\n`compileGlob` anchors a pattern with no `*` to the whole name, so `authorization` matches only the exact header. Common non-standard spellings fall through.\n\n**Verified:**\n\n```\n$ talaria call ... --header 'X-Authorization=Bearer SEKRIT1' --header 'X-Auth=SEKRIT2' \\\n    --header 'Authentication=SEKRIT3' --output json --dry-run\n\"headers\":{\"Authentication\":\"SEKRIT3\",\"X-Auth\":\"SEKRIT2\",\"X-Authorization\":\"Bearer SEKRIT1\"}\n```\n\nThis matches DESIGN §5a's list literally, so it is a design choice rather than an implementation bug, and `redact.headers` in the config file is the sanctioned escape hatch. But `*auth*` would subsume `authorization`, `proxy-authorization`, `x-authorization`, `x-auth-token` and `authentication` at no cost beyond a few harmless false positives, and the built-in list is explicitly the non-configurable floor.\n\n---\n\n## Checked and clean\n\n- **Curl config-document injection.** `escapeDirective` uses a single-pass `strings.Replacer` covering `\\ \" \\t \\n \\r`, which correctly avoids the double-escaping bug. I ran a real call with `--header $'X-Evil=a\\noutput = \"/tmp/pwned.txt\"\\nupload-file = \"/etc/passwd\"'`: the newlines were escaped, no directive was injected, and `/tmp/pwned.txt` was never created. The emitted-curl shell quoting (`word.String`, single-quote with `'\\''`, double-quote escaping `\\ \" $ backtick` only when a `$VAR` reference is present) is also sound.\n- **argv.** `BuildConfig` returns exactly `[\"curl\",\"-K\",\"-\"]`; capture paths and body go through the config document, not argv. `exec.Command` with a `LookPath`-resolved absolute path, never a shell string.\n- **File permissions.** Config refuses any mode beyond 0600 at read time; history file 0600 in a 0700 dir with explicit `Chmod` after `OpenFile`; atomic replace via `CreateTemp` + `Chmod` + `Rename`; spec cache 0600/0700; body temp file 0600 and removed by `cleanup`; response capture dir 0700 with files pre-created 0600 so curl's umask cannot widen them. `cleanupWith` also zeroes the config buffer.\n- **Path traversal.** The spec cache filename is `hex(sha256(url))` — fully sanitized. `--fixtures` reads only `filepath.Join(dir, entry.Name())` from `ReadDir`. Path params are `url.PathEscape`d and a leftover `{}` is a hard failure.\n- **Credential firewall structure.** `SecretRef` has no value field and redacts under `String`/`GoString`/`MarshalJSON`; `Resolve` is called only from `internal/curl` (`config.go:263` and `exec.go:237`). `exec.go`'s `scrubber` correctly rewrites both raw and percent-encoded forms of resolved credentials out of curl's stderr. `profileRef` refuses a literal credential in the config file without quoting it.\n- **CI workflow.** `permissions: contents: read`, `pull_request` (not `pull_request_target`), no `${{ }}` interpolation into any `run:` block, pinned major-version actions. No secrets in the diff (scanned for private-key headers, `AKIA`, `ghp_`, `sk_live_`, `xox*`).",
