        "input": {
          "level": "high",
          "findings": [
            {
              "file": "cmd/talaria/root.go",
              "line": 129,
              "category": "correctness",
              "short_summary": "Unknown command exits 1 (network) not 2 (usage)",
              "summary": "An unknown subcommand or a bad argument count on `version` exits 1 — the code DESIGN.md §4 reserves for \"request could not be completed (network, curl failure)\" — instead of the exit 2 usage code, because cobra's unknown-command error is never classified and falls through `exitCode`'s unclassified default.",
              "failure_scenario": "`talaria bogus` → `{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":1,\"message\":\"unknown command \\\"bogus\\\" for \\\"talaria\\\"\"}}`, exit 1. Same for `talaria version extra`. AGENT.md:201 tells the agent that code 1 means \"check the host and --base-url; retrying once is reasonable\", so an agent that typos a subcommand is instructed to retry a network call that was never attempted. `talaria history extra` and `talaria list a b c` correctly exit 2, so the contract is inconsistent across the tree.",
              "verdict": "CONFIRMED"
            },
            {
              "file": "cmd/talaria/version.go",
              "line": 17,
              "category": "completeness",
              "short_summary": "`talaria version` ignores --output; emits no JSON envelope",
              "summary": "`version` writes bare text and never consults --output or the TTY default, violating DESIGN.md §3.1 (\"Machine-readable output everywhere: `--output json` on every command\" and \"Versioned output schema (`\\\"schema\\\": \\\"talaria/v1\\\"`)\").",
              "failure_scenario": "`talaria version --output json` prints `talaria dev` and exits 0; `talaria version | cat` (non-TTY, which §3.1 and §8 say must default to JSON) also prints `talaria dev`. An agent that json-parses it — as AGENT.md:185 (\"`--output json|pretty|tsv` on every command … Every payload carries `\\\"schema\\\": \\\"talaria/v1\\\"`\") and README (\"Every command takes `--output json|pretty|tsv`\") promise, with `talaria version` listed in AGENT.md's command table at line 46 — gets a parse error. It is also the one command an agent would call first to detect capabilities.",
              "verdict": "CONFIRMED"
            },
            {
              "file": "cmd/talaria/run.go",
              "line": 388,
              "category": "spec-drift",
              "short_summary": "`run` without --fail-on-error never exits 4",
              "summary": "DESIGN.md §4's exit-code table defines 4 as \"Validation failure (response violates spec) — only with `--fail-on-error` / `run`\", i.e. `run` is a second trigger alongside the flag; the implementation gates exit 4 on `failOnError` alone, so a bare `run` over an API whose responses violate the spec exits 0.",
              "failure_scenario": "`talaria run spec.yaml` against a server returning an undocumented 500 prints `\"summary\":{\"total\":1,\"passed\":0,\"failed\":1,\"skipped\":0}` and exits 0 (verified). A CI job that follows §4 and branches on the exit code of `talaria run` alone goes green on a completely broken API. README.md's own exit-code table repeats the DESIGN wording (\"only with `--fail-on-error` or `run`\") while its prose two sections earlier states the implemented behaviour, so the two READMEs disagree.",
              "verdict": "CONFIRMED"
            },
            {
              "file": "cmd/talaria/run.go",
              "line": 325,
              "category": "correctness",
              "short_summary": "Malformed missing-credential message: \"security 1 scheme petKey\"",
              "summary": "`pluralise(n, \"scheme\")` already returns \"1 scheme\", but it is interpolated after the word \"security\", producing \"no credential for security 1 scheme petKey (set $TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_PETKEY)\". The same bug is repeated in `verdict` at run.go:385.",
              "failure_scenario": "`talaria run cmd/talaria/testdata/call.yaml` with `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_PETKEY` unset emits that string in three places an agent is told to relay verbatim: the per-operation `reason` in the JSON report, the `<skipped message=…>` of the JUnit report, and the exit-5 stderr JSON. AGENT.md:130-131 instructs the agent to \"ask a human to export the variable named in its `reason`\", so the garbled sentence goes straight to a human. Fix: drop the stray count, e.g. `\"no credential for security %s %s\", pluralise(len(missing), \"scheme\"), strings.Join(missing, \", \")` → `\"no credential for %s %s\"`, or inline the plural without the leading \"security\".",
              "verdict": "CONFIRMED"
            },
            {
              "file": "cmd/talaria/auth.go",
              "line": 31,
              "category": "correctness",
              "short_summary": "`talaria auth` prints help to stdout and exits 0",
              "summary": "The `auth` parent command has no Run/RunE, so cobra falls back to printing the help block on stdout and exiting 0 — unstructured text on the machine-readable stream, and a success code for an invocation that did nothing. DESIGN.md §3.1 requires machine-readable output on every command and deterministic exit codes agents branch on.",
              "failure_scenario": "`talaria auth --output json` prints `Inspect the credentials a spec's security schemes need\\n\\nUsage:\\n  talaria auth [command]…` on stdout and exits 0. An agent that forgot the `check` subcommand parses that as JSON and fails, or worse reads exit 0 as \"credentials are fine\". Fix: give `auth` a RunE that returns `clierr.Usage(\"talaria auth needs a subcommand\").WithAlternatives(\"check\")`, so it exits 2 with the valid alternative.",
              "verdict": "CONFIRMED"
            },
            {
              "file": "internal/validate/validate_test.go",
              "line": 277,
              "category": "test-coverage",
              "short_summary": "strict-3.0 fixture: exclusiveMaximum has no assertion",
              "summary": "DESIGN.md §8's resolved open question claims the adversarial fixture pins all four 3.0-only constructs so \"a library upgrade that re-imposed 3.1 semantics would fail the suite\". The behaviour is real (I verified all four out-of-band), but only `nullable` and boolean `exclusiveMinimum` have assertions; boolean `exclusiveMaximum` and singular `example` are carried by the fixture with nothing checking them.",
              "failure_scenario": "I ran the fixture's schema against a live server via the built binary: `{\"id\":100,…}` correctly reports `exclusiveMaximum: got 100, want 100` and `{\"id\":5,\"label\":null,\"score\":null,\"tags\":null}` validates, so DESIGN.md's edit is truthful today. But `TestResponseEnforcesOpenAPI30ExclusiveBounds` only asserts the minimum side (id=0), so a regression that dropped the boolean→numeric rewrite for `exclusiveMaximum` alone would pass the suite while DESIGN.md claims otherwise. Add an `id: 100` case to that test.",
              "verdict": "CONFIRMED"
            }
          ]
        },
        "caller": {
          "type": "direct"
        }
      }
