  "summary": "Cleanup done, working tree restored. Here are my findings.\n\n## What I ran\n\n- `go test -race ./...` — **all 15 packages pass, no race reports**. Expected: `grep -rn \"go func|sync\\.|errgroup|make(chan\"` over non-test code finds only two `sync.Once` values (`internal/curl/version.go:30`, `internal/secret/response.go:225`) and **zero goroutines**. `cmd/talaria/run.go:117-120` is explicitly sequential, so the libopenapi-shared-model and results-collection races in the brief do not exist.\n- Throwaway probe in `internal/curl` (deleted): 32 MiB response through `Execute` → returned 33554432 bytes, no deadlock. The `cmd.Stdout/Stderr = &bytes.Buffer` + `cmd.Run()` pattern is the correct one; `Wait` is called exactly once via `Run`, and the stdin-copy goroutine is os/exec-managed (EPIPE-tolerant), so no leak on early curl exit. **The §5a stdin contract is implemented correctly** (`internal/request/body.go:70-84` reads Go's own stdin to EOF; `internal/curl/exec.go:67` gives curl a fresh `bytes.NewReader` pipe).\n- Throwaway probe in `internal/corpus` (deleted): concurrent `Append` above vs. below the retention cap, `-race -count=3`.\n- Live SIGTERM test with a built binary against a 120s-hanging server on 127.0.0.1:38111.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. CRIT — No timeout of any kind on the curl subprocess: `talaria` hangs forever on a non-responding server\n`internal/curl/exec.go:64` (and `internal/curl/config.go:94`)\n\n`exec.Command` is used, not `exec.CommandContext`, and the config document `document.build` emits only `url, request, header, cookie, data-raw, silent, show-error, write-out, output, dump-header` — no `--max-time`, no `--connect-timeout`. There is no timeout anywhere in the call path (`grep -rn \"Timeout|context\\.\" --include=*.go` outside tests matches **only** `internal/spec/source.go`).\n\nProven: against an endpoint that sleeps 90s, `Execute` was still blocked at 25s and only returned when the server eventually answered. Against a server that never answers, it blocks indefinitely.\n\nFailure mode: `talaria run` over a spec with one hung endpoint never terminates, and the caller has no flag to bound it. This is worse for `run` than for `call` — the suite stalls on operation *k* of *n* and never emits the report, so CI/an agent gets no partial output at all. The design doc applies exactly this reasoning to the *spec fetch* (`internal/spec/source.go:21-23`: \"A spec that never arrives has to become an exit code rather than a hung process an agent cannot interpret\") while leaving the higher-risk path — the actual network call — unbounded.\n\nFix: emit `connect-timeout` and `max-time` directives in `document.build`, defaulted and overridable via a `--timeout` flag. curl enforces these itself and returns exit 28, which `runFailure` already classifies correctly, so no Go-side plumbing is needed for the happy path. Pair it with `exec.CommandContext` + `cmd.WaitDelay` at a slightly larger deadline so a curl that ignores its own timeout is still reaped.\n\n### 2. CRIT — `corpus.trim` is an unlocked read-modify-write: concurrent `Append`s are silently destroyed\n`internal/corpus/store.go:179` (window from `os.ReadFile` at 179 to `replace` at 224; entered from `Append` at `store.go:104`)\n\n`Append` does `write()` (O_APPEND, atomic) then `trim()`, which `ReadFile`s the whole store, filters, and `replace()`s it via temp+rename. Nothing serialises the two halves. Any entry appended by another goroutine or another `talaria` process between the `ReadFile` and the `Rename` is inside the snapshot that gets overwritten and is lost. `Append` returns `nil` for those entries, so `recordCall`'s \"the call was not recorded in history\" warning (`cmd/talaria/call.go:250`) never fires — the loss is completely silent.\n\nProven: 8 writers × 10 appends against a store seeded to `maxPerSource-1`, `-race -count=3` → **57, 60 and 61 of 80 successfully-appended entries were gone** from the final file, every `Append` having returned nil. The entry *count* stays at exactly 1000, which is what makes this invisible to a count-based test. Below the cap (`trim` returns early at `store.go:203`) I measured 0/80 lost, so the exposure is precisely the rewrite path.\n\n`run.go` is sequential so there is no in-process exposure today; the live exposure is two `talaria` processes sharing `$XDG_STATE_HOME/talaria/history.jsonl` — an agent issuing parallel `call`s, or a `run` overlapping an interactive `call`. Note the cap is per-source and `run` alone can push `SourceRun` past 1000 in a single suite, so a concurrent `call` at that moment loses history.\n\nFix: take an exclusive `syscall.Flock` on the history file (or a sibling `.lock`) for the whole of `Append` — write *and* trim — released with `defer`. The temp+rename in `replace` is already correct for readers; it is only the unsynchronised snapshot that is wrong.\n\n### 3. WARN — Killing `talaria` orphans the curl child and permanently leaks the response capture directory\n`internal/curl/exec.go:53` and `internal/curl/exec.go:64`\n\n`cleanupCapture` (which `os.RemoveAll`s the 0700 `talaria-call-*` dir holding `body` and `headers`) and `cleanupConfig` (which removes `talaria-body-*` and zeroes the resolved-credential buffer) are both plain `defer`s. There is no signal handling anywhere in `cmd/talaria`, and no context on the child.\n\nProven: started a call against the hanging server, `kill -TERM` on the talaria pid. talaria died; `ps -o pid,ppid` showed the curl child **reparented to PPID 1 and still running**, and `/tmp/talaria-call-2594644524/` survived with its `body` and `headers` files, still being written to by the orphan.\n\nTwo consequences: (a) the orphan keeps talking to the API after the operator killed the tool — relevant for a tool that otherwise gates mutations carefully; (b) response headers, which is where `Set-Cookie` and refreshed tokens land, are left in TMPDIR at 0700 forever, and repeated kills (see finding 1 — a hung `run` is the thing a harness kills) accumulate them. `cleanupWith`'s credential-zeroing (`internal/curl/config.go:250-257`) also never runs.\n\nInteractive Ctrl-C is partially mitigated because SIGINT reaches the whole foreground process group, but the temp-dir leak happens there too, and the pid-directed kill an agent harness issues is unmitigated.\n\nFix: `exec.CommandContext` with a context cancelled by a `signal.NotifyContext(SIGINT, SIGTERM)` installed in `cmd/talaria/root.go`, plus `cmd.WaitDelay` so the child is killed and reaped rather than left; and run the two cleanup funcs from that signal path, not only from `defer`.\n\n---\n\n## Checked and clean (no finding)\n\n- `internal/spec/source.go:156-184` — spec cache write is temp-file + `Chmod` + `Rename` with `defer os.Remove(tmp.Name())` on every path. Two processes fetching the same URL concurrently is safe: worst case both fetch and one rename wins atomically; no reader ever sees a partial file. Dropped errors here are documented best-effort and correct.\n- `internal/spec/source.go:121` — `defer resp.Body.Close()` present; the 30s client timeout is set.\n- `internal/curl/config.go:201-226` — temp body files are registered in `d.files` *before* the Chmod/Write that can fail, so `cleanup` removes them on every error path.\n- `internal/curl/exec.go:55-59` — `cleanupConfig` is deferred before the `err` check and `BuildConfig` guarantees non-nil, so no nil-deref and no missed cleanup.\n- All `//nolint:errcheck` sites: each drops a `Close` error on a path that already returns a more specific error. `internal/corpus/entry.go:119` discards a `req.URL(request.Redacted)` error, but the redacted renderer resolves no secrets and cannot fail. Nothing swallowed that breaks functionality.\n- `preflightOnce` / `QueryKeyWarner.once` — both correct; `sync.Once` gives the happens-before for the `preflightErr` read at `version.go:51`.",
