g returned nil. The entry *count* stays at exactly 1000, which is what makes this invisible to a count-based test. Below the cap (`trim` returns early at `store.go:203`) I measured 0/80 lost, so the exposure is precisely the rewrite path.\n\n`run.go` is sequential so there is no in-process exposure today; the live exposure is two `talaria` processes sharing `$XDG_STATE_HOME/talaria/history.jsonl` — an agent issuing parallel `call`s, or a `run` overlapping an interactive `call`. Note the cap is per-source and `run` alone can push `SourceRun` past 1000 in a single suite, so a concurrent `call` at that moment loses history.\n\nFix: take an exclusive `syscall.Flock` on the history file (or a sibling `.lock`) for the whole of `Append` — write *and* trim — released with `defer`. The temp+rename in `replace` is already correct for readers; it is only the unsynchronised snapshot that is wrong.\n\n### 3. WARN — Killing `talaria` orphans the curl child and permanently leaks the response capture directory\n`internal/curl/exec.go:53` and `internal/curl/exec.go:64`\n\n`cleanupCapture` (which `os.RemoveAll`s the 0700 `talaria-call-*` dir holding `body` and `headers`) and `cleanupConfig` (which removes `talaria-body-*` and zeroes the resolved-credential buffer) are both plain `defer`s. There is no signal handling anywhere in `cmd/talaria`, and no context on the child.\n\nProven: started a call against the hanging server, `kill -TERM` on the talaria pid. talaria died; `ps -o pid,ppid` showed the curl child **reparented to PPID 1 and still running**, and `/tmp/talaria-call-2594644524/` survived with its `body` and `headers` files, still being written to by the orphan.\n\nTwo consequences: (a) the orphan keeps talking to the API after the operator killed the tool — relevant for a tool that otherwise gates mutations carefully; (b) response headers, which is where `Set-Cookie` and refreshed tokens land, are left in TMPDIR at 0700 forever, and repeated kills (see finding 1 — a hung `run` is the thing a harness kills) accumulate them. `cleanupWith`'s credential-zeroing (`internal/curl/config.go:250-257`) also never runs.\n\nInteractive Ctrl-C is partially mitigated because SIGINT reaches the whole foreground process group, but the temp-dir leak happens there too, and the pid-directed kill an agent harness issues is unmitigated.\n\nFix: `exec.CommandContext` with a context cancelled by a `signal.NotifyContext(SIGINT, SIGTERM)` installed in `cmd/talaria/root.go`, plus `cmd.WaitDelay` so the child is killed and reaped rather than left; and run the two cleanup funcs from that signal path, not only from `defer`.\n\n---\n\n## Checked and clean (no finding)\n\n- `internal/spec/source.go:156-184` — spec cache write is temp-file + `Chmod` + `Rename` with `defer os.Remove(tmp.Name())` on every path. Two processes fetching the same URL concurrently is safe: worst case both fetch and one rename wins atomically; no reader ever sees a partial file. Dropped errors here are documented best-effort and correct.\n- `internal/spec/source.go:121` — `defer resp.Body.Close()` present; the 30s client timeout is set.\n- `internal/curl/config.go:201-226` — temp body files are registered in `d.files` *before* the Chmod/Write that can fail, so `cleanup` removes them on every error path.\n- `internal/curl/exec.go:55-59` — `cleanupConfig` is deferred before the `err` check and `BuildConfig` guarantees non-nil, so no nil-deref and no missed cleanup.\n- All `//nolint:errcheck` sites: each drops a `Close` error on a path that already returns a more specific error. `internal/corpus/entry.go:119` discards a `req.URL(request.Redacted)` error, but the redacted renderer resolves no secrets and cannot fail. Nothing swallowed that breaks functionality.\n- `preflightOnce` / `QueryKeyWarner.once` — both correct; `sync.Once` gives the happens-before for the `preflightErr` read at `version.go:51`."
      }
    ],
