{
  "type": "system",
  "subtype": "task_notification",
  "task_id": "afb5a8f4986dfb22a",
  "tool_use_id": "toolu_013DiMpnQbSnWUJHhopxjCAW",
  "status": "completed",
  "output_file": "/tmp/claude-1000/-home-casper-projects-talaria/40743458-5293-43fb-a819-2616e10ba7ee/tasks/afb5a8f4986dfb22a.output",
  "summary": "## Integration review — `ralph/design` (build + run verified)\n\nBuilt `go build -o /tmp/talaria ./cmd/talaria`, stood up a python3 echo server on `127.0.0.1:8731` (logs method/path/headers/body) plus a spec-compliant HEAD server on `:8732`, and drove real `call` / `run` / `history replay` against `cmd/talaria/testdata/*.yaml`, `internal/e2e/testdata/*` and purpose-built 2.0/3.0 fixtures with real curl 8.14.1.\n\n**What I verified as working** (no findings): dry-run `request` block is byte-identical to the real call for headers/URL/query/method; Swagger 2.0 → 3.x conversion carries `formData`/`body` params, `consumes`/`produces`, `host`+`basePath`+`schemes` → server, and all three security-scheme kinds through to `auth check` and a real basic-auth `-u` exec; the fixture priority chain takes effect at runtime in the documented order (spec `example` \"42\" beat `--fixtures` \"FIXTURE-ID\"; fixture body beat generation; fixture headers reached the wire); JUnit counts/`time`/`<skipped>` vs `<failure>` are correct and Go's XML encoder escapes `<`, `&` and control chars safely; history is written for 4xx/5xx and for connection failures; exit codes 0/1/2/4/5 hold; `history replay` re-resolves `<redacted:env:NAME>` back into a real credential rather than replaying the placeholder (verified both header and query-string forms on the wire); preflight runs from all three executing commands; large (1.2 MB) and binary request bodies go on the wire byte-exact; `internal/curl` and `internal/e2e` drive the real curl binary and a real built binary, so the tested path is the production path; CI's lint step is `test -z \"$(gofmt -l .)\" && go vet ./...`, which does fail on unformatted files, and `go test ./...` really does gate `internal/canary`.\n\nNote: `internal/curl/zz_probe_test.go` and `internal/corpus/zz_probe_test.go` are untracked probe files from another agent running concurrently — not part of this diff, and the one failing test in my `go test ./...` run came from them.\n\n### Findings\n\n**CRIT — `talaria call`/`run` hang forever on any HEAD operation** — `internal/curl/config.go:78`\nThe config document emits `request = \"HEAD\"`, which is `curl -X HEAD`, not `--head`. curl then waits for a body of `Content-Length` bytes that a compliant server never sends. HEAD is in `safeMethods` (`internal/operation/operation.go:106`), so it needs no `--allow-mutations` and `run` includes it by default. There is also no `max-time`/`connect-timeout` directive anywhere, so nothing breaks the hang.\nRepro: spec with `head: {operationId: headThing}` against a server that answers HEAD with `Content-Length: 42` and no body → `timeout 20 talaria call … headThing` exits 124; `timeout 15 talaria run …` exits 124 and emits **no report at all**, so one HEAD operation kills the whole suite. Plain `curl -X HEAD http://…` reproduces it standalone (exit 124), while `curl -I` returns instantly. No test in the repo ever executes a HEAD operation — `operation_test.go:227` only asserts `IsMutation()==false`.\nFix: in `document.build`, emit the bare `head` flag when `req.Method == \"HEAD\"` instead of `request = \"HEAD\"` (verified: `head` + `write-out`/`dump-header` completes and populates the header file; note curl also echoes the header block into the `output` file, so route the body capture to `/dev/null` for HEAD). Mirror it in `render.go:36` as `-I`. Separately, add a `max-time` directive so no method can hang unbounded.\n\n**CRIT — `run` sends a JSON body under the wrong `Content-Type`** — `cmd/talaria/run.go:338`\n`gen.bodySchema` (`internal/gen/fixtures.go:228`) deliberately picks the *JSON* media type's schema to generate from, but that choice is never communicated onward: `run` passes only the bytes, and `binder.contentType` independently picks `rb.Content[0].ContentType` (`internal/request/body.go:123`). When the JSON media type is not first, the two disagree.\nVerified: a 3.0 spec declaring `application/xml` before `application/json` → server received `Content-Type: application/xml` with body `{\"name\":\"foxtrot-906\"}`. Worse on the 2.0 path, where it is unconditional: every converted `formData` operation gets `Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded` (or `multipart/form-data`) with a JSON body — my `postForm` fixture sent `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` + `{\"alpha\":\"foxtrot-906\",\"beta\":638}`. talaria reported `outcome: passed` because my echo server accepts anything; a real API returns 415/400 and the smoke test reports a spec bug that is actually talaria's.\nFix: have `gen.Data` carry the media type `bodySchema` selected, and have `run` pass it as an explicit `Content-Type` in `request.Inputs.Headers` — `binder.contentType` already lets a user-set header win, so nothing else changes.\n\n**CRIT — a non-UTF-8 request body is silently corrupted in the corpus and replayed corrupted** — `internal/corpus/entry.go:216`\n`newBody` does `body.Data = string(data)`, and `Entry` is marshalled with `encoding/json`, which replaces every invalid byte with U+FFFD. `replayRequest` then does `Data: []byte(body.Data)` (`cmd/talaria/history.go:511`) with no guard — it checks `Truncated` and refuses, but has nothing to check for this.\nVerified: `talaria call … --body @bin.dat` (1024 bytes, `bytes(range(256))*4`) reached the server as exactly 1024 bytes; `talaria history replay N --allow-mutations` sent **2048 bytes**, silently, with no warning. `internal/curl/config.go:192` has a whole temp-file path specifically so binary bodies survive exec, so one component handles them correctly and the very next one mangles them. Since DESIGN §5 makes this store the twin's corpus, the twin would replay the corrupted bytes too.\nFix: store the body base64-encoded when `!utf8.Valid(data)` (add an `encoding` field to `corpus.Body`), or at minimum set a `lossy` flag at write time and have `replayRequest` refuse it the way it refuses `Truncated`.\n\n**WARN — the emitted/dry-run curl reads a local file for any body starting with `@`** — `internal/curl/render.go:115`\n`Render` uses `--data-binary`, while the config document deliberately uses `data-raw` precisely because \"`data` and `data-binary` read a value starting with `@` as a filename\" (`config.go:169`). The two disagree for exactly that input.\nVerified: `--body @/tmp/atbody.txt` (file contents `@/etc/hostname`) → talaria really sent the literal `@/etc/hostname`; the emitted command `curl -s -X POST … --data-binary '@/etc/hostname' 'http://…'` , pasted verbatim, sent `debian-8gb-hel1-3\\n` — the contents of my host's `/etc/hostname`. So the dry-run preview is wrong, and the \"safe to paste into a bug report\" command is a local-file read. Triggered by `--body @file` or `--body -` whose first byte is `@` (form/text/YAML payloads, webhook signatures).\nFix: use `--data-raw` in `bodyArgs`, matching `config.go`.\n\n**WARN — `history replay` skips the query-string API-key warning** — `cmd/talaria/history.go:201`\nDESIGN §5a requires warning once when a credential travels in the query string, because it lands in the server's access log regardless of redaction. `call` and `run` both call `warnQueryCredentials`; `replay` re-issues the identical request and does not.\nVerified: `talaria call … getKeyed` printed the warning; `talaria history replay 1` printed nothing on stderr while the server logged `/keyed?api_key=qk`. An agent whose only path to a keyed endpoint is replay never learns the key is being logged.\nFix: call `warnQueryCredentials(cmd.ErrOrStderr(), warner, req)` after `replayRequest` returns, with a warner built alongside the command as `call` does.\n\n**WARN — a remote spec is cached forever with no expiry and no way to refresh** — `internal/spec/source.go:87`\n`loadURL` returns the cached bytes whenever the file exists; nothing writes a timestamp, checks one, sends a conditional request, or exposes a `--refresh`/`--no-cache` flag. README:48 states the behaviour (\"fetched once per URL\") but neither it nor `AGENT.md` tells anyone how to invalidate it. Once an API publishes a changed `openapi.json`, every `list`/`describe`/`call`/`run` against that URL keeps binding to the old operation model — new operations invisible, removed ones still callable, response contracts stale — until someone finds and deletes a SHA-256-named file under `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/talaria/specs`. That is the failure mode the spec-centric pitch (\"nothing to curate, nothing to drift\") exists to prevent, and an agent has no way to detect or fix it.\nFix: add a TTL (mtime-based, e.g. 1h) plus a `--refresh` flag on the root command that bypasses the read.",
