      {
        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01GMR6VZ4KsKaEgttnLFJMUm",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "1\t# Review findings — branch `ralph/design` vs `main`\n2\t\n3\tReviewers run: security, spec-compliance, concurrency, integration (per `.ralph/stack.json`).\n4\tBaseline: `go build ./...`, `gofmt -l .`, `go vet ./...` and `go test -race ./...` all pass on\n5\tthe tracked tree. Every finding below was reproduced by reading the code and running the built\n6\tbinary or a probe test; nothing here is speculative.\n7\t\n8\tScope: Phases 1–4 of `docs/design/DESIGN.md` §7 (tasks 1–33). The twin (Phases 5–8) is\n9\tcorrectly absent and is not reported.\n10\t\n11\t20 findings: 11 CRIT, 7 WARN, 2 INFO.\n12\t\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## Finding 1: A credential passed as a query parameter is never redacted\n16\t- **Reviewer:** security\n17\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n18\t- **File:** internal/request/build.go:76\n19\t- **Description:** `Build` wraps `req.Headers` (line 77) and `req.Cookies` (line 78) in `hide()`,\n20\t  the §5a built-in name matcher, but leaves `req.Query` bare. Query is the one credential\n21\t  location where the firewall silently does not apply, and DESIGN.md §5a's leak-channel table\n22\t  names it explicitly: \"Query-string API keys (`?api_key=`) → Symbolic in emitted curl,\n23\t  `<redacted>` in output URL fields.\" Reproduced with the built binary — an identically named\n24\t  header is redacted while the query key is not:\n25\t  `talaria call spec.yaml getPub --query api_key=SUPERSECRET123 --header X-Api-Key=SUPERSECRET456 --output json --dry-run`\n26\t  emits `\"curl\":\"curl -s -H 'X-Api-Key: <redacted>' 'http://…/pub?api_key=SUPERSECRET123'\"` and\n27\t  `\"url\":\"http://…/pub?api_key=SUPERSECRET123\"`. On a real call the same value is written verbatim\n28\t  into `$XDG_STATE_HOME/talaria/history.jsonl`, the artifact §5a calls \"the highest-risk surface in\n29\t  the tool\", where redaction is required at write time. It also reaches `validate.Input.URL` via\n30\t  `curl.URL(req)`, so a validation error message can quote it. This is a direct breach of the\n31\t  invariant the product exists to enforce.\n32\t- **Suggested fix:** `req.Query = hide(append(b.located(bound, inQuery), b.pairs(in.Query, \"--query\")...))`.\n33\t  Note the display consequence: `urlWord` (internal/curl/render.go:139) and `Request.QueryString`\n34\t  (internal/request/request.go:238) branch on `IsSecret()` rather than `IsSensitive()`, so a hidden\n35\t  literal currently renders as `%3Credacted%3E`; both should skip `url.QueryEscape` for a sensitive\n36\t  value. Add a `--query` case to `TestAUserSuppliedHeaderIsRedactedLikeASpecCredential` in\n37\t  internal/canary/canary_test.go — the canary suite has no `--query` counterpart today, which is why\n38\t  this shipped.\n39\t\n40\t## Finding 2: Base URL scheme is never validated, so any spec can make curl speak gopher/file/dict\n41\t- **Reviewer:** security\n42\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n43\t- **File:** internal/request/build.go:141\n44\t- **Description:** `baseURL` checks only that the URL parses and has a non-empty `Scheme` and\n45\t  `Host`. The scheme itself is never compared against anything, even though the error message on\n46\t  the very next line promises \"is not an absolute http(s) URL\". The value flows into\n47\t  `document.build`'s `url = \"…\"` directive, and internal/curl/config.go sets no `proto` /\n48\t  `proto-redir` restriction, so system curl will speak whatever protocol the scheme names.\n49\t  Confirmed against the built binary: `talaria call spec.yaml getPub --base-url gopher://127.0.0.1:18201\n50\t  --output json --dry-run` is accepted and emits `curl -s 'gopher://127.0.0.1:18201/pub'`. The\n51\t  candidate list in `baseURL` includes `firstServer(b.in.Doc)` — the spec's own `servers[0].url` —\n52\t  and the product's premise is that an agent points this at any OpenAPI doc it found, so that field\n53\t  is untrusted input. A spec with `servers: [{url: \"gopher://127.0.0.1:18201\"}]` and a path of\n54\t  `/_SET%20talaria%20pwned` made a plain `talaria call` (no `--base-url`, no `--allow-mutations`)\n55\t  deliver `SET talaria pwned\\r\\n` to a raw TCP listener — the classic Redis/memcached SSRF gadget\n56\t  with an attacker-controlled payload. It also bypasses the `--allow-mutations` rail entirely, since\n57\t  that rail gates the HTTP method and gopher has none. `file://` is likewise accepted and read.\n58\t- **Suggested fix:** In `baseURL`, reject any scheme other than `http` and `https`, making the check\n59\t  match the message it already prints. Defence in depth: emit `proto = \"=http,https\"` and\n60\t  `proto-redir = \"=http,https\"` directives in `document.build`. cmd/talaria/history.go:494 rebuilds\n61\t  `BaseURL` from a stored URL with the same missing check and needs the same guard.\n62\t\n63\t## Finding 3: HEAD operations emit `-X HEAD` and hang forever\n64\t- **Reviewer:** integration\n65\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n66\t- **File:** internal/curl/config.go:78\n67\t- **Description:** `document.build` emits `request = \"HEAD\"`, which is `curl -X HEAD`, not `--head`.\n68\t  curl then waits for a body of `Content-Length` bytes that a compliant server never sends. `HEAD`\n69\t  is in `safeMethods` (internal/operation/operation.go:106), so it needs no `--allow-mutations` and\n70\t  `run` includes it by default. Reproduced against a local HTTP/1.1 server answering HEAD with\n71\t  `Content-Length: 42` and no body: `timeout 12 talaria call headspec.yaml headThing --base-url\n72\t  http://127.0.0.1:18878` exits 124 — hung. Against an HTTP/1.0 server that closes the connection it\n73\t  instead fails outright: `{\"error\":{\"code\":1,\"message\":\"…curl exited 18: curl: (18) end of response\n74\t  with 42 bytes missing\"}}`. Standalone `curl -X HEAD` reproduces both (exit 18 / hang) where\n75\t  `curl -I` returns instantly. Because there is no `max-time` (Finding 4), one HEAD operation in a\n76\t  spec makes `talaria run` never terminate and emit no report at all. No test in the repo ever\n77\t  executes a HEAD operation — operation_test.go:227 only asserts `IsMutation()==false`.\n78\t- **Suggested fix:** In `document.build`, emit the bare `head` directive when `req.Method == \"HEAD\"`\n79\t  instead of `request = \"HEAD\"`; curl also echoes the header block into the `output` file for `--head`,\n80\t  so route the body capture to `/dev/null` for that method. Mirror it in internal/curl/render.go:36\n81\t  as `-I`. Add a HEAD case to the executor tests.\n82\t\n83\t## Finding 4: No timeout of any kind on the curl subprocess\n84\t- **Reviewer:** concurrency\n85\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n86\t- **File:** internal/curl/config.go:94\n87\t- **Description:** `document.build` emits only `url, request, header, cookie, data-raw, silent,\n88\t  show-error, write-out, output, dump-header` — no `max-time`, no `connect-timeout` — and\n89\t  internal/curl/exec.go:64 uses `exec.Command`, not `exec.CommandContext`. `grep -rn\n90\t  \"Timeout|context\\.\" --include=*.go` over non-test code matches only internal/spec/source.go, so\n91\t  there is no timeout anywhere in the call path and no flag to bound one. Verified: against an\n92\t  endpoint sleeping 90s, `Execute` was still blocked at 25s; against a server that never answers it\n93\t  blocks indefinitely. The impact is worst for `run` — the suite stalls on operation *k* of *n* and\n94\t  never emits its report, so CI or an agent gets no partial output at all. The design doc applies\n95\t  exactly this reasoning to the *spec fetch* (internal/spec/source.go:21 — \"A spec that never arrives\n96\t  has to become an exit code rather than a hung process an agent cannot interpret\") while leaving\n97\t  the higher-risk path unbounded.\n98\t- **Suggested fix:** Emit `connect-timeout` and `max-time` directives in `document.build`, defaulted\n99\t  and overridable via a `--timeout` flag. curl enforces these itself and exits 28, which `runFailure`\n100\t  already classifies correctly, so no Go-side plumbing is needed on the happy path. Pair with\n101\t  `exec.CommandContext` plus `cmd.WaitDelay` at a slightly larger deadline so a curl that ignores its\n102\t  own timeout is still reaped.\n103\t\n104\t## Finding 5: `corpus.trim` is an unlocked read-modify-write that silently destroys history entries\n105\t- **Reviewer:** concurrency\n106\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n107\t- **File:** internal/corpus/store.go:179\n108\t- **Description:** `Append` (store.go:104) does `write()` — O_APPEND, atomic — then `trim()`, which\n109\t  `os.ReadFile`s the whole store, filters, and `replace()`s it via temp+rename. Nothing serialises\n110\t  the two halves. Any entry appended by another goroutine or another `talaria` process between the\n111\t  `ReadFile` at line 179 and the `Rename` at line 224 falls inside the overwritten snapshot and is\n112\t  lost. `Append` returns `nil` for those entries, so the \"the call was not recorded in history\"\n113\t  warning at cmd/talaria/call.go:250 never fires — the loss is completely silent. Proven with a probe\n114\t  test: 8 writers × 10 appends against a store seeded to `maxPerSource-1`, under `-race -count=3`,\n115\t  lost 57, 60 and 61 of 80 successfully-appended entries, every `Append` having returned nil. The\n116\t  entry count stays at exactly 1000, which is what hides this from a count-based test. Below the cap\n117\t  (`trim` returns early at store.go:203) 0 of 80 were lost, so the exposure is precisely the rewrite\n118\t  path. `run.go` is sequential so there is no in-process exposure today; the live exposure is two\n119\t  `talaria` processes sharing `history.jsonl` — an agent issuing parallel `call`s, or a `run`\n120\t  overlapping an interactive `call`. The cap is per-source and `run` alone can push `SourceRun` past\n121\t  1000 in one suite.\n122\t- **Suggested fix:** Take an exclusive `syscall.Flock` on the history file (or a sibling `.lock`) for\n123\t  the whole of `Append` — write *and* trim — released with `defer`. The temp+rename in `replace` is\n124\t  already correct for readers; only the unsynchronised snapshot is wrong.\n125\t\n126\t## Finding 6: `run` sends its generated JSON body under the wrong `Content-Type`\n127\t- **Reviewer:** integration\n128\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n129\t- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:338\n130\t- **Description:** `gen.bodySchema` (internal/gen/fixtures.go:228) deliberately selects the *JSON*\n131\t  media type's schema to generate from, but that choice is never communicated onward: `run` passes\n132\t  only the bytes, and `binder.contentType` independently picks `rb.Content[0].ContentType`\n133\t  (internal/request/body.go:123). When the JSON media type is not first, the two disagree. Verified\n134\t  against a local echo server: a 3.0 spec declaring `application/xml` before `application/json`\n135\t  produced `Content-Type: application/xml` with body `{\"name\":\"foxtrot-906\"}`. It is worse on the\n136\t  Swagger 2.0 path, where it is unconditional — every converted `formData` operation gets\n137\t  `Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded` (or `multipart/form-data`) carrying a JSON body.\n138\t  talaria reported `outcome: passed` because the echo server accepts anything; a real API returns\n139\t  415/400 and the smoke test then reports a spec bug that is actually talaria's.\n140\t- **Suggested fix:** Have `gen.Data` carry the media type `bodySchema` selected, and have `run` pass\n141\t  it as an explicit `Content-Type` in `request.Inputs.Headers`. `binder.contentType` already lets a\n142\t  user-set header win, so nothing else changes.\n143\t\n144\t## Finding 7: A non-UTF-8 request body is silently corrupted in the corpus and replayed corrupted\n145\t- **Reviewer:** integration\n146\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n147\t- **File:** internal/corpus/entry.go:216\n148\t- **Description:** `newBody` does `body.Data = string(data)`, and `Entry` is marshalled with\n149\t  `encoding/json`, which replaces every invalid byte with U+FFFD. `replayRequest` then does\n150\t  `Data: []byte(body.Data)` (cmd/talaria/history.go:511) with no guard — it checks `Truncated` and\n151\t  refuses, but has nothing to check for this. Verified: `talaria call … --body @bin.dat` (1024 bytes,\n152\t  `bytes(range(256))*4`) reached the server as exactly 1024 bytes; `talaria history replay N\n153\t  --allow-mutations` sent **2048 bytes**, silently, with no warning. internal/curl/config.go:192\n154\t  carries a whole temp-file path specifically so binary bodies survive exec, so one component handles\n155\t  them correctly and the next one mangles them. DESIGN.md §5 makes this store the twin's corpus, so a\n156\t  Phase 6+ twin would replay the corrupted bytes too.\n157\t- **Suggested fix:** Add an `encoding` field to `corpus.Body` and store the body base64-encoded when\n158\t  `!utf8.Valid(data)`. At minimum, set a `lossy` flag at write time and have `replayRequest` refuse it\n159\t  the way it refuses `Truncated`.\n160\t\n161\t## Finding 8: The emitted/dry-run curl uses `--data-binary`, so a body starting with `@` reads a local file\n162\t- **Reviewer:** integration\n163\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n164\t- **File:** internal/curl/render.go:115\n165\t- **Description:** `Render` emits `--data-binary`, while the config document deliberately uses\n166\t  `data-raw` — internal/curl/config.go:169 carries the comment explaining exactly why: \"`data` and\n167\t  `data-binary` read a value starting with `@` as a filename, and a JSON body legitimately can.\"\n168\t  The two paths therefore disagree for precisely that input. Verified: `--body @/tmp/atbody.txt`\n169\t  (file contents `@/etc/hostname`) really sent the literal `@/etc/hostname` on the wire, while the\n170\t  emitted command `curl -s -X POST … --data-binary '@/etc/hostname' 'http://…'`, pasted verbatim,\n171\t  sent the contents of the host's `/etc/hostname`. Two contracts break at once: DESIGN.md §3.4 says\n172\t  `--dry-run` prints \"the exact curl command\" and every call returns \"a portable reproduction for bug\n173\t  reports\" — here it is neither — and the reproduction command turns into a local-file read that\n174\t  exfiltrates to the API. Triggered by `--body @file` or `--body -` whose first byte is `@`\n175\t  (form/text/YAML payloads, webhook signatures).\n176\t- **Suggested fix:** Use `--data-raw` in `bodyArgs`, matching config.go, and add a render test with an\n177\t  `@`-leading body pinning the two paths together.\n178\t\n179\t## Finding 9: An unknown command exits 1 instead of the documented usage code 2\n180\t- **Reviewer:** spec-compliance\n181\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n182\t- **File:** cmd/talaria/root.go:129\n183\t- **Description:** `exitCode` treats an unclassified error as a request failure (1). Cobra's\n184\t  \"unknown command\" error is never classified, so `talaria bogus` returns 1. Verified:\n185\t  `talaria bogus` → exit 1, stderr `{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":1,\"message\":\"unknown\n186\t  command \\\"bogus\\\" for \\\"talaria\\\"\"}}`. DESIGN.md §4's exit table assigns 1 to \"Request could not be\n187\t  completed (network, curl failure)\" and 2 to \"Usage error (unknown operation, missing required\n188\t  param)\". Exit 1 is the code AGENT.md tells an agent is transient and worth retrying, so a typo'd\n189\t  command sends the agent into a retry loop. `history` and `list` classify their own unknown-argument\n190\t  errors as 2 correctly, so the contract is internally inconsistent as well as wrong.\n191\t- **Suggested fix:** Set `SilenceErrors`/`SilenceUsage` as already done and classify cobra's argument\n192\t  and unknown-command errors as `clierr.Usage` before they reach `exitCode` — either by wrapping in\n193\t  `root.SetFlagErrorFunc` plus a `RunE` on the root that returns `clierr.Usage`, or by mapping the\n194\t  unclassified-error default for the pre-`RunE` phase. Add a case to exitcode_test.go.\n195\t\n196\t## Finding 10: `talaria version` ignores `--output` and emits no JSON envelope\n197\t- **Reviewer:** spec-compliance\n198\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n199\t- **File:** cmd/talaria/version.go:17\n200\t- **Description:** The command `fmt.Fprintf`s `talaria dev\\n` unconditionally. Verified:\n201\t  `talaria version --output json` prints `talaria dev` and exits 0 — no `schema` field, no envelope,\n202\t  and the same when piped. DESIGN.md §3.1 requires \"Machine-readable output everywhere: `--output\n203\t  json` on every command\" and \"Versioned output schema (`\\\"schema\\\": \\\"talaria/v1\\\"`) so agent prompts\n204\t  don't break\"; AGENT.md:183 repeats the promise verbatim (\"`--output json|pretty|tsv` on every\n205\t  command\"). An agent that parses every command's stdout as the versioned envelope — which is what\n206\t  AGENT.md instructs — fails on the one command it would use to check compatibility.\n207\t- **Suggested fix:** Route `version` through `output.New(format, cmd.OutOrStdout())` with a\n208\t  `{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"version\":\"…\"}` payload, keeping the bare string as the pretty rendering.\n209\t  Add a case to version_test.go asserting the JSON form.\n210\t\n211\t## Finding 11: libopenapi writes structured error logs directly to `os.Stdout`, corrupting the envelope\n212\t- **Reviewer:** security\n213\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n214\t- **File:** internal/spec/load.go:49\n215\t- **Description:** `libopenapi.NewDocument(data)` leaves `d.config == nil`, so `BuildV3Model` falls\n216\t  back to `datamodel.NewDocumentConfiguration()`, whose default `Logger` is\n217\t  `slog.New(slog.NewJSONHandler(os.Stdout, …))` (libopenapi@v0.38.7/datamodel/document_config.go:233).\n218\t  That writer is the real process stdout — it bypasses `cmd.OutOrStdout()`, the `talaria/v1` envelope,\n219\t  and every redaction path. Verified with a spec carrying an unresolvable remote `$ref`:\n220\t  `talaria list refspec.yaml --output json 2>/dev/null` puts **two** `{\"time\":…,\"level\":\"ERROR\",…}`\n221\t  documents on stdout and nothing else (exit 3). Unresolvable and remote `$ref`s are ordinary in real\n222\t  specs, so an agent parsing stdout as one JSON document breaks on them. It is also an unaudited\n223\t  third-party output channel in a tool whose entire invariant concerns what reaches stdout.\n224\t- **Suggested fix:** Use `libopenapi.NewDocumentWithConfiguration` with a configuration whose `Logger`\n225\t  writes to `io.Discard` (or to stderr, if the diagnostics are wanted). Verified while checking this:\n226\t  `AllowFileReferences` and `AllowRemoteReferences` both default to false, so a hostile spec's `$ref`\n227\t  cannot read local files or fetch remote URLs — keep it that way when passing the config.\n228\t\n229\t---\n230\t\n231\t## Finding 12: Killing `talaria` orphans the curl child and leaks the response capture directory\n232\t- **Reviewer:** concurrency\n233\t- **Severity:** WARN\n234\t- **File:** internal/curl/exec.go:53\n235\t- **Description:** `cleanupCapture` (which `os.RemoveAll`s the 0700 `talaria-call-*` dir holding\n236\t  `body` and `headers`) and `cleanupConfig` (which removes `talaria-body-*` and zeroes the resolved\n237\t  credential buffer) are plain `defer`s. There is no signal handling anywhere in `cmd/talaria` and no\n238\t  context on the child. Verified: started a call against a hanging server, `kill -TERM` on the talaria\n239\t  pid — talaria died, `ps -o pid,ppid` showed the curl child reparented to PPID 1 and still running,\n240\t  and `/tmp/talaria-call-2594644524/` survived with its `body` and `headers` files, still being\n241\t  written to by the orphan. Consequences: the orphan keeps talking to the API after the operator\n242\t  killed the tool, and response headers — where `Set-Cookie` and refreshed tokens land — are left in\n243\t  TMPDIR indefinitely. `cleanupWith`'s credential zeroing (internal/curl/config.go:250) never runs\n244\t  either. Interactive Ctrl-C is partly mitigated by process-group signalling, but the temp-dir leak\n245\t  happens there too, and the pid-directed kill an agent harness issues is unmitigated. Given\n246\t  Finding 4, a hung `run` is exactly the thing a harness kills, so these accumulate.\n247\t- **Suggested fix:** `exec.CommandContext` with a context cancelled by `signal.NotifyContext(SIGINT,\n248\t  SIGTERM)` installed in cmd/talaria/root.go, plus `cmd.WaitDelay` so the child is killed and reaped;\n249\t  run both cleanup funcs from that signal path, not only from `defer`.\n250\t\n251\t## Finding 13: The canary suite still skips the response-validation error path on a stale premise\n252\t- **Reviewer:** security\n253\t- **Severity:** WARN\n254\t- **File:** internal/canary/canary_test.go:424\n255\t- **Description:** The comment reads \"A response-validation failure is not here because response\n256\t  validation is not built yet (plan tasks 26 and 27). Whoever adds `--fail-on-error` adds the case.\"\n257\t  `--fail-on-error` shipped on this same branch (cmd/talaria/call.go:213, cmd/talaria/run.go:147),\n258\t  `internal/validate` exists, and internal/e2e/e2e_test.go exercises the flag in four places. The\n259\t  comment is now false and `TestErrorPathsDoNotLeakTheCredential` still has no exit-4 stage, leaving\n260\t  DESIGN.md §5a's named leak channel \"validation errors quoting the request\" uncovered by the suite\n261\t  that §5a says \"gates every release\". The path traces clean today — `validateResponse` runs on the\n262\t  redacted `responseView` and on `curl.URL(req)` — *except* for Finding 1's query case, which this\n263\t  stage would have caught. A comment documenting a deliberate gap is fine; one documenting a gap on\n264\t  grounds that no longer hold is how a gate quietly stops covering something.\n265\t- **Suggested fix:** Add a `{name: \"response validation\", code: 4}` stage driving `call\n266\t  --fail-on-error` against a server returning a schema-violating body, and delete the stale comment.\n267\t\n268\t## Finding 14: The missing-credential message is garbled: \"no credential for security 1 scheme petKey\"\n269\t- **Reviewer:** spec-compliance\n270\t- **Severity:** WARN\n271\t- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:325\n272\t- **Description:** `res.skip(\"no credential for security %s\", pluralise(len(missing), \"scheme\")+\" \"+\n273\t  strings.Join(missing, \", \"))` interpolates the count between the words \"security\" and \"scheme\",\n274\t  producing \"no credential for security 1 scheme petKey\". cmd/talaria/run.go:385 repeats the same\n275\t  construction for the exit-5 error. The string lands in the JSON `reason`, in the JUnit `<skipped>`\n276\t  element, and on stderr — all three of which AGENT.md instructs the agent to relay verbatim to a\n277\t  human, so the human is asked to act on a sentence that does not parse.\n278\t- **Suggested fix:** `\"no credential for %s %s\"` with `pluralise(n, \"security scheme\")` and a colon\n279\t  before the joined names, at both call sites.\n280\t\n281\t## Finding 15: `history replay` skips the query-string credential warning\n282\t- **Reviewer:** integration\n283\t- **Severity:** WARN\n284\t- **File:** cmd/talaria/history.go:201\n285\t- **Description:** DESIGN.md §5a requires warning once when a credential travels in the query string,\n286\t  because it lands in the server's access log regardless of what talaria redacts. `call` and `run`\n287\t  both call `warnQueryCredentials`; `replay` re-issues the identical request and does not. Verified:\n288\t  `talaria call … getKeyed` printed the warning; `talaria history replay 1` printed nothing on stderr\n289\t  while the server logged `/keyed?api_key=qk`. An agent whose only path to a keyed endpoint is replay\n290\t  never learns the key is being logged.\n291\t- **Suggested fix:** Call `warnQueryCredentials(cmd.ErrOrStderr(), warner, req)` after `replayRequest`\n292\t  returns, with a warner constructed alongside the command as `call` does.\n293\t\n294\t## Finding 16: A remote spec is cached forever with no expiry and no way to refresh\n295\t- **Reviewer:** integration\n296\t- **Severity:** WARN\n297\t- **File:** internal/spec/source.go:87\n298\t- **Description:** `loadURL` returns the cached bytes whenever the file exists. Nothing writes a\n299\t  timestamp, checks one, sends a conditional request, or exposes a `--refresh`/`--no-cache` flag.\n300\t  README.md:48 states the behaviour (\"fetched once per URL\") but neither it nor AGENT.md says how to\n301\t  invalidate it. Once an API publishes a changed `openapi.json`, every `list`/`describe`/`call`/`run`\n302\t  against that URL keeps binding to the stale operation model — new operations invisible, removed ones\n303\t  still callable, response contracts stale — until someone finds and deletes a SHA-256-named file\n304\t  under `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/talaria/specs`. That is precisely the failure mode the spec-centric pitch\n305\t  (\"nothing to curate, nothing to drift\", §2) exists to prevent, and an agent has no way to detect or\n306\t  fix it.\n307\t- **Suggested fix:** Add an mtime-based TTL (e.g. 1h) plus a `--refresh` flag on the root command that\n308\t  bypasses the cache read, and document both in README.md and AGENT.md.\n309\t\n310\t## Finding 17: `run` on its own never exits 4, contradicting the DESIGN.md exit table\n311\t- **Reviewer:** spec-compliance\n312\t- **Severity:** WARN\n313\t- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:388\n314\t- **Description:** `verdict` returns `clierr.Validation` only when `failOnError` is set, so a bare\n315\t  `talaria run` over an API returning undocumented statuses or schema-violating bodies exits 0.\n316\t  DESIGN.md §4 assigns code 4 to \"Validation failure (response violates spec) — only with\n317\t  `--fail-on-error` / `run`\", which reads as `run` exiting 4 in its own right. README.md:418 documents\n318\t  the implemented behaviour instead (\"Failing operations exit 0 unless you pass `--fail-on-error`,\n319\t  which makes them exit 4\") while README.md's exit-code table repeats the DESIGN.md wording. This is\n320\t  recorded as WARN rather than CRIT because the DESIGN.md phrasing is genuinely ambiguous — it can be\n321\t  read as \"in the context of the `run` command\" — and the implemented behaviour is coherent and\n322\t  consistently documented in README prose. It still needs a decision rather than a drift.\n323\t- **Suggested fix:** Pick one. Either make `run` exit 4 on validation failure regardless of\n324\t  `--fail-on-error`, or amend DESIGN.md §4's exit table and README.md's table to say code 4 requires\n325\t  `--fail-on-error` in both `call` and `run`.\n326\t\n327\t## Finding 18: `talaria auth` with no subcommand prints help to stdout and exits 0\n328\t- **Reviewer:** spec-compliance\n329\t- **Severity:** WARN\n330\t- **File:** cmd/talaria/auth.go:31\n331\t- **Description:** The `auth` parent command has no `RunE` and no `Args` constraint, so cobra's\n332\t  default help handler writes usage text to stdout and returns nil. An agent that mistypes `talaria\n333\t  auth` instead of `talaria auth check` receives non-JSON on stdout and a success code, so it has no\n334\t  signal that anything went wrong — the same class of contract break as Finding 10, in a command whose\n335\t  whole purpose is telling an agent whether credentials are in place.\n336\t- **Suggested fix:** Give the parent a `RunE` returning `clierr.Usage` naming the valid subcommands, or\n337\t  set `Args: cobra.NoArgs` with `RunE` that errors. Both put the message in the envelope on stderr with\n338\t  exit 2.\n339\t\n340\t---\n341\t\n342\t## Finding 19: The `authorization` redaction pattern is exact-match, missing common spellings\n343\t- **Reviewer:** security\n344\t- **Severity:** INFO\n345\t- **File:** internal/secret/redact.go:20\n346\t- **Description:** `compileGlob` anchors a pattern containing no `*` to the whole header name, so\n347\t  `authorization` matches only that exact header. Verified: `--header 'X-Authorization=Bearer SEKRIT1'\n348\t  --header 'X-Auth=SEKRIT2' --header 'Authentication=SEKRIT3'` all render unredacted in the `headers`\n349\t  block. This matches DESIGN.md §5a's list literally, so it is a faithful implementation of the design\n350\t  rather than a bug, and `redact.headers` in the config file is the sanctioned escape hatch — hence\n351\t  INFO. But the built-in list is explicitly the non-configurable floor, and `*auth*` would subsume\n352\t  `authorization`, `proxy-authorization`, `x-authorization`, `x-auth-token` and `authentication` at the\n353\t  cost of a few harmless false positives.\n354\t- **Suggested fix:** Replace `authorization` and `proxy-authorization` with a single `*auth*` pattern,\n355\t  and note the widening in the DESIGN.md §5a table so doc and code stay in step.\n356\t\n357\t## Finding 20: `exclusiveMaximum` is carried by the strict-3.0 fixture but never asserted\n358\t- **Reviewer:** spec-compliance\n359\t- **Severity:** INFO\n360\t- **File:** internal/validate/validate_test.go:277\n361\t- **Description:** The DESIGN.md edit on this branch claims the strict-3.0 fixture is \"pinned … so a\n362\t  library upgrade that re-imposed 3.1 semantics would fail the suite\", naming four constructs. Three\n363\t  are asserted; `exclusiveMaximum` is present in internal/validate/testdata/strict-3.0.yaml but no test\n364\t  drives a value above the bound. I confirmed by hand that the library does translate it correctly\n365\t  (`id: 100` is rejected), so the claim is true — it is just not held true by the suite.\n366\t- **Suggested fix:** Add an `id: 100` case to the exclusive-bound test so all four constructs named in\n367\t  DESIGN.md §8 are actually pinned.\n368\t"
      }
    ]
