        "text": "Task 3 complete and committed.\n\n**What changed**\n- `internal/curl/config.go` — `HEAD` now emits the bare `head` flag instead of `request = \"HEAD\"`, and for that method the `output` directive points at `os.DevNull` so curl's header block doesn't land in the response body. `dump-header` still captures the headers; the staged empty body file keeps `readResponse` unchanged.\n- `internal/curl/render.go` — `HEAD` renders `-I`; `bodyArgs` uses `--data-raw` instead of `--data-binary`, matching the config document.\n- Tests: a config test pinning `head` + `/dev/null` output, an executor test that drives real curl against a server answering HEAD with `Content-Length: 42` and no body (with a 10s deadline so a regression fails rather than hangs), and two render tests pinning `-I` and `--data-raw '@/etc/hostname'`. The existing mutation render test was updated to `--data-raw`.\n\n**Verification**\n- Red first: all four new assertions failed for the right reason, and the executor test genuinely hung for the full 10s.\n- `go test ./internal/curl/...` → ok; `go test ./...` → clean; `go build ./...` → clean; `test -z \"$(gofmt -l .)\" && go vet ./...` → clean.\n- Both plan checkboxes confirmed against the real binary: `talaria call … headPets` returned `status 200`, `timing_ms 1`, empty body, exit 0 (was exit 124/18); and the emitted `--data-raw '@/etc/hostname'`, pasted verbatim, made the server receive the literal `@/etc/hostname` — identical to what talaria itself put on the wire.\n\nNo doc updates were needed: no commands, endpoints, env vars, or workflows changed, and no doc referenced the old `--data-binary` or `-X HEAD` forms.\n\nStopping here — one task per session. Tasks 4–9 remain."
      }
    ],
