        "tool_use_id": "toolu_014xrw7nX7y3XjpcRX6GsyRE",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "83\t## Finding 4: No timeout of any kind on the curl subprocess\n84\t- **Reviewer:** concurrency\n85\t- **Severity:** CRIT\n86\t- **File:** internal/curl/config.go:94\n87\t- **Description:** `document.build` emits only `url, request, header, cookie, data-raw, silent,\n88\t  show-error, write-out, output, dump-header` — no `max-time`, no `connect-timeout` — and\n89\t  internal/curl/exec.go:64 uses `exec.Command`, not `exec.CommandContext`. `grep -rn\n90\t  \"Timeout|context\\.\" --include=*.go` over non-test code matches only internal/spec/source.go, so\n91\t  there is no timeout anywhere in the call path and no flag to bound one. Verified: against an\n92\t  endpoint sleeping 90s, `Execute` was still blocked at 25s; against a server that never answers it\n93\t  blocks indefinitely. The impact is worst for `run` — the suite stalls on operation *k* of *n* and\n94\t  never emits its report, so CI or an agent gets no partial output at all. The design doc applies\n95\t  exactly this reasoning to the *spec fetch* (internal/spec/source.go:21 — \"A spec that never arrives\n96\t  has to become an exit code rather than a hung process an agent cannot interpret\") while leaving\n97\t  the higher-risk path unbounded.\n98\t- **Suggested fix:** Emit `connect-timeout` and `max-time` directives in `document.build`, defaulted\n99\t  and overridable via a `--timeout` flag. curl enforces these itself and exits 28, which `runFailure`\n100\t  already classifies correctly, so no Go-side plumbing is needed on the happy path. Pair with\n101\t  `exec.CommandContext` plus `cmd.WaitDelay` at a slightly larger deadline so a curl that ignores its\n102\t  own timeout is still reaped.\n103\t\n104\t## Finding 5: `corpus.trim` is an unlocked read-modify-write that silently destroys history entries"
      }
    ]
  },
