      {
        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01FLygTCA5JUa7vFJbMHL6Gb",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "197\t### Task 5: Serialise history appends so trim cannot destroy entries\n198\t\n199\t**Fixes findings:** #5\n200\t\n201\t**Files:**\n202\t- `internal/corpus/store.go` (modify)\n203\t- `internal/corpus/lock_unix.go` (create)\n204\t- `internal/corpus/lock_other.go` (create)\n205\t- `internal/corpus/store_test.go` (modify)\n206\t\n207\t**Steps:**\n208\t1. `Append` (store.go:104) does an atomic O_APPEND `write` and then `trim`, which reads the whole\n209\t   file, filters, and replaces it. Nothing serialises the two, so any entry appended between\n210\t   `trim`'s `os.ReadFile` (store.go:179) and its `Rename` (store.go:224) is silently dropped —\n211\t   `Append` returned nil for it, so the \"not recorded in history\" warning never fires.\n212\t2. Take an exclusive advisory lock for the whole of `Append` — write *and* trim — released with\n213\t   `defer`. Lock a sibling `history.jsonl.lock` file rather than the store itself, so the lock\n214\t   survives `replace`'s rename. Keep `Read` lock-free: `replace`'s temp+rename is already correct\n215\t   for readers.\n216\t3. Put the lock behind two small files so the package still builds everywhere:\n217\t   `lock_unix.go` (`//go:build unix`) using `syscall.Flock` with `LOCK_EX`, and `lock_other.go`\n218\t   (`//go:build !unix`) whose acquire is a no-op. Do not add a new module dependency.\n219\t4. Regression test: seed a store to `maxPerSource-1` entries for one source, then run N\n220\t   goroutines × M `Append`s, and assert every entry that returned nil is present afterwards.\n221\t   Run it under `-race`. Below the cap the trim path returns early (store.go:203), so the test\n222\t   must seed *over* the cap boundary to exercise the rewrite.\n223\t\n224\t**Verify:** `go test -race -count=3 ./internal/corpus/...`\n225\t- [ ] The concurrency test loses zero entries where the pre-fix code lost ~60 of 80.\n226\t- [ ] Two `talaria` processes appending to the same `history.jsonl` both keep their entries.\n227\t"
      }
    ]
  },
  "parent_tool_use_id": null,
  "session_id": "3d6a7496-a0d0-44bf-a370-5b836be66f57",
  "uuid": "2991a188-1e81-4de0-b465-7fd83d5a6236",
