        "text": "Task 5 done and committed. Stopping here.\n\n**What changed**\n\n`Store.Append` did an atomic `O_APPEND` write followed by `trim`, which reads the whole file, filters, and replaces it by rename. Nothing serialised the two, so any entry appended between `trim`'s `ReadFile` and its `Rename` was silently dropped — and because `Append` had already returned `nil` for it, the \"not recorded in history\" warning never fired.\n\n- `internal/corpus/lock_unix.go` (new) — `flock(LOCK_EX)` on a sibling `history.jsonl.lock`. The sibling matters: `trim` replaces the store by rename, so a descriptor held on the store itself would guard an orphaned inode and the next appender would see no contention.\n- `internal/corpus/lock_other.go` (new) — `//go:build !unix` no-op, so the package still builds everywhere with no new module dependency.\n- `internal/corpus/store.go` — `Append` holds the lock across both the write and the trim. `Read` stays lock-free; temp+rename is already correct for readers.\n- `README.md` — documented the sibling lock file and the multi-process guarantee.\n\n**Verification**\n\n- Red first: the new test failed at `Append returned nil for 60 of 80 entries that are not in the store` — matching the plan's ~60-of-80 prediction, so it was failing for the right reason.\n- `go test -race -count=3 ./internal/corpus/...` → ok (76s).\n- Full suite `go test ./...` → all 15 packages ok.\n- `gofmt -l` clean, `go vet ./...` clean.\n- Cross-compiled `./internal/corpus/` for `GOOS=windows` and `GOOS=js` to confirm the `!unix` path actually builds.\n\nOne note on the plan's second checkbox — \"two `talaria` processes appending to the same file both keep their entries\" — I verified this by construction rather than by spawning two processes: every `Append` opens its own descriptor, and flock is per open file description, so the goroutines in the test contend through exactly the same mechanism separate processes would. If you want a genuine two-process test, that'd be a separate e2e-level addition."
