        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "126:## Finding 6: `run` sends its generated JSON body under the wrong `Content-Type`\n127-- **Reviewer:** integration\n128-- **Severity:** CRIT\n129-- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:338\n130-- **Description:** `gen.bodySchema` (internal/gen/fixtures.go:228) deliberately selects the *JSON*\n131-  media type's schema to generate from, but that choice is never communicated onward: `run` passes\n132-  only the bytes, and `binder.contentType` independently picks `rb.Content[0].ContentType`\n133-  (internal/request/body.go:123). When the JSON media type is not first, the two disagree. Verified\n134-  against a local echo server: a 3.0 spec declaring `application/xml` before `application/json`\n135-  produced `Content-Type: application/xml` with body `{\"name\":\"foxtrot-906\"}`. It is worse on the\n136-  Swagger 2.0 path, where it is unconditional — every converted `formData` operation gets\n137-  `Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded` (or `multipart/form-data`) carrying a JSON body.\n138-  talaria reported `outcome: passed` because the echo server accepts anything; a real API returns\n139-  415/400 and the smoke test then reports a spec bug that is actually talaria's.\n140-- **Suggested fix:** Have `gen.Data` carry the media type `bodySchema` selected, and have `run` pass\n141-  it as an explicit `Content-Type` in `request.Inputs.Headers`. `binder.contentType` already lets a\n142-  user-set header win, so nothing else changes.\n143-\n144-## Finding 7: A non-UTF-8 request body is silently corrupted in the corpus and replayed corrupted\n145-- **Reviewer:** integration\n146-- **Severity:** CRIT\n147-- **File:** internal/corpus/entry.go:216\n148-- **Description:** `newBody` does `body.Data = string(data)`, and `Entry` is marshalled with\n149-  `encoding/json`, which replaces every invalid byte with U+FFFD. `replayRequest` then does\n150-  `Data: []byte(body.Data)` (cmd/talaria/history.go:511) with no guard — it checks `Truncated` and\n151-  refuses, but has nothing to check for this. Verified: `talaria call … --body @bin.dat` (1024 bytes,\n152-  `bytes(range(256))*4`) reached the server as exactly 1024 bytes; `talaria history replay N\n153-  --allow-mutations` sent **2048 bytes**, silently, with no warning. internal/curl/config.go:192\n154-  carries a whole temp-file path specifically so binary bodies survive exec, so one component handles\n155-  them correctly and the next one mangles them. DESIGN.md §5 makes this store the twin's corpus, so a\n156-  Phase 6+ twin would replay the corrupted bytes too.",
        "is_error": false
      }
    ]
  },
  "parent_tool_use_id": null,
