      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Task 6 complete and committed as `f176666`.\n\n**What was wrong:** `gen.bodySchema` deliberately picks the JSON media type to generate from, but that choice never travelled onward — `request.binder.contentType` independently took `RequestBody.Content[0].ContentType`. Reproduced exactly as the reviewer described: a 3.0 spec listing `application/xml` first sent `{\"name\":\"foxtrot-906\"}` as `Content-Type: application/xml`, and a converted Swagger 2.0 `formData` operation sent the same JSON as `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`.\n\n**Fix:**\n- `internal/gen/fixtures.go`: `bodySchema` now returns the media type alongside the schema; `Data` gained a `ContentType` field. Since everything the package produces is JSON (generated values marshalled by `encoding/json`, fixture bodies compacted as JSON), a non-JSON or absent declared media type yields `application/json`, while a JSON media type keeps its own spelling (`application/vnd.api+json`). No body → no media type.\n- `cmd/talaria/run.go`: `headersFor` merges the fixture headers with a `Content-Type` for the generated body, and a fixture-set `Content-Type` wins.\n\n**Tests:** 3 new gen tests (media-type selection table, fixture body on an undeclared request body, no-body case) and 2 new `run` tests over two new testdata specs (`run_media.yaml`, `run_formdata.yaml`) asserting on what the server actually received. Both `run` tests failed for the right reason before the fix.\n\n**Verification:** `go test ./...` all packages ok; `gofmt`/`go vet` clean. README's `run` section documents the new media-type rule."
      }
    ],
