        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "262\t---\n263\t\n264\t### Task 7: Preserve non-UTF-8 request bodies in the corpus, and refuse lossy replays\n265\t\n266\t**Fixes findings:** #7\n267\t\n268\t**Files:**\n269\t- `internal/corpus/entry.go` (modify)\n270\t- `cmd/talaria/history.go` (modify)\n271\t- `internal/corpus/entry_test.go` (modify)\n272\t- `cmd/talaria/history_test.go` (modify)\n273\t\n274\t**Steps:**\n275\t1. `newBody` (entry.go:206) does `body.Data = string(data)` and the entry is marshalled with\n276\t   `encoding/json`, which replaces every invalid byte with U+FFFD. `replayRequest`\n277\t   (history.go:511) then does `Data: []byte(body.Data)` with no guard, so a 1024-byte binary body\n278\t   replays as 2048 bytes, silently.\n279\t2. Add an `Encoding string \\`json:\"encoding,omitempty\"\\`` field to `corpus.Body`. In `newBody`,\n280\t   when `!utf8.Valid(data)` after truncation, store `base64.StdEncoding.EncodeToString(data)` in\n281\t   `Data` and set `Encoding` to `\"base64\"`. Leave valid UTF-8 exactly as it is today, so existing\n282\t   stores and their tests are unaffected and the common case stays readable.\n283\t3. In `replayRequest`, decode `\"base64\"` back to bytes before building `request.Body`. Refuse any\n284\t   `Encoding` value it does not recognise with a `clierr.Usage` error, the way it already refuses\n285\t   `Truncated` — a store written by a newer talaria must not be replayed as literal text.\n286\t4. Check every other reader of `Body.Data` (`history show`, and any display path) and render a\n287\t   base64 body without pretending it is text.\n288\t5. Tests: a body of `bytes(range(256))*4` round-trips byte-for-byte through\n289\t   `Entry` → JSON → `replayRequest`; an unknown `encoding` is refused; a UTF-8 body's stored form\n290\t   is unchanged from today.\n291\t\n292\t**Verify:** `go test ./internal/corpus/... ./cmd/talaria/...`\n293\t- [ ] `talaria history replay N` sends exactly the bytes the original call sent.\n294\t- [ ] The corpus the twin will read (DESIGN.md §5) holds the real bytes, not U+FFFD.\n295\t\n296\t---\n297\t"
      }
    ]
  },
