        "text": "Task 9 done — and that was the last one, so all 9 in `tasks.json` are now `done: true`.\n\n**What changed** (`internal/spec/load.go:71`): `libopenapi.NewDocument(data)` left the document config nil, so `BuildV3Model` fell back to a default whose logger is a JSON `slog` handler writing to the real `os.Stdout` — bypassing `cmd.OutOrStdout()`, the `talaria/v1` envelope and every redaction path. Parsing now goes through `NewDocumentWithConfiguration` with a config whose `Logger` points at `io.Discard`, leaving `AllowFileReferences`/`AllowRemoteReferences` at their `false` defaults.\n\n**Red → green:** the new test swaps `os.Stdout` for an `os.Pipe` around the load (the file descriptor is the channel that leaked, not a cobra writer). It failed with exactly the two `{\"time\":…,\"level\":\"ERROR\"}` documents the plan predicted, and passes now. Two further tests pin remote and file `$ref` resolution as disabled — that's the protection an explicit config could silently drop.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `go test ./...` — all 15 packages ok\n- Lint (`gofmt -l` + `go vet`) — clean\n- `talaria list <unresolvable-ref spec> --output json 2>/dev/null` → 0 documents on stdout, exit 3, one `talaria/v1` error envelope on stderr\n- A well-formed spec → exactly 1 JSON document on stdout\n\n**Docs:** the final conventions pass found README:184 already documented the http/https base-URL restriction from task 2, but the agent-facing `--base-url` entry in `AGENT.md` did not — added there, committed separately.\n\nTwo commits: `a4f61a8` (fix + `tasks.json`) and `4cde768` (doc). Stopping here rather than starting anything else."
