    "role": "assistant",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "tool_use",
        "id": "toolu_01VEE42UpZAaaZAFzjcQnCtC",
        "name": "Agent",
        "input": {
          "description": "Security review",
          "subagent_type": "general-purpose",
          "run_in_background": true,
          "prompt": "You are the SECURITY reviewer for a code review of the branch `ralph/design` in /home/casper/projects/talaria (Go CLI called `talaria`). Base branch is `main`. Review the FULL branch diff: `git diff main...HEAD`.\n\nPRODUCT CONTEXT — this matters enormously: talaria is an OpenAPI CLI whose *entire reason to exist* is a \"credential firewall\". Read `docs/design/DESIGN.md` §3 principle 0, §5a in full, and §5 \"Auth\". The invariant is: **no consumer of stdout or stderr ever observes a credential value.** Secrets must be structurally `SecretRef` values everywhere except `internal/curl` at exec time, where they are written to a `curl -K -` config document on curl's stdin (never argv, because /proc/*/cmdline is world-readable).\n\nFocus your review on these packages (read the actual source, not just the diff hunks):\n- internal/secret/ (redact.go, response.go, secret.go) — the trust boundary itself\n- internal/curl/ (config.go, exec.go, render.go, version.go) — argv construction, config doc escaping, temp files\n- internal/corpus/ (store.go, entry.go, lock_unix.go) — history is a PERMANENT artifact, redaction must be at WRITE time\n- internal/config/ (auth.go, config.go) — profile file, env var mapping, file permissions (design says mode 0600)\n- internal/canary/ — the leak test suite that is supposed to catch all of this\n- cmd/talaria/*.go — every output surface: json, pretty, tsv, dry-run, error messages, junit, history show/replay\n\nYour checklist:\n1. **Credential leakage** — trace every path a resolved secret value can take. Can it reach stdout, stderr, the corpus, a JUnit report, an error message, a temp file that outlives exec, or curl's argv? Check the emitted/dry-run curl is *always* symbolic. Check query-string API keys, cookies, basic auth. Check that error paths built from a request use the redacted representation.\n2. **Config-document escaping** (internal/curl/config.go) — curl's `-K` format has specific quoting/escaping rules. If a header value, URL, or body containing a `\"`, backslash, newline, or leading/trailing whitespace is written unescaped, an attacker-controlled or merely awkward value could inject a *new curl directive* (e.g. a newline followed by `output = /etc/foo` or `proxy = ...`). This is the highest-value thing to check. Verify against curl's real config-file parsing rules.\n3. **Command/argument injection** — anything that reaches os/exec argv; any place a value could be interpreted as a curl flag (leading `-`).\n4. **Path traversal** — spec cache paths, `--fixtures dir/`, `--corpus`, `--body @file`, history file naming. Are operationIds (attacker-controllable, they come from the spec) used to build filesystem paths without sanitisation? A spec is untrusted input fetched from a URL.\n5. **SSRF / scheme restriction** — base URLs and spec URLs. A prior review fixed http/https restriction; verify it is complete and covers redirects (does curl follow redirects? `--location`?) and the spec-fetch path too.\n6. **File permissions** — corpus, history, cache, temp files, config. Secrets or real response data must not be world-readable.\n7. **Redaction completeness** — the built-in sensitive-header list, response `Set-Cookie`, response body paths. Is matching case-insensitive? Does it cover the spellings that matter?\n8. **Secrets committed in the diff** — any real credential, key, or token.\n\nIMPORTANT CONTEXT: a previous review cycle already found and FIXED these — do NOT re-report them unless the fix is incomplete or wrong (in which case say precisely how): query-string credential redaction; http/https base-URL restriction; `-X HEAD`; curl subprocess timeouts; corpus.trim locking; run's body Content-Type; non-UTF-8 bodies in corpus; `--data-binary` `@` file read in emitted curl; unknown-command exit 2; version JSON envelope; libopenapi logs on stdout. Verify each fix actually holds, then hunt for NEW issues.\n\nAlso do NOT report pre-existing issues outside the branch diff, and do not report things documented as accepted limitations in DESIGN.md §5a \"Threats explicitly not covered\".\n\nVerify claims before reporting. Where feasible, actually run the binary or write a small Go test to prove a leak or an escaping bug — a confirmed finding is worth ten speculative ones. `go build ./...` works; the module is github.com/Teeeep/talaria.\n\nReport back a list of findings. For EACH finding give exactly:\n- Title (one line)\n- Severity: CRIT (real vulnerability, data loss, broken security invariant) | WARN (missing hardening, defence in depth) | INFO (style/doc)\n- File:line (mandatory, must be precise)\n- Description: what is wrong and why it matters\n- Suggested fix: concrete steps or code\n- Whether you CONFIRMED it by execution/test or it is reasoned-only\n\nBe honest about severity — a leak of an actual credential value is CRIT; a theoretical hardening gap is WARN. Report each distinct issue once."
        },
        "caller": {
          "type": "direct"
        }
      }
    ],
