          "subagent_type": "general-purpose",
          "run_in_background": true,
          "prompt": "You are the SPEC-COMPLIANCE reviewer for a code review of branch `ralph/design` in /home/casper/projects/talaria (Go CLI `talaria`). Base branch `main`. Review the FULL branch diff: `git diff main...HEAD`.\n\nTHE SPEC IS `docs/design/DESIGN.md`. Read it completely and carefully. Also read `README.md` and `AGENT.md` (both are shipped deliverables and DESIGN.md §3 principle 7 says AGENT.md is a first-class deliverable to be treated with the same care as code).\n\nThe branch implements Phases 1–4 of the §7 roadmap:\n- Phase 1: `list`, `describe`, `search`, `uses`, `call --dry-run`, spec loading (3.x via libopenapi, 2.0 via openapi2conv), operation model, curl builder\n- Phase 2: real execution, the credential firewall (all of §5a), `history`, JSON output, exit codes, `--base-url`, profiles, env-var auth, mutation gating, `auth check`, exit code 5, canary CI suite, corpus store, AGENT.md v1\n- Phase 3: response validation, `validation` block, exit code 4\n- Phase 4: `run` smoke mode, tag/operation filters, examples→fixtures→gen data priority chain, JUnit/JSON reports\nPhases 5–8 (twin, recording proxy) are explicitly NOT in scope — their absence is not a finding.\n\nYour checklist:\n1. **Completeness** — go through §4 \"CLI surface\" line by line. Every command and EVERY FLAG listed there for phases 1–4 must exist and behave as described. Build a table: flag → implemented? → behaves as documented? Check `list --tag`, `--output json|pretty|tsv`, `describe`, `search --kind operation|schema|param`, `uses`, all of `call`'s flags (`--param`, `--query`, `--header`, `--body` with '@file' and '-', `--base-url`, `--profile`, `--dry-run`, `--allow-mutations`, `--timeout`, `--output`), `history` with `--operation`/`--since`/`--status`/`--output` plus `show`/`replay`, `run` with `--tag`/`--operation`/`--base-url`/`--profile`/`--allow-mutations`/`--report json|junit|pretty`/`--timeout`/`--fail-on-error`, `auth check [--profile]`, `version`.\n2. **Exit codes** — §4 has an exact table (0,1,2,3,4,5). Verify each is produced in the circumstances described and NOT in others. Especially: HTTP 4xx/5xx is exit 0 for `call`; exit 4 only with `--fail-on-error`/`run`; exit 5 distinct from exit 2 for missing credentials.\n3. **Output contract** — §4 \"Output shape for call\" gives a JSON sketch with `schema`, `request.curl`, `request.method`, `request.url`, `request.headers`, `response.status/headers/body/timing_ms`, `validation.status_documented/body_valid/errors`. Does the real output match these field names and nesting? Is `\"schema\": \"talaria/v1\"` on every command's output (§3.1 says versioned output schema)? Is the default really pretty-on-TTY / JSON-when-piped (§3.1, §8 \"Default output\" locked)?\n4. **Behavioural correctness** — does it do what the doc *describes*, not just what it names? e.g. §5 \"Test data for run mode\" mandates the priority order spec `example`/`examples` → `--fixtures dir/` matched by operationId → schema-generated. Is that order actually implemented? §3.5 safe-by-default: read-only GET/HEAD/OPTIONS unless `--allow-mutations`. §3.1 \"Never prompt. Never page. No interactivity, ever.\" §3.6 \"Spec via arg, `--spec`, or env var\" — does the env var exist and is it named consistently?\n5. **Drift** — anything in the code that contradicts the doc without a recorded reason. Also check the reverse: does README.md or AGENT.md document behaviour the binary does not have, or omit behaviour it does? An AGENT.md that lies to an LLM is a real defect.\n6. **Edge cases the doc calls out** — §5 library choices: \"Phase 1 must verify conversion fidelity against real 2.0 specs\"; the libopenapi-validator 3.0 strictness question (§8, marked Resolved with a fixture at internal/validate/testdata/strict-3.0.yaml — verify that fixture actually asserts all four constructs it claims); curl >= 7.70 hard floor with a version preflight (§3.4); the `--write-out '%{json}'` requirement.\n\nIMPORTANT: a previous review already found and FIXED these — do NOT re-report unless the fix is incomplete: query-string credential redaction; http/https base-URL restriction; `-X HEAD`; curl timeouts; corpus.trim locking; run body Content-Type; non-UTF-8 corpus bodies; `--data-binary @`; unknown-command exit 2; `version` ignoring `--output`; libopenapi logs on stdout; garbled missing-credential message; `history replay` skipping the query-key warning; spec cache never expiring; `run` never exiting 4; `talaria auth` with no subcommand exiting 0.\n\nVerify by RUNNING the binary — `go build -o /tmp/talaria ./cmd/talaria` then exercise commands against the testdata specs in cmd/talaria/testdata/ and internal/*/testdata/. Do not report a missing feature you did not try to invoke. Do not report on Phases 5-8.\n\nReport back a list of findings, each with exactly:\n- Title (one line)\n- Severity: CRIT (unmet design-doc requirement, broken documented behaviour) | WARN (spec drift, doc/code mismatch) | INFO (minor doc gap)\n- File:line (mandatory and precise)\n- Description: what the doc requires vs what the code does\n- Suggested fix\n- Whether you CONFIRMED by running it\n\nBe honest about severity. A missing flag from §4 is CRIT; a slightly differently-worded help string is INFO."
