{
  "type": "user",
  "message": {
    "role": "user",
    "content": [
      {
        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Nuxxs2MpMoEAGtfPqn3bsU",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "1\t# talaria — operating manual for agents\n2\t\n3\tYou drive an HTTP API through this binary. It reads an OpenAPI/Swagger spec and runs the calls\n4\tthrough curl. **You never see credentials.** Values are resolved inside the process at the moment\n5\tof the request and appear in no output — not in JSON, not in errors, not in the curl it prints,\n6\tnot in history. You work with credential *names*.\n7\t\n8\tThere is no `--show-secrets` flag and no way to reveal a value. Asking a human to paste one into\n9\tyour context is not a supported workflow — see [Credentials](#credentials) for what to do instead.\n10\t\n11\t## The loop\n12\t\n13\t```sh\n14\ttalaria list ./openapi.yaml                       # what operations exist\n15\ttalaria search ./openapi.yaml invoice             # find one by concept\n16\ttalaria describe ./openapi.yaml getPet            # its params, body, responses\n17\ttalaria call ./openapi.yaml getPet --param petId=42 --dry-run   # check the request\n18\ttalaria call ./openapi.yaml getPet --param petId=42             # send it\n19\t```\n20\t\n21\tDo not read the spec file yourself. It is often megabytes; these commands are the point.\n22\t\n23\t## The spec\n24\t\n25\tEvery spec-reading command takes it from the first of: the positional argument, `--spec`, then\n26\t`$TALARIA_SPEC`. Export `TALARIA_SPEC` once and drop the argument. A file path or an `http(s)`\n27\tURL, OpenAPI 3.x or Swagger 2.0.\n28\t\n29\tWith two positional arguments the spec is first (`talaria describe ./openapi.yaml getPet`); with\n30\tone, the argument is the operationId.\n31\t\n32\t## Commands\n33\t\n34\t| Command | What it gives you |\n35\t|---|---|\n36\t| `talaria list [spec] [--tag t]` | one line per operation: method, path, operationId, summary |\n37\t| `talaria search [spec] <query> [--kind operation\\|schema\\|param]` | substring match across operations, schemas and params |\n38\t| `talaria describe [spec] <operationId> [--depth n]` | one operation's params, request body and responses |\n39\t| `talaria uses [spec] <schema>` | every operation that touches a component schema |\n40\t| `talaria call [spec] <operationId>` | build and send one request; `--dry-run` builds only, `--fail-on-error` exits 4 on an HTTP or spec violation |\n41\t| `talaria run [spec] [--tag t] [--operation id]` | smoke-test many operations at once and report one line each |\n42\t| `talaria auth check [spec]` | which credentials the spec needs and whether they are set |\n43\t| `talaria history [--operation id] [--since 1h] [--status 4xx] [--source call\\|run\\|replay]` | what has already been called |\n44\t| `talaria history show <n>` | one recorded request/response in full |\n45\t| `talaria history replay <n>` | send a recorded request again |\n46\t| `talaria version` | the binary's version |\n47\t\n48\tOperations the spec did not name get a synthesised operationId (`GET /pets/{petId}` →\n49\t`getPetsByPetId`). It is stable and callable.\n50\t\n51\t## Calling\n52\t\n53\t```sh\n54\ttalaria call getPet --param petId=42 --query verbose=true --header 'X-Trace: abc'\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\t- `--param name=value` binds a parameter the operation declares, in any location. `--query` and\n58\t  `--header` add ones it does not. All three repeat.\n59\t- `--body` takes a literal, `@file`, or `-` for stdin. Content type comes from your\n60\t  `--header Content-Type`, else from the operation's declared media type.\n61\t- Every binding problem is reported at once. Fix them in one edit, not one per run.\n62\t- `--base-url` overrides the spec's server. `--profile` selects a named profile from the user's\n63\t  config file. Only `http` and `https` are accepted, from either source — a spec's own\n64\t  `servers[0].url` is untrusted input, so a `file://` or `gopher://` base is a usage error\n65\t  (exit 2) rather than a request.\n66\t- Every request is bounded: 10s to connect, 30s in total, `--timeout <seconds>` to change the\n67\t  total. An API that stops answering exits 1 with curl's status 28 in the message — talaria\n68\t  never hangs waiting for one.\n69\t\n70\tThe response block carries status, headers, timing and body; a JSON body is embedded as JSON, so\n71\tone parse gets you the fields. **An HTTP 4xx or 5xx exits 0** — it is a successful observation.\n72\tRead `response.status`; do not infer failure from the exit code.\n73\t\n74\t### The validation block\n75\t\n76\tEvery executed call also carries `validation`:\n77\t\n78\t```json\n79\t\"validation\": { \"status_documented\": true, \"content_type_documented\": true, \"body_valid\": true, \"errors\": [] }\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\tThe three booleans answer independently, so an undocumented 500 is distinguishable from a\n83\tdocumented 200 that came back with the wrong shape. Each schema failure is one entry in\n84\t`errors`, with the JSONPath of the offending field.\n85\t\n86\tA violation is an observation too: it still exits 0. `--fail-on-error` is how you ask for the\n87\tother behaviour — it exits **4** when the response is an HTTP error or violates the spec, and\n88\tprints the same envelope either way. Use it when you are testing; leave it off when you are\n89\texploring. A dry run has no validation block, because nothing came back.\n90\t\n91\tValidation reads the *redacted* response, so a field redacted by configuration is checked as\n92\t`<redacted>` and may report a violation the server did not commit.\n93\t\n94\t### Mutations are gated\n95\t\n96\t`GET`, `HEAD` and `OPTIONS` run. Every other method needs `--allow-mutations` and exits 2 without\n97\tit. The gate is checked before the request is built, and `--dry-run` does not exempt it.\n98\t\n99\tNeeding `--allow-mutations` is a decision, not a formality. Do not add it to a command that\n100\tfailed on it without saying what you are about to change and on which host. The sanctioned way\n101\tto exercise a `DELETE` is against a disposable environment — point `--base-url` at one. (The\n102\tbuilt-in digital twin that will make this cheap is designed but not yet shipped.)\n103\t\n104\t## Smoke testing\n105\t\n106\t`run` is `call` over many operations: it answers \"is this whole API behaving?\" in one command.\n107\t\n108\t```sh\n109\ttalaria run ./openapi.yaml --tag pets --operation getPet --base-url http://localhost:9000\n110\t```\n111\t\n112\t- `--tag` and `--operation` both repeat, and combining them is the **union**, not the\n113\t  intersection. No filter at all runs the whole spec. A filter that matches nothing exits 2\n114\t  rather than passing with nothing tested.\n115\t- Operations run one at a time, in spec order.\n116\t- Request data comes from the spec's own `example` first, then a file in `--fixtures dir/` named\n117\t  `<operationId>.json`, then generation from the schema. A fixture is `{\"params\": {…},\n118\t  \"headers\": {…}, \"body\": {…}}`; every field is optional.\n119\t- `--report json|pretty|tsv|junit` is `run`'s own format flag and overrides `--output`. `junit`\n120\t  writes a JUnit XML suite — one `<testcase>` per operation, `<failure>` or `<skipped>` carrying\n121\t  the reason — for a CI job to collect. It is a `--report` value only; `--output junit` exits 2.\n122\t- `--timeout` bounds each operation, not the suite. An endpoint that never answers becomes one\n123\t  failed line and the report still arrives.\n124\t\n125\tEach operation reports `passed`, `failed` or `skipped`, plus a `reason` for the last two:\n126\t\n127\t```json\n128\t{\"operation_id\":\"getPet\",\"method\":\"GET\",\"path\":\"/pets/{petId}\",\"outcome\":\"passed\",\"status\":200,\n129\t \"timing_ms\":12,\"validation\":{\"status_documented\":true,\"body_valid\":true,\"errors\":[]}}\n130\t```\n131\t\n132\t**A skip is not a failure.** A `DELETE` left alone without `--allow-mutations`, or an operation\n133\twhose required parameter nothing could supply, is correct behaviour — read the `reason` before\n134\treacting. The `summary` block carries `total`, `passed`, `failed` and `skipped`.\n135\t\n136\tExit codes follow `call`: failures exit 0 unless you pass `--fail-on-error`, which makes them\n137\texit 4. A missing credential exits **5** whichever flags you passed — that operation was never\n138\ttested, so ask a human to export the variable named in its `reason`. Every request `run` makes is\n139\trecorded in history with `\"source\": \"run\"`, and `history --source call` filters them back out.\n140\t\n141\t## Credentials\n142\t\n143\tYou cannot read a credential and you do not need to. talaria maps each security scheme the spec\n144\tdeclares onto an environment variable, and carries the name:\n145\t\n146\t| Scheme in the spec | Variable |\n147\t|---|---|\n148\t| `type: http`, `scheme: bearer` | `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` |\n149\t| `type: http`, `scheme: basic` | `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC` (`user:password`) |\n150\t| `type: apiKey`, named `petKey` | `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_PETKEY` (scheme name, upper-cased) |\n151\t\n152\t```sh\n153\ttalaria auth check ./openapi.yaml\n154\t{\"scheme\":\"bearerAuth\",\"source\":\"env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\",\"present\":true}\n155\t```\n156\t\n157\t`present` is a lookup; the value is never read. A profile can redirect a scheme to a different\n158\tvariable, and `auth check` reports whichever one is actually in force.\n159\t\n160\t**When a credential is missing** (exit 5, or `present: false`), the fix is one sentence to a\n161\thuman — name the variable and stop:\n162\t\n163\t> This API needs a bearer token. Export it before I retry:\n164\t> `export TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=…`. Do not paste the value to me; talaria reads it from the\n165\t> environment and I never see it.\n166\t\n167\tNever ask for the value, never suggest putting a literal token in a config file (talaria refuses\n168\tthem), and never try to read one out of the environment yourself. A credential you supply inline\n169\t— `--header 'X-Api-Key: sk-live-…'` — is redacted by name in the output and in the emitted curl,\n170\tso it is not copy-pasteable either.\n171\t\n172\tThe emitted curl references variables symbolically:\n173\t\n174\t```\n175\tcurl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\" 'https://api.example.com/v1/pets/42'\n176\t```\n177\t\n178\tThat command is runnable in a shell where the variable is set and useless to anyone else. Quote\n179\tit freely in reports.\n180\t\n181\tOne thing redaction cannot fix: an API key that belongs in the *query string* travels in the URL\n182\tand lands in server access logs. talaria warns once on stderr. Report the warning; it is a\n183\tproperty of the API, not a bug you can work around.\n184\t\n185\tResponse bodies are redacted only at the OAuth2 token fields and whatever JSON paths the user\n186\tconfigured. A login or token-issuing endpoint is for a human to run, not for you.\n187\t\n188\t## Output\n189\t\n190\t`--output json|pretty|tsv` on every command. Piped output defaults to `json`, so you normally get\n191\tJSON without asking. Every payload carries `\"schema\": \"talaria/v1\"` — branch on it if you cache\n192\tparsing logic.\n193\t\n194\tErrors go to **stderr** as one line of JSON in the same envelope:\n195\t\n196\t```json\n197\t{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"unknown operation \\\"getpet\\\"\",\"valid_alternatives\":[\"getPet\",\"getPets\"]}}\n198\t```\n199\t\n200\tWhen `valid_alternatives` is present it is the complete set of right answers. Pick from it rather\n201\tthan guessing again.\n202\t\n203\t## Exit codes\n204\t\n205\t| Code | Meaning | What to do |\n206\t|---|---|---|\n207\t| 0 | Success. A 4xx/5xx response is still success | read `response.status` |\n208\t| 1 | The request could not be completed: network, TLS, curl itself | check the host and `--base-url`; retrying once is reasonable |\n209\t| 2 | Usage error: unknown command or operation, missing parameter, bad flag, or a mutation without `--allow-mutations` | fix the invocation using `valid_alternatives` and the message |\n210\t| 3 | The spec could not be read or parsed | check the path or URL; do not retry unchanged |\n211\t| 4 | With `--fail-on-error`: the response was an HTTP error or violated the spec | read the `validation` block and `response.status` on stdout; report what failed |\n212\t| 5 | A required credential is not set | tell a human which variable to export; do not retry until they have |\n213\t\n214\t## History\n215\t\n216\tEvery real call is recorded, redacted at write time, newest first. Use it instead of re-issuing a\n217\trequest to remember what happened:\n218\t\n219\t```sh\n220\ttalaria history --operation getPet --status 4xx\n221\ttalaria history show 3\n222\t```\n223\t\n224\tThe index `history` prints is the entry's position in the whole store, so it stays valid under\n225\tfilters. `talaria history replay <n>` re-sends an entry and records the result as a new one;\n226\treplaying a mutation needs `--allow-mutations` too. Dry runs are never recorded. Recording is off\n227\twhere a profile says so, or everywhere under `TALARIA_HISTORY=off` — if history is empty, that is\n228\tusually why.\n229\t\n230\tCredentials are stored as names, so nothing in history can be read back into a value.\n231\t\n232\t## Rules\n233\t\n234\t1. Never ask a human for a credential value. Ask them to export a named variable.\n235\t2. Never add `--allow-mutations` silently.\n236\t3. Treat a 4xx as data, not as a crash. Exit 0 means talaria worked.\n237\t4. Prefer `describe` over reading the spec, and `history` over repeating a call.\n238\t5. Use `--dry-run` when you are unsure what a request will look like. It costs nothing.\n239\t"
      }
    ]
  },
