  "subagent_type": "general-purpose",
  "task_type": "local_agent",
  "prompt": "You are the CONCURRENCY reviewer for a code review of branch `ralph/design` in /home/casper/projects/talaria (Go CLI `talaria`). Base branch `main`. Review the FULL branch diff: `git diff main...HEAD`.\n\nContext: talaria is a Go CLI that shells out to `curl` via os/exec, stores request/response history in a file-backed \"corpus\" store, and runs smoke tests over many operations (`talaria run`). Read `docs/design/DESIGN.md` §5 (architecture) and §5a (how secrets reach curl: a config document written to the curl subprocess's stdin while the Go process owns the real stdin).\n\nFocus packages (read the real source):\n- internal/curl/exec.go — subprocess lifecycle, stdin/stdout/stderr pipes, context cancellation, timeouts, temp files\n- internal/corpus/store.go, lock_unix.go, lock_other.go, entry.go — file locking, read-modify-write, concurrent append/trim\n- cmd/talaria/run.go — does `run` execute operations concurrently? if so, shared state, ordering, error aggregation\n- cmd/talaria/call.go, history.go — signal handling, cleanup\n- internal/e2e/ — test-level races\n\nYour checklist:\n1. **Data races** — shared state written without synchronisation; maps/slices mutated from multiple goroutines; loop-variable capture; shared *bytes.Buffer or writers. RUN THE RACE DETECTOR: `go test -race ./...` and report anything it flags. Also try `go test -race -count=5 ./internal/corpus/... ./internal/curl/...` to shake out flakiness, and consider writing a small concurrent stress test against the corpus store (concurrent Append + Trim from several goroutines/processes) to prove or disprove the locking is sound.\n2. **Deadlock / pipe stalls** — writing to a subprocess's stdin while not concurrently draining its stdout/stderr will deadlock once the OS pipe buffer (64KB on Linux) fills. Check internal/curl/exec.go very carefully: if the config document is large (big request body) OR the response is large, can Go block writing stdin while curl blocks writing stdout? Prove it with a test using a large body if you can.\n3. **Goroutine and process leaks** — is every spawned goroutine guaranteed to exit? Is the curl child always reaped (cmd.Wait called on every path, including the timeout and error paths)? On timeout, is the child actually killed, and is its whole process group killed or just the parent? Are temp files and capture directories always removed, including on error and on signal?\n4. **Context cancellation** — is context.Context threaded through and honoured? Does a timeout actually abort in-flight work? Is exec.CommandContext used, and does the Cancel/WaitDelay behaviour match intent (Go 1.20+ has cmd.Cancel and cmd.WaitDelay — is WaitDelay set so a child ignoring SIGKILL-on-cancel cannot hang Wait forever)?\n5. **Swallowed errors** — errors from Close(), Wait(), Flush(), file sync, or lock release that are discarded, especially where discarding them means silent data loss in the corpus/history.\n6. **File-lock correctness** — is the lock advisory flock? Is it taken for the full read-modify-write? Is it released on every path? Does lock_other.go (non-unix fallback) provide any safety at all, or does it silently no-op in a way that corrupts data? Is the lock file opened/created with correct flags and permissions? Is the corpus written atomically (temp file + rename) or is a partial write possible if the process dies mid-write?\n7. **Signal handling** — Ctrl-C during a call: orphaned curl, leaked temp dir containing response data.\n\nIMPORTANT: a previous review already found and FIXED these — do NOT re-report unless the fix is incomplete or wrong (say precisely how): `corpus.trim` unlocked read-modify-write destroying entries; no timeout on the curl subprocess. It also raised as WARN and possibly did NOT fix: \"Killing talaria orphans the curl child and leaks the response capture directory\" — check whether that is still true and report it if so.\n\nDo not report pre-existing issues outside the branch diff. Prefer CONFIRMED findings — actually run the race detector and write reproducing tests.\n\nReport back a list of findings, each with exactly:\n- Title (one line)\n- Severity: CRIT (data race, data loss, deadlock, leak that breaks functionality) | WARN (robustness, unlikely-but-real) | INFO\n- File:line (mandatory and precise)\n- Description: what is wrong and why it matters\n- Suggested fix: concrete\n- Whether you CONFIRMED it (race detector output, failing test) or it is reasoned-only — include the evidence\n\nBe honest about severity. Report each distinct issue once.",
  "uuid": "f319cf64-ad70-4699-876e-5bb711b92462",
  "session_id": "88f23283-cb35-4a05-8ac7-d68cad62b8c2"
}
