        "type": "text",
        "text": "Review complete. I removed my scratch files; the repo tree is as I found it (note: `internal/corpus/xproc_review_test.go` appeared untracked during my session and is not mine — it was not in the starting `git status`).\n\n## Findings\n\n---\n\n### 1. curl reads `~/.curlrc` on every call — one writable file exfiltrates every resolved credential\n- **Severity: CRIT**\n- **File:line:** `internal/curl/config.go:103` (`argv = []string{\"curl\", \"-K\", \"-\"}`), consumed at `internal/curl/exec.go:83`\n- **Description:** talaria never passes `-q` / `--disable`, so curl parses its default config file (`$CURL_HOME/.curlrc`, else `$HOME/.curlrc`) *before* the `-K -` document. Every directive there applies to the request that carries the real credential. `trace-ascii = /path` writes the full resolved `Authorization: Bearer …` to an attacker-chosen file; `proxy = http://…` ships it to an attacker-controlled host; `verbose`, `dump-header`, `output` are all equally available. This defeats the entire firewall, and it defeats it *specifically in the deployment §5a calls \"fully realised\"* — an agent that has no credential env vars but does have write access to `$HOME` can plant `.curlrc`, wait for one `talaria call`, and read the token out of a file it chose. §5a's \"threats not covered\" only concedes an agent that can *read* env vars or the profile file; writing one file is a strictly weaker capability.\n- **Suggested fix:** Make argv `[]string{\"curl\", \"-q\", \"-K\", \"-\"}` (curl requires `-q` to be the first parameter). Belt-and-braces: set `cmd.Env` explicitly (see finding 8) so `CURL_HOME` cannot redirect the lookup. Add a canary case that plants a `.curlrc` with `trace-ascii` in the harness `HOME` and scans the resulting file — `canary_test.go`'s harness already sets `HOME` to a temp dir, so it is a few lines.\n- **CONFIRMED by execution.** With `HOME=/tmp/curlhome` containing `.curlrc` → `trace-ascii = /tmp/curlhome/leak.txt`, `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=CANARY-SECRET-abc123 talaria call … listPets` produced `/tmp/curlhome/leak.txt` (mode 0644) containing `CANARY-SECRET-abc123`, while stdout showed the correct `<redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER>`. Re-running the same config document through `curl -q -K -` produced no trace file.\n\n---\n\n### 2. A malformed `--header` / `--query` / `--param` / `--body` flag is echoed verbatim into the structured stderr error — and AGENT.md documents the exact syntax that triggers it\n- **Severity: CRIT**\n- **File:line:** `internal/request/build.go:311` (`b.fail(\"%s %q is not name=value\", flag, raw)`); same class at `internal/request/build.go:53` (repeated `--body`, joins every body value into the message) and `internal/request/build.go:186`\n- **Description:** `--header 'Authorization: Bearer sk-live-…'` — curl's syntax, and the syntax AGENT.md itself prints at `AGENT.md:54` (`--header 'X-Trace: abc'`) and `AGENT.md:169` (`--header 'X-Api-Key: sk-live-…'`) — is not `name=value`, so the binder rejects it and puts the *whole argument, credential included* into the exit-2 JSON on stderr. The value has by then been expanded by the shell, so `--header \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\"` prints the real token. This is the highest-probability leak in the tool: the operating manual tells the agent to use the colon form, promises \"is redacted by name in the output\", and the actual behaviour is the opposite. The `--body` variant is worse in one respect — it echoes entire request bodies, which routinely carry `client_secret` / `password`.\n- **Suggested fix:** Never echo the value half of a rejected `name=value` flag. Report only the part before the first `=`/`:`, or a positional index: `--header 1 is not name=value (got \"Authorization: …\" — did you mean --header 'Authorization=Bearer $TOK'?)` with the value elided. Same for `--body`: report the count and the *kind* of each value (literal / `@file` / `-`), never the bytes. Also fix AGENT.md's two examples to `name=value`, or accept the colon form in the parser. Add a canary case: a malformed flag carrying the canary, scanned across every format.\n- **CONFIRMED by execution.** `talaria call spec.yaml listPets --header 'Authorization: Bearer sk-live-REALSECRET123' --dry-run --output json` → `{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"cannot build a request for listPets: --header \\\"Authorization: Bearer sk-live-REALSECRET123\\\" is not name=value\"}}`. Likewise `--body '{\"password\":\"hunter2\"}' --body '@/etc/hostname'` echoes both bodies.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Credentials in a URL's userinfo reach stdout, the emitted curl, and the permanent history file in cleartext\n- **Severity: CRIT**\n- **File:line:** `internal/request/build.go:144-150` (`baseURL` validates scheme and host but keeps `parsed.User`), surfacing at `internal/curl/render.go:146` (`w.literal(req.BaseURL + req.Path)`) and `internal/corpus/entry.go:154` (`entry.URL, _ = req.URL(request.Redacted)`)\n- **Description:** `http://user:pass@host` is an accepted absolute http(s) URL, so `--base-url`, a profile's `base-url`, **and a spec's `servers[0].url` — untrusted input** — can carry a credential in the authority. Nothing strips or redacts it: the password is printed in `request.url`, in the copy-pasteable `request.curl`, and is appended to `history.jsonl`, which §5a calls \"a permanent artifact — the highest-risk surface in the tool\". There is no way for the user to get it back out short of deleting the store.\n- **Suggested fix:** In `baseURL`, if `parsed.User != nil`, either reject with a message pointing at `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC` (preferred — talaria already has a basic-auth path that keeps the value symbolic), or strip the userinfo and re-inject it as `request.Secret`/`Sensitive` so every display form renders `<redacted>`. Apply the same check in `replayRequest` (`cmd/talaria/history.go:511`), which re-parses a stored URL.\n- **CONFIRMED by execution.** `talaria call spec.yaml listPets --base-url 'http://admin:s3cr3t@127.0.0.1:8898' --output json` printed `\"curl\":\"curl -s 'http://admin:s3cr3t@127.0.0.1:8898/pets'\"` and `grep -o 's3cr3t' $XDG_STATE_HOME/talaria/history.jsonl` matched.\n\n---\n\n### 4. No `globoff`: a spec-supplied base URL turns one authenticated call into N\n- **Severity: WARN**\n- **File:line:** `internal/curl/config.go:132` (`d.directive(\"url\", url)`) — the document sets `proto`, `proto-redir` and the timeouts but never `globoff`\n- **Description:** curl interprets `{a,b,c}` and `[1-100]` in a URL as glob ranges unless `-g`/`globoff` is given. `IsHTTPScheme` + `url.Parse` block globs in the *host* (Go rejects `{` and bracket ranges there — I checked), but not in the *path*. A hostile or merely awkward `servers[0].url` of `http://host/{a,b,c}` makes curl issue three requests, each carrying the resolved credential, to three paths the user never asked for; talaria then gets three `%{json}` documents on stdout and dies with a misleading `cannot parse curl's response metadata` (exit 1), so the operator cannot even tell what happened. It also breaks legitimate URLs containing `[`/`]`.\n- **Suggested fix:** Emit `globoff` as a flag directive in `document.build`, and `-g` in `curl.Render`, so the URL is always literal. Add a `build.go` guard rejecting a base URL whose path contains `{`, `}`, `[` or `]` if you want the failure to be a clean exit 2 instead.\n- **CONFIRMED by execution.** With `servers[0].url = http://127.0.0.1:8898/{a,b,c}`, talaria exited 1 with `cannot parse curl's response metadata: invalid character '{' after top-level value`; feeding the equivalent document to `curl -K -` directly showed three completed requests (`/a/pets`, `/b/pets`, `/c/pets`, all 200).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Request bodies are written to history with only the three RFC 6749 *response* token fields redacted\n- **Severity: WARN**\n- **File:line:** `internal/corpus/entry.go:232` (`return newBody(b.ContentType, r.Response.Body(b.Data))`), list at `internal/secret/response.go:17`\n- **Description:** `builtinBodyPaths` is `access_token`, `refresh_token`, `id_token` — the fields of a token *response*. A token *request* body carries `client_secret`, `password`, `assertion`, `client_assertion`, `code_verifier`, and none of those are redacted, so they land in `history.jsonl` verbatim and forever. §5a's \"threats explicitly not covered\" concedes only *response* bodies; a request body is data talaria itself assembled and chose to record, so this is inside the boundary the design claims.\n- **Suggested fix:** Give the request side its own built-in path list (`client_secret`, `client_assertion`, `password`, `passwd`, `secret`, `assertion`, `code_verifier`) applied by `Redactors.requestBody`, or at minimum a `redact.request-body-paths` config key alongside `redact.body-paths`. Note the cost is only to the recording — `request.Body.Data` still goes on the wire unchanged.\n- **CONFIRMED by execution.** `--body '{\"client_id\":\"abc\",\"client_secret\":\"SUPER-SECRET-XYZ\",\"password\":\"hunter2\"}'` → both values present in `history.jsonl` (and on stdout in `request.body`, which is the documented display of what was sent).\n\n---\n\n### 6. The built-in sensitive-name list misses common credential spellings, including `Authentication`\n- **Severity: WARN**\n- **File:line:** `internal/secret/redact.go:19-27`\n- **Description:** The list is exactly §5a's table (`authorization`, `proxy-authorization`, `cookie`, `set-cookie`, `*api*key*`, `*token*`, `*secret*`), matched case-insensitively against the lower-cased name — that part is correct. But `Authentication` (an exact-match miss against `authorization`), `*password*`, `*credential*`, `*signature*`, `*session*` and bare `x-auth` all fall through, on requests *and* responses. Since `hide()` and the corpus both key off this list, such a header is printed on stdout, emitted in the copy-pasteable curl, and appended to the permanent store.\n- **Suggested fix:** Extend `builtinPatterns` with `authentication`, `*auth`, `*password*`, `*passwd*`, `*credential*`, `*signature*`, `*session*`. The list is add-only by design so widening it cannot weaken anything; the only cost is over-redacting a benign `X-Session-Id`, which is the right way to be wrong here. Add the new spellings to `TestAUserSuppliedHeaderIsRedactedLikeASpecCredential`'s case table.\n- **CONFIRMED by execution.** `--header 'X-Password=hunter2' --header 'Authentication=Bearer TOK123' --header 'X-Signature=SIGVAL'` → all three appear unredacted in `request.headers`, in `request.curl`, and in `history.jsonl`.\n\n---\n\n### 7. `scrubber` misses sensitive *literals*, so curl's stderr is only scrubbed for structural secrets\n- **Severity: WARN**\n- **File:line:** `internal/curl/exec.go:265` (`if !p.Value.IsSecret() { continue }`)\n- **Description:** The replacer that rewrites credential values out of curl's stderr is built only from pairs where `IsSecret()` is true — i.e. values backed by a `SecretRef`. A value the *name matcher* caught (`--query api_key=…`, `--header X-Api-Key=…`, a profile header) is `IsSensitive()` but not `IsSecret()`, has no ref, and is therefore invisible to the scrubber. If curl ever echoes the request URL or a header into its error text, that literal goes to stderr unscrubbed. The asymmetry is exactly the kind §5a warns about (\"error paths are where redaction bugs live\"), even though I could not find a curl message that echoes the URL on the common failure paths.\n- **Suggested fix:** In `scrubber`, also add `(p.Value.Reveal(), secret.Placeholder)` pairs for values where `IsSensitive() && !IsSecret()`, plus their `url.QueryEscape` form, mirroring the existing ref branch.\n- **Reasoned-only** (code-path confirmed; no curl message found that demonstrates the echo).\n\n---\n\n### 8. `curl` inherits talaria's whole environment, contradicting the comment that says it does not\n- **Severity: INFO**\n- **File:line:** `internal/curl/exec.go:40-41` (comment) vs `internal/curl/exec.go:83` (`exec.CommandContext` with `cmd.Env` left nil)\n- **Description:** The doc comment asserts the credential values are \"not in the environment curl inherits (§5a)\". `cmd.Env` is never set, so curl inherits `os.Environ()` in full — every `TALARIA_AUTH_*` var, plus `http_proxy`, `CURL_CA_BUNDLE`, `CURL_HOME`, `SSLKEYLOGFILE`. This is not itself a privilege escalation (a same-uid process can read the parent's `/proc/self/environ` anyway), but the comment states a property the code does not implement, and closing it is the natural companion to finding 1's `-q`: a curl launched with a curated `cmd.Env` cannot have its config path, CA bundle or proxy redirected by the ambient environment either.\n- **Suggested fix:** Either set `cmd.Env` to a minimal allowlist (`PATH`, `HOME` only if needed, deliberately-chosen proxy vars) and keep the comment, or correct the comment to say the guarantee is about argv only.\n- **CONFIRMED by code reading** (`cmd.Env` is provably never assigned in `ExecuteWith`).\n\n---\n\n## Prior fixes: all verified holding\n\nRe-tested each of the eleven, all still correct: query-string credential redaction (`api_key=<redacted>` in URL and curl); http/https base-URL restriction (`file:///etc/passwd` → exit 2; `proto`/`proto-redir` both `=http,https`; no `location` directive, so redirects are not followed at all); `head` flag instead of `-X HEAD`; `connect-timeout`/`max-time` directives plus the `MaxTime+killGrace` context and `WaitDelay`; the flock-on-sibling covering append *and* trim; `run`'s generated `Content-Type` travelling with the bytes; non-UTF-8 bodies base64'd with an explicit `encoding` and truncated bodies refused on replay; `--data-raw` in both the config document and the emitted curl; `talaria bogus` → exit 2 with alternatives; `version --output json` inside the envelope; libopenapi's slog handler pointed at `io.Discard`.\n\nI also specifically re-derived curl's `-K` quoting rules against `unslashquote` and confirmed `internal/curl/config.go:379-387` is complete and correctly single-pass — a header value of `a\"\\noutput = /tmp/pwned\\n\"b` renders as one escaped directive and cannot inject. A 200 KB inline `data-raw` line round-trips intact through curl 8.14 (no line-length splitting). Other things checked clean: no operationId ever reaches a filesystem path (`LoadFixtures` keys off the directory listing; the spec cache uses a SHA-256 of the URL); `AllowFileReferences`/`AllowRemoteReferences` left at their false defaults with a comment saying why; file modes 0700/0600 on the state dir, history, lock, spec cache, response-capture dir and body temp file, all verified on disk; JUnit escaped by `encoding/xml` rather than string-assembled; `Resolve()`/`Reveal()` called from exactly two lines, both in `internal/curl`; and no committed credentials anywhere in the diff."
      }
