    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "## Concurrency review — `ralph/design`\n\nRace detector is clean: `go test -race ./...` passes, and `go test -race -count=5 ./internal/corpus/... ./internal/curl/...` passes (corpus 123s, curl 4.7s, exit 0). I found no data races.\n\nI also wrote and ran a **cross-process** corpus stress test (8 real OS processes × 40 appends, seeded to `maxPerSource-1` so every append takes trim's rewrite path, ×2 runs): **zero lost entries**. The previous review's `trim` lock fix is complete and correct on unix. Test file removed afterwards; working tree is unchanged.\n\nI also **disproved** the stdin/stdout pipe-deadlock hypothesis (checklist item 2) — see \"Checked and clean\" below.\n\n---\n\n### Finding 1 — Killing talaria orphans curl, which then completes the request and writes the unredacted response into a temp dir nothing ever deletes\n\n- **Severity:** CRIT\n- **File:line:** `internal/curl/exec.go:69` (`defer cleanupCapture()`), with `internal/curl/exec.go:158` (`os.MkdirTemp`) and `internal/curl/config.go:291` (`os.CreateTemp` for the body). Root cause: nothing anywhere installs a signal handler — `grep -rn \"os/signal\\|signal.Notify\"` over the whole tree returns nothing, and `cmd/talaria/main.go:8` is `os.Exit(run(...))`.\n- **Description:** All cleanup is `defer`-based. A signal that is not handled terminates the Go process immediately, so no defer runs. Three things follow, and I confirmed all three against the real binary:\n  1. **curl is orphaned and keeps going.** `ps`/`/proc` shows the child reparented to `ppid=1` and still running long after talaria is gone.\n  2. **The request completes after talaria is dead.** The orphan finished the HTTP exchange 8s post-mortem. For a `--allow-mutations` POST this means Ctrl-C/SIGTERM does *not* cancel the write — the server still applies it, and because `recordCall` never ran, the call is absent from history entirely. That directly breaks history's stated contract (\"what have I already tried\").\n  3. **The capture directory leaks with real response data in it.** `talaria-call-*/{body,headers}` survives indefinitely, containing the *raw, unredacted* response — including exactly the material §5a's leak table says must be redacted.\n\n  Confirmed output (my probe sends the signal to talaria only; the same leak occurs on terminal Ctrl-C, where SIGINT reaches curl via the process group but the directory still survives):\n\n  ```\n  talaria pid: 879355\n  BEFORE curl procs: [pid=879365 ppid=879355 cmd=/usr/bin/curl -K -]\n  sending terminated to talaria only\n  talaria exited: signal: terminated\n  AFTER      curl procs: [pid=879365 ppid=1 cmd=/usr/bin/curl -K -]   <-- orphaned\n  AFTER      tmp: talaria-call-4161126053/body (0 bytes)\n  AFTER+8s   curl procs: []\n  AFTER+8s   tmp: talaria-call-4161126053/body (46 bytes)             <-- written post-mortem\n  ```\n  ```\n  $ cat /tmp/.../talaria-call-4161126053/headers\n  HTTP/1.1 200 OK\n  Set-Cookie: session=abc123\n  $ cat /tmp/.../talaria-call-4161126053/body\n  {\"access_token\":\"SUPER-SECRET-RESPONSE-VALUE\"}\n  ```\n  The same applies to `talaria-body-*` (the >1MB / non-UTF-8 request-body temp file at `config.go:291`), which may carry credentials the user put in the body.\n\n  This is the WARN the previous review raised and did not fix. It is **still true**, and the evidence shows it is worse than \"leaks a directory\": it is a post-mortem uncancelled request plus a persistent plaintext credential file.\n- **Suggested fix:** Install a handler in `cmd/talaria/root.go` (`signal.NotifyContext(context.Background(), os.Interrupt, syscall.SIGTERM)`), thread that `ctx` into `curl.ExecuteWith` in place of `context.Background()` at `exec.go:77` (`context.WithTimeout(parent, ...)`), and drive shutdown through it. Cancellation then kills curl via `CommandContext`'s `Cancel`, `cmd.Run` returns, and the deferred `cleanupCapture`/`cleanupConfig` run normally. Belt-and-braces: set `cmd.SysProcAttr = &syscall.SysProcAttr{Setpgid: true}` and a `cmd.Cancel` that signals `-pgid`, so anything curl spawned dies too. For an unhandleable death (SIGKILL) also `os.Remove` the capture files immediately after `newCapture` returns them, keeping only the open descriptors — curl needs paths, so an alternative is to have `run`/`call` sweep stale `talaria-call-*`/`talaria-body-*` under `TMPDIR` at start-up.\n- **CONFIRMED** (probe output above, real `talaria` binary, real curl, real HTTP server).\n\n---\n\n### Finding 2 — The curl-timeout fix does not cover the preflight subprocess: `curl --version` can hang talaria forever\n\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:line:** `internal/curl/version.go:42` — `exec.Command(path, \"--version\").Output()`\n- **Description:** Commit `828ade8` (\"bound the curl subprocess with connect and total timeouts\") bounded `ExecuteWith` but not the version preflight, which runs *before* it on every call path. `preflight` uses a plain `exec.Command` with no context, no deadline, and no `WaitDelay`. If the curl binary is wedged (hung NFS/fuse mount holding the binary, a shim/wrapper on `PATH`, a `curl` that blocks on a slow ldd/plugin load), talaria hangs indefinitely producing no output, and `--timeout` has no effect. This contradicts the stated invariant in `config.go:24-26` — \"a wedged one becomes an exit code rather than a process an agent cannot interpret\". `talaria run` is the worst case: the whole suite hangs before operation 1.\n\n  Confirmed with a shim `curl` on `PATH` that sleeps on `--version`:\n  ```\n  $ PATH=/tmp/talrev/fakebin:$PATH timeout 20 talaria call spec.yaml getMed --timeout 3\n  Terminated\n  exit=143 elapsed=20s   # --timeout was 3s; killed only by the external timeout(1)\n  ```\n  Without the external `timeout(1)` this hangs for the full hour the shim sleeps. The hung `curl --version` is also orphaned by Finding 1's mechanism.\n- **Suggested fix:** Use `exec.CommandContext` with a short deadline (a couple of seconds is generous for `--version`) plus `cmd.WaitDelay`, and classify the deadline as `clierr.RequestFailed(\"curl did not answer --version within …\")`. Secondary (INFO, same site): `preflightOnce`/`preflightErr` are package globals keyed on nothing, so the result is memoised across *different* resolved paths — which quietly undercuts the comment at `exec.go:80-82` claiming \"a PATH change mid-run cannot swap the binary between the preflight and the call\". Key the cache on `path`, or drop the comment.\n- **CONFIRMED** (reproduction above).\n\n---\n\n### Finding 3 — `replace` is not crash-safe: no fsync before the rename, so a crash mid-trim can destroy the entire history file\n\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:line:** `internal/corpus/store.go:265-271` (`tmp.Close()` then `os.Rename`); `.Sync()` appears nowhere in the repo.\n- **Description:** The doc comment at `store.go:242-243` claims the swap is atomic and that \"a crash mid-trim leaves the old file intact\". `rename(2)` is atomic with respect to *concurrent readers*, which is true and is what the lock+rename design needs. It is **not** crash-safe: the rename metadata operation can reach disk before the temp file's data does. After a power loss or kernel panic, `history.jsonl` can be the new inode with zero or partial contents — losing up to `maxPerSource` (1000) entries per source, not just the in-flight one. For a file the design calls \"a permanent artifact… the highest-risk surface in the tool\", and given `trim` runs on essentially every append once a source is at cap, the exposure window is every write. The plain-append path at `store.go:184` has the same gap but loses only the last line, which `Read` already tolerates.\n- **Suggested fix:** In `replace`, `tmp.Sync()` before `tmp.Close()`, and after `os.Rename` open the parent directory and `Sync()` it so the directory entry is durable. Then the comment is true as written. (Alternatively, soften the comment to say it is atomic for readers but not durable across a crash — but for this file durability is the cheaper choice.)\n- **Reasoned-only** — a genuine reproduction needs a power-cut/`dm-flakey` harness, which I did not build. The absence of any `Sync()` call is confirmed by grep; the overclaiming comment is confirmed by reading.\n\n---\n\n### Finding 4 — CI never runs the race detector, so the concurrency tests that guard the corpus fix cannot catch a regression\n\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:line:** `.github/workflows/ci.yml:38-39` — `- name: Test` / `run: go test ./...`\n- **Description:** The branch added `TestConcurrentAppendsKeepEveryEntryTheyAcknowledged` (`internal/corpus/store_test.go:288`) specifically to lock in the `trim` fix, and several packages run `t.Parallel()` subtests. Without `-race` none of that detects an unsynchronised write; a future change that drops the flock or shares a buffer across the parallel canary/e2e tests would go green. This is cheap to fix and directly protects the fix the previous review just landed. (Note the race build is not free: `-race` took ~124s on `internal/corpus` alone here, driven by the `maxPerSource-1` seeding loop.)\n- **Suggested fix:** Change the Test step to `go test -race ./...`, or add a second step running `-race` on the concurrency-bearing packages (`./internal/corpus/... ./internal/curl/... ./internal/e2e/...`) if full-suite race timing is a concern. `internal/ci/workflow_test.go` asserts this file matches `.ralph/stack.json`, so update both together.\n- **CONFIRMED** (workflow file read; the difference in behaviour between `go test` and `go test -race` is definitional).\n\n---\n\n### Finding 5 — `lock_other.go` silently disables all locking on non-unix, reintroducing the exact trim data-loss bug\n\n- **Severity:** INFO\n- **File:line:** `internal/corpus/lock_other.go:11` — `func lock(string) (func(), error) { return func() {}, nil }`\n- **Description:** On `!unix` builds `Append`'s read-modify-write runs completely unserialised, so two concurrent talaria processes reproduce the destroyed-entries bug that commit `e0cceae` fixed — with no warning, and with `Append` still returning `nil` for entries that were dropped. The comment is honest about this. It stays INFO rather than WARN because CI builds only `ubuntu-latest`, there is no `GOOS=windows` target anywhere in the repo, and `go build ./...` is host-only — so today nothing ships that binary. It becomes a real bug the moment a Windows release is cut.\n- **Suggested fix:** Either add `//go:build windows` using `LockFileEx` via `golang.org/x/sys/windows`, or make the non-unix `lock` return an error so `Append` fails loudly rather than losing data silently. At minimum, record the constraint where releases are configured, so a future `GOOS=windows` build is a deliberate decision.\n- **Reasoned-only** (build-tag reading; I did not cross-compile).\n\n---\n\n### Checked and clean — no finding\n\n- **Pipe deadlock on a large config document (checklist item 2): does not exist, disproved empirically.** `cmd.Stdin`/`Stdout`/`Stderr` are a `*bytes.Reader` and `*bytes.Buffer`, not `*os.File`, so `os/exec` allocates pipes and copies each on its own goroutine — writer and readers run concurrently by construction. Independently, the response never crosses stdout at all (it goes to the capture files via `output`/`dump-header`), so stdout only ever carries the small `%{json}` payload and cannot fill a 64KB buffer. Verified against a server that stalls 3s before reading the request body: a 900KB **inline** body (`data-raw` in the config document, under `maxInlineBody`) returned `status 200 {'len': 900012}` in 3.21s, and a 2MB body on the **temp-file** path returned `{'len': 2000012}` in 3.48s. No hang, no leftover temp files.\n- **No use-after-free / race on the credential buffer.** `cleanupWith` zeroes `config` (`internal/curl/config.go:345-352`) while `cmd.Stdin = bytes.NewReader(config)` is being drained by an exec goroutine. This is safe: `Cmd.awaitGoroutines` always receives from `c.goroutineErr` before `Wait` returns (`$GOROOT/src/os/exec/exec.go:1001,1004`), including on the `WaitDelay` path where it force-closes the pipes first. `cmd.Run` therefore happens-after the copy goroutine, and the deferred zeroing at `exec.go:72` runs later still.\n- **Child is always reaped.** `cmd.Run` calls `Wait` on every path; `cmd.WaitDelay = killGrace` (`exec.go:87`) bounds the reap so a grandchild holding the output pipe cannot block `Wait` forever; `CommandContext`'s default `Cancel` kills on deadline. This part of the timeout fix is correct.\n- **`run` is sequential** (`cmd/talaria/run.go:121-124`) — no shared mutable state across goroutines, no loop-variable capture, no ordering hazard. `runner.missing` is mutated only from that loop.\n- **`preflightOnce` and `QueryKeyWarner.once`** are `sync.Once`; the post-`Do` reads of `preflightErr` are properly ordered by `Once`'s happens-before guarantee. Not races.\n- **Spec cache writes are atomic** (`internal/spec/source.go:154-179`, temp + rename), and the fetch is bounded by `fetchTimeout`.\n- **Lock hygiene:** advisory `flock(LOCK_EX)` on a sibling `.lock` file that trim never renames — the right choice, and the reason the cross-process test passes. Held across the whole write+trim critical section (`store.go:109-119`), released via `defer` on every path, and released by the kernel even on SIGKILL. `Read` deliberately takes no lock, which is safe: `rename` is atomic for readers, and a torn final line from a concurrent `O_APPEND` is skipped by `Read`'s per-line tolerance without affecting earlier entries.\n- **Swallowed errors:** every `//nolint:errcheck` site is a genuine best-effort cleanup where a more important error is already being returned. `Close` on the write paths (`store.go:184`, `store.go:265`, `config.go:310`) is checked, so no silent corpus data loss from a discarded `Close`."
      }
