  "output_file": "/tmp/claude-1000/-home-casper-projects-talaria/88f23283-cb35-4a05-8ac7-d68cad62b8c2/tasks/ae306b8e436f43444.output",
  "summary": "Server cleanup done. Here are my findings.\n\n---\n\n## SPEC-COMPLIANCE FINDINGS — `ralph/design` vs `docs/design/DESIGN.md`\n\n**Completeness table (§4, Phases 1–4): every flag exists and behaves as documented.** `list --tag/--output(json|pretty|tsv)`; `describe [--output json|pretty]` (+`--depth`); `search --kind operation|schema|param`; `uses`; `call --param/--query/--header/--body('@file'|'-'|literal)/--base-url/--profile/--dry-run/--allow-mutations/--timeout/--output` (+`--fail-on-error`); `history --operation/--since/--status/--output` (+`--source`) with `show`/`replay`; `run --tag/--operation/--base-url/--profile/--allow-mutations/--report json|junit|pretty(+tsv)/--timeout/--fail-on-error` (+`--fixtures`); `auth check [--profile]`; `version`. No §4 flag is missing. Exit codes 0/1/2/3/4/5 all verified in and out of their circumstances (4xx→0 for `call`; 4 only under `--fail-on-error`/`run`; 5 distinct from 2). Output contract matches the §4 sketch field-for-field, `\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\"` is on every command incl. `version`, and pretty-on-TTY / JSON-when-piped is confirmed via a pty. §5 test-data priority (spec `example` → `--fixtures` → gen) is correctly ordered and per-field. curl ≥7.70 preflight and `--write-out '%{json}'` present. `strict-3.0.yaml` does carry all four constructs. 2.0→3.x conversion verified end-to-end.\n\n---\n\n### 1. `redact.headers` from the config file never reaches the displayed request or the emitted curl\n- **Severity: CRIT**\n- **File:line:** `internal/request/build.go:80-82` and `:289-302` (`hide()` uses only the built-in list); `cmd/talaria/call.go:239-240` (the config-derived redactors are wired to corpus + response only)\n- **Description:** DESIGN §5a's leak table (DESIGN.md:339) requires `request.headers` in JSON output to be redacted against the \"Built-in list … **+ user-extensible**\", and says it \"Applies to pretty output too\". README.md:209-210 states `headers` globs are \"applied to request and response headers alike\". In reality `request.Build` marks header/query/cookie values sensitive using a hard-coded `var builtin *secret.Redactor` (nil = built-ins only); `cfg.Redact.Headers` is only ever passed to `corpus.Redactors`. Confirmed with `redact: {headers: [\"x-session-*\"]}`: the *history entry* stores `\"X-Session-Id\": \"<redacted>\"` while the *stdout* `request.headers` and `request.curl` both print `X-Session-Id: zzz` in the clear. That is exactly inverted — the permanent artifact is protected and the surface the agent reads is not. Response headers *are* redacted correctly (`x-echo-*` → `<redacted>`), so the gap is specifically the request side. No test covers `redact.headers` at any level; every existing test and the canary suite only exercise `redact.body-paths`.\n- **Suggested fix:** thread the extra patterns into `request.Inputs` and have `hide()` take a `*secret.Redactor` built from `secret.NewRedactor(cfg.Redact.Headers...)`; add a canary case that configures `redact.headers` and greps stdout/dry-run/pretty for the value.\n- **CONFIRMED by running:** yes.\n\n### 2. `talaria auth` and `talaria auth <typo>` exit 0 with help on stdout\n- **Severity: CRIT**\n- **File:line:** `cmd/talaria/auth.go:30-39` (`newAuthCmd` sets no `Args`/`RunE`)\n- **Description:** `talaria auth` → exit 0, and `talaria auth bogus` → exit 0, both dumping cobra help to **stdout**. DESIGN §4 assigns 2 to usage errors \"unknown operation … stderr JSON lists valid options\", and AGENT.md:209 tells the agent code 2 covers \"unknown command\". The previous fix (commit f747586, plan Task 8) installed `unknownCommand` on the root only, so `talaria bogus` correctly exits 2 while the `auth` group does not — an agent that types `talaria auth chekc` gets exit 0 and unparseable text where `--output json` was promised (§3.1). This is the same defect the earlier review recorded; the fix is incomplete, not applied to subcommand groups.\n- **Suggested fix:** give `newAuthCmd` `Args: unknownCommand` plus `RunE: cmd.Help` (mirroring root), or better, a shared `groupCommand()` helper so any future group inherits it; assert `talaria auth bogus` → 2 in `exitcode_test.go`.\n- **CONFIRMED by running:** yes.\n\n### 3. AGENT.md and README document `--header 'Name: value'`; the binary only accepts `name=value`\n- **Severity: CRIT**\n- **File:line:** `AGENT.md:54`, `AGENT.md:169`, `README.md:274` vs `internal/request/build.go:306-313`\n- **Description:** DESIGN §4 specifies `--header X-Foo=bar` and the code implements that. But AGENT.md's headline `call` example is `talaria call getPet --param petId=42 --query verbose=true --header 'X-Trace: abc'`, which exits **2** with `--header \"X-Trace: abc\" is not name=value`. AGENT.md:169 and README.md:274 repeat the colon form. §3.7 makes AGENT.md a first-class deliverable, and this is the one line an LLM copies verbatim. Worse, the colon form is not always rejected: `--header 'X-Trace: abc=1'` is *accepted*, cut at the first `=`, and produces the malformed wire header `X-Trace: abc: 1` with a JSON `headers` key of `\"X-Trace: abc\"` — silent corruption rather than an error. `agentdoc_test.go` checks command names, exit codes and env vars but never flag argument syntax, so this drifts unpoliced.\n- **Suggested fix:** correct the three doc lines to `--header 'X-Trace=abc'`; optionally also accept the curl-style `Name: value` in `binder.pairs` for headers (splitting on `:` when no `=` precedes it) since it is what every agent will reach for. Either way, reject a name containing `:` or whitespace, and extend `agentdoc_test.go` to execute the doc's fenced `talaria …` invocations against a fixture spec with `--dry-run`.\n- **CONFIRMED by running:** yes.\n\n### 4. `history replay` issues query-string credentials without the §5a warning\n- **Severity: WARN**\n- **File:line:** `cmd/talaria/history.go:209` (calls `curl.Execute(req)` with no `warnQueryCredentials`); compare `cmd/talaria/call.go:156` and `cmd/talaria/run.go:360`\n- **Description:** DESIGN §5a's leak table (DESIGN.md:346) requires \"Query-string API keys (`?api_key=`) … These still leak into *server* logs — warn once on stderr\", and AGENT.md:181-183 tells the agent \"talaria warns once on stderr. Report the warning.\" Replaying a recorded call whose credential rides in the query string resolves and sends the key (confirmed: emitted curl is `\"…/keyed?api_key=$TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_PETKEY\"`) with **empty stderr and exit 0**. `call` and `run` both warn; replay is the third request-issuing path and was missed. This was on the earlier review's list and no commit addressed it.\n- **Suggested fix:** call `warnQueryCredentials(cmd.ErrOrStderr(), secret.NewQueryKeyWarner(), req)` immediately after `replayRequest` at history.go:205; add a test asserting stderr carries the warning on replay.\n- **CONFIRMED by running:** yes.\n\n### 5. The remote-spec cache never expires and has no refresh escape hatch\n- **Severity: WARN**\n- **File:line:** `internal/spec/source.go:86-90`\n- **Description:** `loadURL` returns the cached bytes unconditionally whenever the file is readable — no TTL, no `If-None-Match`, no `--refresh`/`--no-cache` flag, no documented location an agent can point a human at. The code comment at source.go:98 states the situation plainly (\"the cache has no expiry an agent could reach\") without recording a design decision for it. This undercuts §3.3 (\"The spec *is* the collection … nothing to curate, nothing to drift\") — a spec fetched once drifts silently forever, and the failure mode is `talaria call` producing 404s against an endpoint the current spec renamed. AGENT.md never mentions the cache, so an agent has no lever at all. README.md:48-50 is honest about the behaviour but offers no remedy. Also on the earlier review's list, unaddressed.\n- **Suggested fix:** stat the cache file and re-fetch past a modest TTL (an hour is enough to keep a session cheap), falling back to the cached copy when the fetch fails; add `--refresh` and document both in README and AGENT.md.\n- **CONFIRMED by running:** yes (code-verified; no TTL/refresh path exists, and repeated invocations never re-fetch).\n\n### 6. Garbled missing-credential message in `run`: \"no credential for security 1 scheme bearerAuth\"\n- **Severity: WARN**\n- **File:line:** `cmd/talaria/run.go:336-337` and `cmd/talaria/run.go:395-397`\n- **Description:** `pluralise(n, \"scheme\")` returns `\"1 scheme\"`, which is then interpolated *after* the word \"security\", producing `no credential for security 1 scheme bearerAuth (set $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER)` and `no credential for security 2 schemes bearerAuth (…), petKey (…)`. §3.1 requires errors that say \"what failed, why, valid alternatives\", and code 5's whole purpose (§4) is a sentence a human can act on. `cmd/talaria/auth.go:152-155` gets the same message right; only `run` is garbled, so the earlier fix did not cover this call site.\n- **Suggested fix:** `clierr.CredentialMissing(\"no credential for %s %s\", pluralise(len(r.missing), \"security scheme\"), strings.Join(r.missing, \", \"))` at both sites.\n- **CONFIRMED by running:** yes (`talaria run testdata/run.yaml` with no credentials exported).\n\n### 7. The emitted curl and `request.body` are lossy for non-UTF-8 request bodies\n- **Severity: WARN**\n- **File:line:** `internal/curl/render.go:114-125` (always `--data-raw string(req.Body.Data)`), vs `internal/curl/config.go:256-274` (`data-binary = \"@tempfile\"` for non-UTF-8 or >1 MiB)\n- **Description:** §3.4 promises \"Every executed call returns its curl equivalent: a portable reproduction\". For a body with invalid UTF-8 (`--body @image.png`), the wire path correctly stages a 0600 temp file and sends the bytes verbatim, but the *rendered* curl and the `request.body` field embed the bytes as a Go string, so JSON encoding replaces each invalid byte with U+FFFD — confirmed: `--data-raw '\\ufffdPNG\\r\\n\\u001a\\n\\ufffd\\ufffd\\u0000binary'`. Pasting the emitted command sends different bytes than talaria did. The earlier review fixed exactly this class for the corpus (commit 146dd1c, which now refuses lossy replays); the render path has the same defect. Secondary: a 2 MB body is inlined into `request.curl` in full, dropping 2 MB into the agent's context — the opposite of §3.2.\n- **Suggested fix:** in `bodyArgs`, when `!utf8.Valid(data)` or the body exceeds a display threshold, emit `--data-binary @<path>` with a placeholder path and a sibling `body_bytes`/`body_encoding: base64` field, mirroring what `corpus` already does; note the substitution in AGENT.md's Calling section.\n- **CONFIRMED by running:** yes.\n\n### 8. `run --report tsv` (and `pretty`) appends a prose summary line to TSV output\n- **Severity: INFO**\n- **File:line:** `cmd/talaria/run.go:539` (`rows = append(rows, []string{summaryLine(view.Summary)})`)\n- **Description:** README.md:456 states \"`tsv` prints bare tab-separated rows with no header line, for `cut` and `awk`\". `run --report tsv` emits a trailing single-column `5 operations: 1 passed, 2 failed, 2 skipped`, which `awk -F'\\t'` reads as a bogus record. (Same class, smaller: `describe --output tsv` emits the pretty prose block as single-cell rows — harmless, but not tab-separated data either.)\n- **Suggested fix:** drop the summary row from the TSV renderer and keep it in `pretty` only; or state the exception in README.\n- **CONFIRMED by running:** yes.\n\n### 9. README's status line claims a scaffolded command tree that does not exist\n- **Severity: INFO**\n- **File:line:** `README.md:6-9`\n- **Description:** \"…`talaria history` and `talaria run` work so far; **the rest of the command tree is scaffolded**.\" Nothing else is scaffolded — `twin` and its subcommands are not registered at all (`talaria twin` → exit 2, unknown command). AGENT.md:102 handles this correctly (\"designed but not yet shipped\").\n- **Suggested fix:** replace with \"the twin (Phases 5–8) is designed but not yet implemented\".\n- **CONFIRMED by