        "type": "text",
        "text": "Cleaned up. Here are the integration findings.\n\n---\n\n## What I verified works (so the negatives below are calibrated)\n\nI built `/tmp/talaria` and drove real flows against a recording `httptest`-style Go server, with an isolated `HOME`/`XDG_*` per case. Confirmed reaching the wire and back:\n\n- `--header` (including repeats), `--query` (including `&`-in-value escaping), `--param`, `--body` all reach curl and the server verbatim.\n- `--dry-run` curl is **byte-identical** to the executed call's curl, and pasting the emitted command into a shell with `$TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` set reproduces the exact request (tested with `'` and `&` in the body).\n- **Replay re-resolves SecretRefs rather than replaying `<redacted:…>`** — the classic CRIT is *not* present. Verified for bearer headers, header API keys, query API keys (`?api_key=qk-secret` went out on replay), cookie API keys, and basic auth (`-u` → `Basic dXNlcjpwdw==`). Bodies + content types round-trip exactly.\n- Swagger 2.0 → 3.x: `$ref`'d parameters, `formData`, query/basic/header `securityDefinitions`, `definitions` `$ref`s all survive to the wire and to the validator (a converted schema violation still produces exit 4). 3.x `components/parameters`, `components/requestBodies`, path-level `parameters` `$ref`s all resolve before the operation model reads them.\n- Exit codes propagate from the inner package to the process: spec load = 3 on *all seven* commands, curl failure = 1, usage = 2, validation = 4, missing credential = 5 (5 correctly beats 4 in `run`'s verdict).\n- One wedged endpoint does not cost the report: `run` with a 30s-hang endpoint and `--timeout 2` failed that operation and still ran and reported the other two, in 2.1s wall.\n- **Environment parity is genuinely good**: no `testing.Testing()`, no injected fake executor, no overridable `exec.Command` var. Every test drives the real binary and real `curl`.\n- **§5 boundary rules hold**: `go list -deps` confirms `operation`, `validate`, `gen` reach neither `curl` nor `corpus`. `SecretRef.Resolve()` has exactly two callers, both in `internal/curl` (`config.go:366`, `exec.go:269`).\n\n`go test ./...` is fully green while every finding below is live.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. Built-in response-body redaction silently fails on array-shaped bodies; the secret is written permanently to the history store\n**Severity: CRIT**\n**File:** `internal/secret/response.go:155` (`decodeObject` rejects any body not starting with `{`) and `internal/secret/response.go:190` (`redactPath` only descends into `map[string]any`)\n\n`ResponseRedactor.Body` returns the body **unchanged** if it is a top-level JSON array, and never descends into arrays nested under an object. So the built-in `access_token`/`refresh_token`/`id_token` paths — and any user-configured `redact.body-paths` — do nothing for two extremely common shapes. Because §5a mandates redaction at *write* time, the un-redacted value lands in `~/.local/state/talaria/history.jsonl`, which the design calls \"the highest-risk surface in the tool\".\n\n**CONFIRMED.** Server returning three shapes, config `redact: body-paths: [\"items.access_token\"]`:\n\n```\ngetToken   {\"access_token\": \"<redacted>\", \"expires_in\": 3600}      <- object: redacted\nlistTokens [{\"access_token\": \"TOPSECRET-ARR\"}]                     <- top-level array: LEAKED\ngetNested  {\"items\": [{\"access_token\": \"TOPSECRET-NEST\"}]}         <- nested array: LEAKED\n\n$ grep -o 'TOPSECRET-[A-Z]*' $H/state/talaria/history.jsonl | sort -u\nTOPSECRET-ARR\nTOPSECRET-NEST\n```\n\nWhy unit tests miss it: `internal/secret/response_test.go:127` `TestBodySkipsNonJSONBodies` **asserts the leak as correct behaviour** — it lists `[{\"access_token\":\"x\"}]` alongside `\"not json at all\"` and requires it be returned unchanged. The canary suite (`TestAnUnconfiguredResponseBodySecretIsNotRedacted`) only ever exercises an object body.\n\n**Suggested fix:** make `decodeObject` decode into `any` (accept `[` as well as `{`), and make `redactPath` fan out across `[]any` elements at each segment (`items.access_token` applies to every element of `items`). Rename/retarget `TestBodySkipsNonJSONBodies` and add an array case to the canary suite so this is gated.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Curl's error text is corrupted by the credential scrubber when the credential is a short/common string\n**Severity: WARN**\n**File:** `internal/curl/exec.go:257–284` (`scrubber`, `pairs = append(pairs, resolved, redacted)`)\n\n`scrubber` builds a `strings.Replacer` from the raw credential value with no minimum length or word-boundary guard, then applies it to curl's stderr. Any occurrence of the credential *as a substring* of an ordinary word is rewritten, destroying the diagnostic. This is the last surface an operator has when a call fails.\n\n**CONFIRMED** with `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=t`:\n\n```json\n{\"code\":1,\"message\":\"the request could not be completed: curl exited 7: curl: (7) Failed <redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER>o connec<redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER> <redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER>o 127.0.0.1 por<redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER> 1 af<redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER>er 0 ms: ...\"}\n```\n\nThe corrupted text also flows into `runResult.Reason` → the JSON report → the JUnit `<failure message=…>`.\n\n**Suggested fix:** skip values shorter than some floor (8 chars is a reasonable line for anything worth calling a credential) and, above it, keep the substring replace. If a short credential must be scrubbed, drop curl's detail entirely rather than emit mangled text — e.g. `if len(resolved) < minScrubbable { return the generic \"curl exited %d\" form }`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `--report junit` drops the per-field validation errors `internal/validate` computed\n**Severity: WARN**\n**File:** `cmd/talaria/run.go:429` (the reason is a *count*) and `cmd/talaria/run.go:553–567` (`junitSuite` copies only `res.Reason` into `output.Case.Failure`)\n\n`validate.Result.Errors` carries `{message, reason, field}` per violation and reaches the JSON report and the exit code correctly. It does **not** reach JUnit: the `<failure>` element gets only `\"the response violates the spec: 2 validation errors\"`. Since `--report` selects exactly one renderer, a CI job that asked for JUnit has no other artifact — the actionable data is computed, then discarded at the last boundary. `internal/output/junit.go:36–37` states the opposite intent: *\"the reason is the whole value of the report: 'getPet failed' sends someone to the logs, 'the server returned 500' does not.\"* For validation failures it is the former.\n\n**CONFIRMED:**\n```xml\n<testcase name=\"getBroken\" time=\"0.001\">\n  <failure message=\"the response violates the spec: 2 validation errors\"></failure>\n</testcase>\n```\nwhile the JSON report for the same run carries `missing property 'name'` and `got number, want string` at `$.id`.\n\n**Suggested fix:** add a `Detail string` to `output.Case`, emit it as the `<failure>` element's character data (`xml:\",chardata\"` — `xml.MarshalIndent` already escapes it), and fill it in `junitSuite` by joining `res.Validation.Errors` as `field: reason` lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4. History indices are positional and shift on every write, so an index read from `history` addresses a different entry after any intervening call\n**Severity: WARN**\n**File:** `cmd/talaria/history.go:271` (`newHistoryEntryView(len(entries)-i, entry)`) and `cmd/talaria/history.go:393` (`return index, entries[len(entries)-index], nil`)\n\nThe index is `len(entries) - position`, computed fresh on every read. `call`, `run` and `replay` all append, so every index in a previously-printed listing silently shifts. There is no stable identifier on `corpus.Entry` at all. The type comment at `history.go:28–31` reasons carefully about *filtering* not renumbering, but not about appending.\n\n**CONFIRMED.** Two calls recorded (index 1 = POST `createPetForm`, index 2 = GET `getPet`). Then:\n```\n$ talaria history replay 2      # replays the GET  — correct, appends an entry\n$ talaria history replay 1      # intended: the POST.  Actually replayed the GET again.\nserver saw: GET /pets/42?api_key=…   (twice, no POST)\n```\nAlso reproduced with `run`: a `call` at index 1 became index 2 after one `run`.\n\nThe e2e suite cannot catch this — step 7 replays index 1 exactly once, immediately after listing.\n\n**Suggested fix:** give `corpus.Entry` a stable id (monotonic counter, or the RFC3339Nano timestamp) written at append time; have `history` print it alongside the positional index and have `show`/`replay` accept it. At minimum, refuse the positional form when the store has grown since — but a stable handle is the real fix.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Fetched specs are cached forever with no revalidation, TTL, or CLI affordance to refresh\n**Severity: WARN**\n**File:** `internal/spec/source.go:85–90` (`loadURL` returns the cached bytes unconditionally when the file exists)\n\nA spec fetched by URL is keyed by `sha256(url)` and served from disk on every subsequent run, with no `If-None-Match`/`If-Modified-Since`, no expiry, and no `--refresh`/`--no-cache` flag anywhere in the CLI. The only remedy is manually deleting a hex-named file under `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/talaria/specs`. §3.3 sells the tool on \"The spec is the collection. There is no import step, no curation, no drift\" — the cache reintroduces exactly that drift, invisibly.\n\n**CONFIRMED:**\n```\nload1: {\"operations\":[{\"id\":\"opOne\",\"method\":\"GET\",\"path\":\"/one\",...}]}\n# upstream file replaced; curl confirms it now serves operationId: opTwo\nload2: {\"operations\":[{\"id\":\"opOne\",\"method\":\"GET\",\"path\":\"/one\",...}]}   # still opOne\n$ talaria --help | grep -iE 'cache|refresh'   # nothing\n```\n\n**Suggested fix:** store the `ETag`/`Last-Modified` alongside the body and issue a conditional GET (falling back to the cache on any network error), plus a `--refresh` persistent flag on the root command that bypasses the read.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Response-body redaction and response validation collide, producing spurious `run` failures and exit 4 with no signal that talaria substituted the value\n**Severity: WARN**\n**File:** `cmd/talaria/call.go:339–346` (`redactResponse`) → `cmd/talaria/call.go:390–398` (`validationInput` is built from the redacted view)\n\nValidation runs on the redacted body, so a redacted field is validated as the string `<redacted>`. `call.go:348–360` acknowledges the tradeoff and argues \"a spurious error is visible and correctable\" — but nothing on any surface marks the error as talaria-induced, and this is not opt-in: the built-in list already redacts `access_token`/`refresh_token`/`id_token`, so any spec that types one of those as non-string (or constrains it with `pattern`/`maxLength`) fails out of the box.\n\n**CONFIRMED** (spec types `expires_in: integer`, config redacts `expires_in`):\n```\nvalidation: {\"body_valid\":false,\"errors\":[\n  {\"message\":\"200 response body for '/token' failed to validate schema\",\n   \"reason\":\"got string, want integer\",\"field\":\"$.expires_in\"}]}\ncall --fail-on-error  exit=4\nrun  --fail-on-error  exit=4   \"1 operation failed\"\n```\nThe server's response was perfectly spec-compliant.\n\n**Suggested fix:** validate against the un-redacted bytes and pass the resulting `Error` list through the same scrubbing that already exists for curl's stderr — or, cheaper and sufficient, have `redactResponse` return the set of paths it rewrote and drop/annotate any `validate.Error` whose `Field` is one of them (`\"field redacted by talaria; not validated\"`).\n\n---\n\n### 7. `internal/corpus` depends on `internal/curl`, which will block the Phase 6 twin from using the store\n**Severity: WARN**\n**File:** `internal/corpus/entry.go:24` (`import \".../internal/curl\"`) and `internal/corpus/entry.go:143` (`NewEntry(source Source, req *request.Request, resp *curl.Response, red Redactors)`)\n\n§5 says corpus \"backs both `history` and the twin, so it is defined in Phase 2\". `internal/validate` handles the identical requirement correctly — `validate.go:5–9` explicitly refuses `http.Response` or anything from `internal/curl` and takes plain data. `corpus` does the opposite: its only entry-construction API is keyed to the outbound executor's `*curl.Response`, so the twin's record path (which will hold an `http.Response`) must synthesise a fake `curl.Response` to write an entry. `boundary_test.go` cannot catch this because corpus is in the `forbidden` list rather than the `shared` list, even though the design assigns it the same dual-consumer role.\n\n`corpus` also transitively imports `internal/config` (via `request`), directly contradicting its own package doc at `entry.go:12–15` (\"The package deliberately does not import internal/config\"). Confirmed with `go list -deps ./internal/corpus`.\n\n**Suggested fix:** give `corpus` a plain-data input mirroring `validate.Input` (status, headers, body, timing) and have `cmd/talaria` do the `curl.Response` → that-type conversion, exactly as it already does for `validate.Input` in `call.go:390`. Then move `internal/corpus` into `boundary_test.go`'s `shared` list.\n\n---\n\n### 8. JUnit reports 0.000s for the operation that consumed the entire run\n**Severity: INFO**\n**File:** `cmd/talaria/run.go:574–580` (`seconds()` returns 0 when `TimingMS == nil`)\n\nA timed-out operation has no `TimingMS`, so it is reported as taking no time. **CONFIRMED:** a `--timeout 2` run whose wall clock was 2.11s emitted `<testcase name=\"getSlow\" time=\"0.000\">` and `<testsuite … time=\"0.002\">`. The comment reasons about operations that \"never reached a server\" — correct for a skip, wrong for a timeout, which is precisely the case a CI timing report exists to surface.\n\n**Suggested fix:** record the elapsed wall time in `runner.execute` around `curl.ExecuteWith` and use it when curl returned no timing.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Repeated request headers are comma-folded through the corpus, so replay sends one header where the original sent two\n**Severity: INFO**\n**File:** `internal/corpus/entry.go:71` (`Headers map[string]string`) → `entry.go:191–194` (join with `\", \"`) → `cmd/talaria/history.go:583–596` (`replayPairs` restores one pair)\n\n**CONFIRMED:** original sent `X-Multi: one` + `X-Multi: two` as two headers; replay sent a single `X-Multi: one, two`. RFC 9110 field-value folding makes this equivalent for most headers, and the code comments say it is deliberate — but it is a real deviation from \"replay reissues the same request\", and there is no warning. Worth an explicit note in `AGENT.md` if it stays.\n\n**Suggested fix:** if fidelity matters, change `EntryRequest.Headers` to `map[string][]string` (as `EntryResponse.Headers` already is) and have `replayPairs` emit one `request.Pair` per value."
      }
