    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Committed as `854166a` — **35 findings (9 CRIT, 16 WARN, 10 INFO)**. No code was changed.\n\n## The 9 CRIT\n\nThree are credential leaks that defeat the product's core invariant (§5a: \"no consumer of stdout or stderr ever observes a credential value\"):\n\n1. **No `-q` on curl** — talaria never disables curl's default config file, so a planted `~/.curlrc` with `trace-ascii` writes the resolved `Authorization: Bearer …` to an attacker-chosen file. I reproduced this independently: stdout showed the correct `<redacted:env:…>` while the trace file held the plaintext token. This breaks the boundary in exactly the deployment §5a calls \"fully realised\" — writing one file is strictly weaker than the \"can read env vars\" capability the design already concedes.\n2. **Malformed `--header`/`--body` echoes the value** into the exit-2 JSON, shell-expanded. Compounded by #9: AGENT.md's headline example uses the colon syntax that triggers it.\n3. **URL userinfo** (`http://user:pass@host`) survives to stdout, the emitted curl, and permanently to `history.jsonl` — and it can arrive from a spec's `servers[0].url`, which is untrusted input.\n\nThe other six: `redact.headers` config protects the history file but *not* the stdout the agent reads (inverted); response-body redaction silently no-ops on array bodies, writing secrets to history — with a unit test that **asserts the leak as correct**; Ctrl-C orphans curl so the request completes post-mortem and leaves an unredacted response on disk; `history replay <n>` re-issues the wrong entry because indices shift on every write and replay itself appends; `talaria auth <typo>` exits 0; and AGENT.md documents a `--header` syntax the binary rejects, with a near-miss variant that silently corrupts the header.\n\n## Judgement calls worth flagging\n\n- **Promoted `history replay` to CRIT** (no reviewer ranked it there): two replays in a row is enough to trigger it, which is ordinary agent behaviour.\n- **Kept `run` not exiting 4 as INFO, not WARN.** The prior cycle called it a contradiction; DESIGN.md:248 is genuinely ambiguous and the implemented behaviour is defensible, so I wrote it as a wording fix rather than a code bug.\n- **Findings 13 and 14 are opposite defects in the same function** (`scrubber` misses some values; over-replaces short ones) — reported separately but flagged to fix together.\n\n## Carried forward\n\nThe prior cycle turned 11 CRITs into 9 tasks and fixed them — I re-verified all eleven hold. But its WARN/INFO findings were never actioned and `REVIEW_FINDINGS.md` was deleted, so six were about to be lost silently. They're back as findings 21, 22, 23, 25, 32, 34. Two of those show the earlier fixes were partial: the garbled credential message was fixed in `auth.go` but not `run.go`, and the unknown-command exit-2 fix landed on root but not subcommand groups.\n\n**Baseline is otherwise healthy:** build, `gofmt`, `vet` clean; all 15 packages green; `go test -race ./...` clean, including a cross-process corpus stress test (8 processes × 40 appends over the trim path, zero lost entries) that confirms the previous locking fix is sound. Environment parity is genuinely good — no fake executor, every test drives the real binary and real curl. One caveat on that: CI runs no `-race` (finding 17), so those guarantees aren't enforced."
      }
    ],
