        "text": "Task 5 done and committed. Stopping here.\n\n**What changed**\n- `internal/secret/response.go` — `decodeObject` → `decodeDocument`, which accepts an `[` root as well as `{` and decodes into `any`, keeping both existing guards (`dec.UseNumber()`, trailing-token `io.EOF`). `redactPath` now takes `any` and fans out across `[]any` *without consuming a path segment* — an array has no keys, so `data.token` reaches the token of every element of a `data` list and a top-level `access_token` reaches every element of a top-level array. Nested arrays under a rooted path (`items.access_token`) work the same way.\n- `internal/secret/response_test.go` — `TestBodySkipsNonJSONBodies` was asserting the leak as correct; `[{\"access_token\":\"x\"}]` moved out into two new tests that assert redaction, array shapes added to the no-op test, and a bare-string root plus an array-with-trailing-junk case added to the skip list.\n- `internal/canary/canary_test.go` — `TestAnArrayResponseBodyIsRedacted` drives both shapes end to end across every output format, with a marker field proving the body actually reached the surface.\n- `README.md` — documented that a `body-paths` path is rooted but arrays along the way cost it nothing.\n\n**Verification**\n- Red proof: the new unit tests failed with the canary in the output; with `response.go` stashed, the canary suite reported the value reaching 3 surfaces including `history/talaria/history.jsonl`.\n- `go test ./...` — all 15 packages pass.\n- `test -z \"$(gofmt -l .)\" && go vet ./...` — clean.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the plan's checklist reads as if `{\"items\":[{\"access_token\":\"…\"}]}` should be redacted with no config. It isn't, and shouldn't be — a dotted path is rooted, so the built-in top-level `access_token` doesn't name a key one object deep; making it match at arbitrary depth would be a different feature (recursive name search) that would also start redacting `{\"data\":{\"access_token\":…}}`, which today is deliberately not redacted. The nested canary case therefore configures `items.access_token`, which is the semantics the plan's own Steps section describes."
      }
    ],
