        "type": "text",
        "text": "Task 6 built, verified, and committed. Stopping here.\n\n**What changed**\n\n- `cmd/talaria/root.go` — `run` now installs `signal.NotifyContext` for SIGINT/SIGTERM and delegates to a new `runContext`, which uses `root.ExecuteContext`. Call sites (`call.go`, `run.go` via a new `runner.ctx` field, `history.go` replay) pass `cmd.Context()` down.\n- `internal/curl/exec.go` — `Execute`/`ExecuteWith` take a `context.Context`; the timeout context is derived from it, so cancellation and the deadline both kill curl through the existing `CommandContext` and every `defer` (capture cleanup, config cleanup) runs. A cancelled request now reports \"the request was cancelled before it completed\" rather than the timeout message.\n- `internal/curl/procgroup_unix.go` / `procgroup_other.go` — `Setpgid` plus a `cmd.Cancel` that signals `-pgid`, behind the same build-tag split `internal/corpus` uses for flock. Windows and darwin cross-compile clean.\n- `internal/curl/sweep.go` — `SweepStale()` at start-up removes only `talaria-call-*`/`talaria-body-*` entries in TMPDIR older than 24h (the SIGKILL backstop). Grace is deliberately generous: sweeping a live call's capture would break a working request, while an extra day costs nothing the SIGKILL hadn't already cost.\n\n**Verification** — new tests failed first for the right reasons (the cancelled call didn't return; the stale files survived the sweep), and the CLI-level test was confirmed to fail when the `cmd.Context()` wiring was temporarily reverted. `go test -count=1 ./...` and `go test -race ./...` are green, `gofmt`/`go vet` clean. README.md and AGENT.md gained a note on interruption behaviour."
      }
