        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01496TPzyfPPHHgo7Dpgmyny",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "366\ttalaria history replay 3\n367\t```\n368\t\n369\t`history` lists the store newest first: index, time, source, method, path, status, operation.\n370\t`--operation` filters by operationId, `--since` takes a duration (`30m`, `1h`, `168h`),\n371\t`--status` takes an exact code (`404`) or a class (`4xx`), and `--source` takes `call`, `run`\n372\tor `replay` — so a smoke run over a large spec does not bury the calls you made by hand. The\n373\tindex is an entry's position in the whole store, not in the filtered list, so it stays the\n374\tnumber `show` and `replay` take.\n375\t\n376\t`history show <n>` prints one entry in full; `history replay <n>` sends it again and records\n377\tthe result as a new entry, leaving the original alone. Replay resolves credentials from the\n378\tenvironment exactly as the original call did — history holds their *names*, so there is nothing\n379\tin the file to read back. It is gated the same way `call` is: replaying a `POST` needs\n380\t`--allow-mutations`. A header whose value was a literal talaria redacted by name cannot be\n381\treproduced, and replay says so on stderr rather than pretending it sent one.\n382\t\n383\tEntries are written **redacted, at write time**. The store is the highest-risk artifact talaria\n384\tproduces, so un-redacted recording is not an option and there is no flag for it. It lives at\n385\t`$XDG_STATE_HOME/talaria/history.jsonl` (falling back to `~/.local/state/talaria/`), directory"
      }
    ]
  },
  "parent_tool_use_id": null,
  "session_id": "29830245-be95-4300-94c2-abbd22bb63db",
  "uuid": "67d3297d-3cc1-4f6f-8516-21a9d1c3e801",
