        "content": "479\t## Errors and exit codes\n480\t\n481\tFailures go to stderr as a single line of JSON in the same versioned envelope, saying what\n482\tfailed, why, and — where there is a fixed set of right answers — what would have been valid:\n483\t\n484\t```json\n485\t{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"unknown output format \\\"xml\\\": valid values are json, pretty, tsv\",\"valid_alternatives\":[\"json\",\"pretty\",\"tsv\"]}}\n486\t```\n487\t\n488\tExit codes are deterministic, so agents can branch on them:\n489\t\n490\t| Code | Meaning |\n491\t|---|---|\n492\t| 0 | Success. An HTTP 4xx/5xx is still a successful *observation* for `call`; use `--fail-on-error` to change that |\n493\t| 1 | The request could not be completed (network, curl failure) |\n494\t| 2 | Usage error — unknown operation, missing required parameter, bad flag value |\n495\t| 3 | Spec parse/load error |\n496\t| 4 | Validation failure: the response violates the spec (only with `--fail-on-error` or `run`) |\n497\t| 5 | A required security scheme has no credential — distinct from a usage error so an agent can ask a human to set `$NAME` |\n498\t\n499\t## Building\n500\t\n501\tRequires Go 1.26+ and curl 7.70+ (curl is the execution engine; `--write-out '%{json}'` is\n502\trequired).\n503\t\n504\t```sh\n505\tgo build -o talaria ./cmd/talaria\n506\t./talaria version\n507\t```\n508\t"
