    "content": [
      {
        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01GR7FdX5k47bmJiuFgN7Hj9",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "297\t### Auth\n298\t\n299\tThe spec declares security schemes; credentials come from outside and are named, never shown:\n300\t- Env vars by convention: `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER`, `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC`, `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_<SCHEME>`.\n301\t- Profiles (`~/.config/talaria/config.yaml`, mode 0600): named sets of base-url + auth +\n302\t  headers, selected with `--profile`. Secrets in profiles may reference env vars.\n303\t- `auth check` reports which schemes are satisfied and from which *source* — never the value —\n304\t  so an agent can diagnose a broken auth setup blind and tell the human exactly what to set.\n305\t- v1 scope: bearer, basic, API key (header/query/cookie). OAuth flows out of scope (bring your\n306\t  own token); Restish shows the cost of doing them properly.\n307\t\n308\t## 5a. The credential firewall\n309\t\n310\t**Invariant: no consumer of stdout or stderr ever observes a credential value.**\n311\t\n312\tThe setup is asymmetric by design. A human (or CI) places secrets in env vars or a profile file\n313\tonce. The agent only ever names them. This is what makes it safe to point an agent at a\n314\tproduction API doc.\n315\t\n316\t### How secrets reach curl: the config document on curl's stdin\n317\t\n318\t**Primary mechanism: `curl -K -`.** curl reads a config document from stdin when the filename\n319\tis `-`. Secret-bearing headers, credentials, and the request body go in that document;\n320\t**nothing sensitive is ever an argv element.** This is the documented way to keep credentials\n321\tout of the process list — `/proc/*/cmdline` is readable by any process on the host, including\n322\tones the agent spawns.\n323\t\n324\t**Resolving stdin contention.** `--body -` (body from stdin) and `-K -` (config from stdin)\n325\tappear to collide. They do not:\n326\t\n327\t> **The Go process owns the real stdin. curl's stdin is always a fresh pipe that Go writes.**\n328\t\n329\t`--body -` is read to EOF by the Go process from *its* stdin. curl is then spawned with a new\n330\tpipe on *its* stdin, carrying the config document — including the body as a `data` directive.\n331\tOne stdin consumer at each level. For large or binary bodies, fall back to a 0600 temp file\n332\treferenced as `data = \"@/path\"`, deleted immediately after exec. The temp file is the exception.\n333\t\n334\t### Leak channels and countermeasures\n335\t\n336\t| Leak channel | Countermeasure |\n337\t|---|---|\n338\t| Emitted/dry-run curl commands | Always symbolic: `-H \"Authorization: Bearer $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\"`. Runnable where the env var exists; useless to exfiltrate |\n339\t| `request.headers` in JSON output | Sensitive headers rendered `<redacted:env:NAME>`. Built-in list (`Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Proxy-Authorization`, `*api*key*`, `*token*`, `*secret*`) + user-extensible. Applies to pretty output too |\n340\t| curl process argv (`ps`, `/proc/*/cmdline`) | Never argv. Config document on curl's stdin (above) |\n341\t| Response headers/bodies (`Set-Cookie`, login endpoints returning `access_token`) | Redact `Set-Cookie` and configurable response-body JSON paths by default; document that auth-issuing endpoints are for humans, not agents |\n342\t| **History** | Same redaction as output, applied at write time. History is a permanent artifact — it is the highest-risk surface in the tool |\n343\t| Error paths (curl stderr, validation errors quoting the request) | Errors are built from the redacted representation, never the raw one. Test explicitly — error paths are where redaction bugs live |\n344\t| Recordings / corpus | Redaction at **write** time, not read time. Un-redacted recording is not an option |\n345\t| Spec cache, verbose/debug logs | Debug output goes through the same redacted representation. No `--verbose` that bypasses it |\n346\t| Query-string API keys (`?api_key=`) | Symbolic in emitted curl, `<redacted>` in output URL fields. These still leak into *server* logs — warn once on stderr |\n347\t\n348\t**Architecture consequence.** Exactly one component (`internal/curl`, at exec time) ever holds\n349\tresolved secret values, and only long enough to write the config document. Everything else —\n350\toutput, validation, history, corpus, errors — operates on a `Request` whose secret fields are\n351\t*structurally* references (`SecretRef{EnvVar: \"…\"}`), not strings. Leaking then requires\n352\tdeliberately resolving a ref, which is greppable and reviewable. Forgetting to scrub a string\n353\tis neither.\n354\t\n355\t**Twin synergy.** The twin needs **zero real credentials**. It enforces that *a* credential is\n356\tpresent (auth realism) but accepts placeholders — so the agent's entire develop-and-test loop\n357\truns secret-free, and real credentials exist only in a final human- or CI-gated run.\n358\t\n359\t**Testing requirement.** A CI suite injects canary secrets through every auth mechanism and\n360\tgreps every output surface — json, pretty, tsv, dry-run, errors, junit reports, history store,\n361\tcorpus files, debug logs — for them. Redaction regressions fail the build. **This suite is\n362\tPhase 2 work and gates every release thereafter.**\n363\t\n364\t### Threats explicitly not covered\n365\t\n366\tHonesty about the boundary's limits:\n367\t- **A response body containing a secret** is returned to the agent, because the tool cannot know\n368\t  a field is sensitive unless configured. Mitigated by default `Set-Cookie` redaction and\n369\t  configurable body paths; not solved.\n370\t- **An agent that can read env vars or the profile file directly** bypasses everything here.\n371\t  This tool secures the *API-calling* path, not the machine. Running the agent without those\n372\t  vars in its environment — and letting the binary read them from a file it alone opens — is\n373\t  the deployment that fully realises the boundary.\n374\t- **Server-side logs** may record query-string keys regardless of what this tool does.\n375\t\n376\t### Test data for `run` mode\n377\t\n378\tPriority order: spec `example`/`examples` → user fixture files (`--fixtures dir/`, matched by\n379\toperationId) → schema-generated data (`gen`). Examples-first keeps requests realistic.\n380\t\n381\t## 6. The digital twin\n382\t\n383\t**Goal: mimic the real API, not just return schema-valid noise.**\n384\t\n385\t**What is table stakes and what is not.** Replay-from-recordings and stateful CRUD already exist\n386\tin mature products — Specmatic records real traffic into spec-validated mocks; WireMock does"
      }
    ]
  },
  "parent_tool_use_id": "toolu_01169B27FvFuehrb6E82u6Uu",
