    "prompt": "You are the SPEC-COMPLIANCE reviewer for the Go CLI project `talaria` at /home/casper/projects/talaria (branch `ralph/design`, base `main`).\n\n`main` contains only docs — the ENTIRE Go codebase on this branch is the diff. Get the changed-file list with `git diff --name-only main...HEAD` and read the source files directly (the diff is 29k lines).\n\nThe specification is `docs/design/DESIGN.md`. Read it COMPLETELY first. Also read `README.md`, `AGENT.md`, `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`, and `docs/research/2026-08-02-design-review.md` (conventions files).\n\nSCOPE: The branch implements Phases 1-4 of DESIGN.md §7 (list, describe, search, uses, call --dry-run, real execution + credential firewall + history, response validation, `run` smoke mode). Phases 5-8 (recording proxy, twin serve, stateful twin, fault injection) are DELIBERATELY out of scope — there is a decision gate after Phase 4. Do NOT report missing twin functionality as a finding.\n\nCheck, against DESIGN.md:\n- §4 CLI surface: does EVERY documented command, subcommand and flag exist with the documented name and semantics? Actually run the built binary (`go build -o /tmp/talaria ./cmd/talaria` then run it) to check `--help` output against the spec's flag list for `list`, `describe`, `search`, `uses`, `call`, `history`, `history show`, `history replay`, `run`, `auth check`, `version`.\n- §4 Exit codes table: verify each of 0,1,2,3,4,5 is produced in the documented circumstance. Test them for real.\n- §4 \"Output shape for call\": does the actual JSON match the sketched shape (`schema`, `request.curl`, `request.method`, `request.url`, `request.headers`, `response.status/headers/body/timing_ms`, `validation.status_documented/body_valid/errors`)? Field names matter — an agent's prompt breaks on drift.\n- §3 principles: `--output json` on EVERY command; pretty-on-TTY / JSON-when-piped default; never prompts, never pages; errors to stderr as structured JSON naming valid alternatives; `\"schema\": \"talaria/v1\"` present everywhere.\n- §5 architecture boundary rules: `operation`, `validate`, `gen` must NOT import `curl` or `twin`. Verify with `go list -deps` or grep of imports. `secret` resolves values for exactly one caller (internal/curl at exec time).\n- §5 Auth: env var conventions TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER / TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC / TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_<SCHEME>; profiles at ~/.config/talaria/config.yaml mode 0600; v1 scope bearer/basic/apikey in header/query/cookie — is cookie apikey actually implemented?\n- §5a: `curl -K -` config on stdin; the stdin-ownership rule for `--body -`; temp-file fallback for large/binary bodies at 0600 deleted after exec; the canary CI suite covering json/pretty/tsv/dry-run/errors/junit/history/corpus/debug.\n- §5 \"Test data for run mode\": priority order spec example/examples → --fixtures dir matched by operationId → gen.\n- Swagger 2.0 conversion fidelity (Phase 1 requirement), OpenAPI 3.0/3.1/3.2 support, JSON and YAML.\n- §7 Phase 2: AGENT.md v1 as a first-class deliverable — does it accurately describe the shipped binary? Every example in README.md and AGENT.md should actually work; try running them.\n- Drift: anything in the code that contradicts the doc without a recorded reason. Also anything in README/AGENT.md that contradicts the binary.\n\nA PRIOR review round already fixed 9 CRIT issues including: doc/binary mismatch on `--header` syntax (docs now say name=value), a command group with no subcommand now exits 2, history entries now have stable ids for replay. Verify those are consistent across ALL docs and ALL command groups (check `talaria history` and `talaria twin` if present, not just `talaria auth`).\n\nAlready-known, do NOT re-report unless now CRIT: DESIGN.md calls `search` \"fuzzy find\" but it is substring matching; README status line claims a scaffolded tree; DESIGN.md exit-4 row ambiguous for bare `run`; `run --report tsv` appends a prose summary line; JUnit drops per-field validation errors; JUnit reports 0.000s timing.\n\nReport findings as a numbered list. For each: Title, Severity (CRIT/WARN/INFO), File:line, Description, Suggested fix. CRIT = an unmet DESIGN.md requirement or behaviour that contradicts the doc in a way that would break an agent following AGENT.md. WARN = spec drift. Every finding needs a verified file:line. Verify by RUNNING the binary, not by reading alone.",
