    "content": [
      {
        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01RLDTgzRj4YfsJhXxmPS55q",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "1\t# talaria\n2\t\n3\t*Postman for agents. Point it at any API doc; your agent works the API and never sees\n4\tyour credentials.*\n5\t\n6\t> **Status: early implementation.** `talaria version`, `talaria list`, `talaria describe`,\n7\t> `talaria search`, `talaria uses`, `talaria call`, `talaria auth check`,\n8\t> `talaria history` and `talaria run` work so far; the rest of the command tree is scaffolded.\n9\t> The [design document](docs/design/DESIGN.md) is the source of truth.\n10\t\n11\tEvery API client — Postman, Insomnia, Bruno, curl itself — assumes the operator is a human who\n12\tis entitled to see their own secrets. Hand an agent a Postman collection with an environment, a\n13\t`.bru` file, or a shell with `$STRIPE_KEY` exported, and the agent has your key: in its context\n14\twindow, in the transcript, in the logs.\n15\t\n16\t**talaria is the trust boundary.** The agent operates on credential *names*; the binary\n17\tresolves *values* at the last possible moment and never emits them — not in output, not in\n18\terrors, not in the curl commands it prints, not in history.\n19\t\n20\tA single Go binary that turns any OpenAPI/Swagger spec into three things:\n21\t\n22\t1. **An explorable API client** — list, describe, search, and call operations, curl underneath.\n23\t2. **A spec-driven tester** — validate real responses against the contract, smoke-test whole APIs.\n24\t3. **A digital twin** — a local server that mimics the real API for safe, fast, offline testing.\n25\t\n26\tAgents discover an API progressively (`list` → `describe` → `call`), paying token cost only for\n27\twhat they need, instead of loading a 2MB `swagger.json` into context. Every request comes back\n28\tas a runnable curl command.\n29\t\n30\t## Design commitments\n31\t\n32\t- **Secrets never reach the agent.** There is no `--show-secrets` flag, by design.\n33\t- **No collection to maintain.** The spec *is* the collection — nothing to curate, nothing to\n34\t  drift, and it works against an API the agent has never seen before.\n35\t- **Safe by default.** Read-only unless `--allow-mutations`. The sanctioned way to test a\n36\t  `DELETE` is against the twin.\n37\t- **Not an MCP server.** Ever. The absence is the point.\n38\t\n39\t## Pointing talaria at a spec\n40\t\n41\tSpec-reading commands take an OpenAPI 3.x or Swagger 2.0 spec as a file path or an `http(s)` URL,\n42\tfrom whichever of these is set first:\n43\t\n44\t1. the positional argument\n45\t2. `--spec`\n46\t3. `$TALARIA_SPEC`\n47\t\n48\tSwagger 2.0 is converted to OpenAPI 3.x at load time, so every command sees one shape. Remote\n49\tspecs are cached under `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/talaria/specs` (falling back to `~/.cache/talaria/specs`)\n50\tand fetched once per URL, not once per call. Cache files are written `0600` in a `0700` directory.\n51\t\n52\t## Listing operations\n53\t\n54\t```sh\n55\ttalaria list https://petstore3.swagger.io/api/v3/openapi.json\n56\ttalaria list ./openapi.yaml --tag pets\n57\t```\n58\t\n59\tOne line per operation — method, path, operationId and a truncated summary — because the whole\n60\tpoint is that nobody pipes a 2 MB `swagger.json` into a context window. `--tag` filters to a\n61\tsingle tag; a tag nothing carries prints an empty list and exits 0, since \"nothing has that tag\"\n62\tis an answer rather than a failure.\n63\t\n64\tOperations the spec never named get a synthesised operationId derived from method and path\n65\t(`GET /pets/{petId}` → `getPetsByPetId`). It is stable across loads and as callable as an\n66\tauthored one.\n67\t\n68\t## Describing one operation\n69\t\n70\t```sh\n71\ttalaria describe ./openapi.yaml getPet\n72\ttalaria describe getPet --spec ./openapi.yaml --depth 2\n73\t```\n74\t\n75\tThe next step after `list`: the parameters, request body and declared responses of a single\n76\toperation. With one positional argument, that argument is the operationId and the spec comes\n77\tfrom `--spec` or `$TALARIA_SPEC`; with two, the spec comes first.\n78\t\n79\tSchemas render one field per line in the form `name*: (type) description`, where `*` marks a\n80\trequired field:\n81\t\n82\t```\n83\tGET /pets/{petId}  getPet\n84\tGet one pet\n85\t\n86\tParams:\n87\t  petId*: (string) [path] The pet's identifier\n88\t  verbose: (boolean) [query] Include the pet's history\n89\t\n90\tResponses:\n91\t  200 The pet\n92\t    application/json:\n93\t      id*: (string) Unique identifier\n94\t      status: (string) one of: available, pending, sold\n95\t      tags: ([]string)\n96\t      owner: (object)\n97\t        name: (string) Who owns the pet\n98\t```\n99\t\n100\tArrays show their element type as `([]string)`. Enums are listed inline. `allOf` branches are\n101\tmerged into one field list, since that is what a caller has to supply; `oneOf` and `anyOf`\n102\trender as a summary of their branch types (`(oneOf: object|string)`) rather than expanding\n103\teach branch.\n104\t\n105\tTwo things stop the output from growing without bound. A schema that refers back to one of its\n106\town ancestors — which real specs do constantly — ends at `[circular]` instead of recursing. A\n107\tschema nested deeper than `--depth` (6 by default) ends at `[max depth]`, so a truncated branch\n108\tis visibly truncated rather than silently absent.\n109\t\n110\t`--output json` returns the same information as a structured tree, not the rendered string.\n111\t\n112\t## Searching a spec\n113\t\n114\t```sh\n115\ttalaria search ./openapi.yaml invoice\n116\ttalaria search ./openapi.yaml invoice --kind schema\n117\t```\n118\t\n119\t`list` assumes you know roughly what you are looking at. `search` is for when you know the\n120\tconcept but not the endpoint. It matches a substring, case-insensitively, against three kinds of\n121\tthing at once, and labels each result with the kind it is:\n122\t\n123\t| Kind | Matched against | `where` reports |\n124\t|------|-----------------|-----------------|\n125\t| `operation` | operationId, path, summary, description | `GET /invoices` |\n126\t| `schema` | component schema name and description | `#/components/schemas/Invoice` |\n127\t| `param` | parameter name and description | the location: `query`, `path`, … |\n128\t\n129\t`--kind` restricts the search to one of them. Results are ranked with name matches first, then\n130\tpath matches, then prose, so the endpoint actually named after the concept leads. A term that\n131\tmatches nothing prints an empty result set and exits 0 — an agent probing for a concept the API\n132\tdoes not have has not made a usage error.\n133\t\n134\t## Finding what uses a schema\n135\t\n136\t```sh\n137\ttalaria uses ./openapi.yaml Pet\n138\t```\n139\t\n140\tThe reverse lookup: every operation that touches a schema, and where — in a parameter, in the\n141\trequest body, or in a response.\n142\t\n143\t```\n144\tGET  /pets          listPets    response:200  indirect\n145\tGET  /pets/{petId}  getPet      response:200  direct\n146\t```\n147\t\n148\tReferences are followed, so an operation returning a `PetList` that contains an array of `Pet` is\n149\treported for `Pet` and marked `indirect` — the cue that the fields will not be at the top level of\n150\tthe response. Reference cycles are handled; `Pet` referring to `Owner` referring back to `Pet` is\n151\tordinary in real specs. A schema name the spec does not define exits 2 with the closest valid\n152\tnames; a schema nothing references exits 0 with no operations.\n153\t\n154\t## Credentials and profiles\n155\t\n156\tCredentials are named, never shown. talaria maps each security scheme a spec declares onto an\n157\tenvironment variable by convention, and carries the *name* from there on:\n158\t\n159\t| Scheme in the spec | Environment variable |\n160\t|---|---|\n161\t| `type: http`, `scheme: bearer` | `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` |\n162\t| `type: http`, `scheme: basic` | `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC` (as `user:password`) |\n163\t| `type: apiKey`, named `petKey` | `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_PETKEY` |\n164\t\n165\tAPI keys are supported in all three locations — `header`, `query` and `cookie`. OAuth2 and\n166\tOpenID Connect flows are out of scope for v1: bring your own token and let a `bearer` scheme\n167\tcarry it. When a spec offers several alternative security requirements, talaria uses the first\n168\tone it can satisfy, so a spec offering \"OAuth2 or a bearer token\" resolves to the bearer token.\n169\t\n170\tFor more than one environment, `~/.config/talaria/config.yaml` holds named profiles selected\n171\twith `--profile`. `--profile` and `--base-url` are accepted by every command that makes\n172\trequests:\n173\t\n174\t```yaml\n175\tprofiles:\n176\t  staging:\n177\t    base-url: https://staging.example.com\n178\t    headers:\n179\t      X-Env: staging\n180\t    auth:\n181\t      bearerAuth: ${STAGING_TOKEN}\n182\t```\n183\t\n184\tA base URL must be `http` or `https`, whichever source it comes from — the flag, a profile, or\n185\tthe spec's `servers[0].url`. Anything else is a usage error, because curl also speaks `file`,\n186\t`gopher` and `smb`, and a spec talaria was pointed at is untrusted input.\n187\t\n188\tIt must also carry no credentials in its userinfo: `http://user:password@host` is a usage error\n189\tfrom every source, including a stored history entry on the way to a replay. A base URL is copied\n190\twhole into the request, the emitted `curl` and `history.jsonl`, so a password written there would\n191\tbe one talaria stores in cleartext forever. Use `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC=user:password` instead, which\n192\tstays a reference everywhere but on the wire.\n193\t\n194\tA profile may also switch its own recording off with `history: {enabled: false}` — see\n195\t[History](#history).\n196\t\n197\tA profile's `auth` entries may only *reference* an environment variable — `${VAR}` or `$VAR`.\n198\tA literal token in the file is refused, because a credential talaria can read from a config file\n199\tis a credential it would have to carry. The file names credentials and internal hosts, so it\n200\tmust be mode `0600` or stricter; anything looser exits 2 telling you to `chmod`. Unknown keys\n201\tare an error rather than silence, so a mistyped `baseurl:` does not quietly send the request\n202\tsomewhere else.\n203\t\n204\tThe same file extends the redaction lists. Both keys only ever *add* — nothing in a config file\n205\tcan stop talaria redacting an `Authorization` header:\n206\t\n207\t```yaml\n208\tredact:\n209\t  headers:\n210\t    - x-session-*\n211\t  body-paths:\n212\t    - data.token\n213\t```\n214\t\n215\t`headers` are globs over the header name, applied to request and response headers alike — and, as\n216\tthe built-in list is, to query parameters and cookies, which are credential locations under\n217\tanother name. They hide the value everywhere it is displayed: `request.headers`, the emitted\n218\tcurl, `--dry-run`, pretty output and history. They go on top\n219\tof the built-in list (`Authorization`, `Proxy-Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Set-Cookie`,\n220\t`*api*key*`, `*token*`, `*secret*`). `body-paths` are dotted JSON paths into a *response* body,\n221\ton top of the built-in `access_token`, `refresh_token` and `id_token`. A path is rooted at the\n222\ttop of the body, but arrays along the way cost it nothing: `data.token` reaches the token of every\n223\telement of `data` when `data` is a list, and a top-level `access_token` reaches every element of a\n224\ttop-level array. `call` reads this file\n225\twhether or not you passed `--profile`, because a security setting that only takes effect when\n226\tyou happen to be using a profile is one that silently does not.\n227\t\n228\t## Checking credentials without seeing them\n229\t\n230\t```sh\n231\ttalaria auth check ./openapi.yaml\n232\ttalaria auth check ./openapi.yaml --profile staging --output json\n233\t```\n234\t\n235\t`auth check` answers the one question an agent must be able to ask about credentials: is one\n236\tthere? It reports every security scheme the spec declares, the variable it comes from, and\n237\twhether that variable is set — never what it is set to.\n238\t\n239\t```json\n240\t{\"scheme\":\"bearerAuth\",\"source\":\"env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\",\"present\":true}\n241\t```\n242\t\n243\tThe source follows the same resolution `call` uses, so `--profile staging` reports the\n244\tprofile's variable (`env:STAGING_TOKEN`) rather than the convention's. Presence is tested with\n245\ta lookup; the value is never read.\n246\t\n247\tIt exits **5** when an operation in the spec has no credential to authenticate it with, naming\n248\teach scheme and the variable to export. Exit 5 is distinct from a usage error on purpose: it is\n249\tthe one failure whose fix is \"ask a human to set `$NAME`\" rather than \"correct the invocation\".\n250\tAn operation that accepts several alternatives is satisfied by any one of them, and schemes\n251\ttalaria cannot supply at all (OAuth2, OpenID Connect) are left out rather than reported missing.\n252\tThe report is printed either way — a code 5 with nothing to read would say what failed but not\n253\twhat to do.\n254\t\n255\t## Making a call\n256\t\n257\t```sh\n258\ttalaria call ./openapi.yaml getPet --param petId=42 --query verbose=true\n259\t```\n260\t\n261\t`call` binds parameters, headers and credentials to an operation, sends it through the system\n262\tcurl, and returns the request and the response as one structured block. `--param` takes a\n263\tparameter the operation declares, in any location; `--query` and `--header` add ones it does\n264\tnot, and all three repeat. Every binding problem is reported at once, so a wrong parameter name\n265\tand a missing required one arrive in the same message rather than one run apart.\n266\t\n267\t`--body` supplies a request body three ways: a literal (`--body '{\"name\":\"Rex\"}'`), a file\n268\t(`--body @pet.json`, read verbatim), or `--body -` to read talaria's own stdin. The bytes are\n269\tresolved in the Go process before curl exists, so a body on stdin and the credentials curl\n270\treads on *its* stdin never share a pipe. The content type is the `Content-Type` you set with\n271\t`--header`, otherwise the media type the operation declares.\n272\t\n273\tThe emitted command references credentials by environment-variable name and never by value:\n274\t\n275\t```\n276\tcurl -q -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\" 'https://api.example.com/v1/pets/42?verbose=true'\n277\t```\n278\t\n279\tIt is runnable wherever the variable is set and useless to exfiltrate. The leading `-q` mirrors\n280\tthe call talaria made: it stops curl reading `~/.curlrc`, so no directive in a file talaria did\n281\tnot write can act on the request carrying the credential. An API key that belongs\n282\tin the query string is symbolic there too; a `basic` scheme renders as curl's `-u\n283\t\"$TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC\"`, because the header form would need the value base64-encoded into it.\n284\t`--output json` returns the same request as a structured block, where credentials read\n285\t`<redacted:env:NAME>` — that field is read, not run. The real value goes on the wire and\n286\tappears in no output surface at all.\n287\t\n288\tA credential you supply yourself is treated the same way. `--header X-Api-Key=sk-live-…`,\n289\ta header from a profile, or a bound parameter whose name matches the redaction list all\n290\tdisplay as `<redacted>` in the request block *and* in the emitted curl, while still being sent\n291\tverbatim. The name decides, not where the value came from — so the emitted command for a\n292\tliteral credential is deliberately not copy-pasteable, and there is no flag that makes it so.\n293\t\n294\tAlongside it, `response` carries the status, headers, timing and body; a JSON body is embedded\n295\tas JSON rather than as a quoted string, so an agent parses the envelope once instead of twice.\n296\tPretty output prints the request line, the curl, and `200 OK in 143ms` — response headers stay\n297\tin `--output json`, since a `Set-Cookie` does not belong in someone's scrollback unasked.\n298\t\n299\tWhat comes back is redacted too, within the limits of what a tool can know. Response headers go\n300\tthrough the same name matcher as request headers, so `Set-Cookie` reads `<redacted>` by default;\n301\tresponse *bodies* are redacted only at the JSON paths you configure, plus the OAuth2 token\n302\tfields (`access_token`, `refresh_token`, `id_token`). A body in which nothing matched is\n303\treturned byte for byte, so what you read is what the server sent. This is a mitigation, not a\n304\tsolution: talaria cannot tell a secret field from an ordinary one by looking at it, so an\n305\tauth-issuing endpoint is for a human, not for an agent.\n306\t\n307\tOne thing redaction cannot reach: an API key that belongs in the *query string* travels in the\n308\tURL, and URLs are written to server access logs. talaria warns once on stderr when an operation\n309\tsends one — naming the parameter and the environment variable, never the value.\n310\t\n311\tTwo rules apply before anything is sent:\n312\t\n313\t- **Read-only by default.** `GET`, `HEAD` and `OPTIONS` need no flag; every other method\n314\t  requires `--allow-mutations` and exits 2 without it. The gate is checked before the request\n315\t  is even built, and `--dry-run` does not exempt it, so the rule is learnable without a\n316\t  network round trip.\n317\t- **`--dry-run` sends nothing.** It prints the same request block a real call would, minus the\n318\t  response, and exits 0. The `curl` field is identical either way.\n319\t\n320\tAn HTTP 4xx or 5xx is a successful observation and exits 0. Only a request that could not be\n321\tcompleted at all — a refused connection, a TLS failure — is exit 1.\n322\t\n323\tEvery request is bounded in time: 10 seconds to connect and 30 seconds in total by default,\n324\t`--timeout <seconds>` to change the total. An API that stops answering becomes an exit 1 with\n325\tcurl's status 28 in the message rather than a process that hangs.\n326\t\n327\tInterrupting talaria — Ctrl-C, or a `kill` — stops the request rather than leaving it running.\n328\tcurl is killed along with talaria, the call exits 1 saying it was cancelled, the attempt is still\n329\trecorded in history, and nothing of the response is left behind in your temp directory.\n330\t\n331\t## Validating what came back\n332\t\n333\tEvery executed call carries a `validation` block alongside the response:\n334\t\n335\t```json\n336\t\"validation\": { \"status_documented\": true, \"content_type_documented\": true, \"body_valid\": true, \"errors\": [] }\n337\t```\n338\t\n339\tThe three booleans answer independently, so \"the server returned an undocumented 500\" is\n340\tdistinguishable from \"the documented 200 came back with the wrong shape\" without parsing prose.\n341\tA schema failure produces one entry in `errors` per failing field, carrying the JSONPath of the\n342\toffending value, because the field is the actionable part. Pretty output adds a single line —\n343\t`validation: ok`, or `validation: 2 errors` — and leaves the messages to `--output json`.\n344\t\n345\tA violation is an observation, like a 404: the call still exits 0. `--fail-on-error` asks for\n346\tthe other behaviour, exiting **4** when the response is an HTTP error or violates the spec. It\n347\tchanges the exit code and nothing else — the full envelope is still on stdout, which is where\n348\tan agent goes to find out what actually failed. A dry run has no validation block; nothing came\n349\tback to check.\n350\t\n351\tValidation runs on the **redacted** response, not on the raw one. Validation errors quote the\n352\tcontent they rejected, so a validator fed the raw body would be the one place a secret\n353\treappeared after redaction removed it. The cost is that a redacted field is validated as\n354\t`<redacted>`, so redacting a field the schema constrains reports a violation the server did not\n355\tcommit — a visible, correctable error, which is the better failure of the two.\n356\t\n357\t## History\n358\t\n359\tEvery call talaria makes is recorded, so an agent can answer \"what have I already tried, and\n360\twhat came back\" without asking again:\n361\t\n362\t```sh\n363\ttalaria history\n364\ttalaria history --operation getPet --since 1h --status 4xx\n365\ttalaria history show 3\n366\ttalaria history replay 3\n367\t```\n368\t\n369\t`history` lists the store newest first: index, id, time, source, method, path, status, operation.\n370\t`--operation` filters by operationId, `--since` takes a duration (`30m`, `1h`, `168h`),\n371\t`--status` takes an exact code (`404`) or a class (`4xx`), and `--source` takes `call`, `run`\n372\tor `replay` — so a smoke run over a large spec does not bury the calls you made by hand. The\n373\tindex is an entry's position in the whole store, not in the filtered list, so a filter does not\n374\trenumber it — but a *write* does, and `call`, `run` and `replay` all write. The id is the handle\n375\tthat does not move: it is assigned once, at record time, and both `show` and `replay` prefer it\n376\tover an index. Entries written before ids existed have none and list as `-`.\n377\t\n378\t`history show <id|n>` prints one entry in full; `history replay <id|n>` sends it again and records\n379\tthe result as a new entry, leaving the original alone. Replay resolves credentials from the\n380\tenvironment exactly as the original call did — history holds their *names*, so there is nothing\n381\tin the file to read back. It is gated the same way `call` is: replaying a `POST` needs\n382\t`--allow-mutations`. A header whose value was a literal talaria redacted by name cannot be\n383\treproduced, and replay says so on stderr rather than pretending it sent one.\n384\t\n385\tEntries are written **redacted, at write time**. The store is the highest-risk artifact talaria\n386\tproduces, so un-redacted recording is not an option and there is no flag for it. It lives at\n387\t`$XDG_STATE_HOME/talaria/history.jsonl` (falling back to `~/.local/state/talaria/`), directory\n388\t`0700` and file `0600`, one JSON entry per line, keeping the most recent 1000 entries *per\n389\tsource* and truncating bodies at 64 KiB with an explicit `\"truncated\": true`. A body that is\n390\tnot valid UTF-8 — a protobuf, an image, a gzip stream — is stored base64-encoded with\n391\t`\"encoding\": \"base64\"`, because a JSON string would otherwise replace each byte it cannot hold\n392\tand a replay would send something the original call did not; `history show` reports such a body\n393\tas `<binary body, N bytes, base64 in --output json>` rather than printing it. Concurrent\n394\ttalaria processes can share one history file: each append takes an exclusive advisory lock on\n395\ta sibling `history.jsonl.lock` for the whole of the write and the retention trim, so an entry\n396\ttalaria reported as recorded is one you will find in the file.\n397\t\n398\tRecording is off for a profile that says so, and off everywhere when the environment says so:\n399\t\n400\t```yaml\n401\tprofiles:\n402\t  prod:\n403\t    history:\n404\t      enabled: false\n405\t```\n406\t\n407\t```sh\n408\tTALARIA_HISTORY=off talaria call ./openapi.yaml getPet --param petId=42\n409\t```\n410\t\n411\t`TALARIA_HISTORY` wins over the profile: the config file is what a user configured, the\n412\tvariable is what someone auditing a machine sets. Either way nothing is written — no entry, no\n413\tfile, no directory. A dry run is never recorded; a request that failed to complete is, with no\n414\tresponse block, because it is still something that was tried.\n415\t\n416\t## Smoke testing\n417\t\n418\t`run` is `call` over many operations at once — the command that answers \"is this whole API\n419\tbehaving?\" and the one a CI job invokes:\n420\t\n421\t```sh\n422\ttalaria run ./openapi.yaml --base-url http://localhost:9000\n423\ttalaria run ./openapi.yaml --tag pets --operation getPet --fail-on-error\n424\ttalaria run ./openapi.yaml --fixtures ./fixtures --allow-mutations --report json\n425\t```\n426\t\n427\t`--tag` and `--operation` both repeat and combine as a **union**; with neither, the whole spec\n428\truns. A filter matching nothing exits 2 rather than passing with nothing tested. Operations run\n429\tone at a time, in spec order — determinism beats speed, and parallel calls against a real API\n430\tare a surprise nobody asked for.\n431\t\n432\tTest data follows one priority chain: the spec's own `example`, then a fixture file, then\n433\tgeneration from the schema. `--fixtures dir/` supplies the middle one, matched by operationId —\n434\t`dir/createPet.json` holds `{\"params\": {…}, \"headers\": {…}, \"body\": {…}}`, every field optional.\n435\t\n436\tA generated body is always JSON, so `run` sends it as `application/json` — or as the operation's\n437\town JSON media type, `application/vnd.api+json` say — whatever else the spec lists first. An\n438\toperation that declares no JSON media type at all, such as a converted Swagger 2.0 `formData`\n439\toperation, still gets `application/json`, because that is what the bytes are; a server that only\n440\tspeaks XML answering 415 is a truer result than JSON labelled `application/xml`. A fixture\n441\t`headers` entry setting `Content-Type` wins, as `--header` does in `call`.\n442\t\n443\tEach operation is reported as `passed`, `failed` or `skipped`, with a `reason` for the last two\n444\tand a `summary` block counting all four numbers. A skip is not a failure: a `DELETE` left alone\n445\twithout `--allow-mutations`, or an operation whose required parameter nothing could supply, is\n446\tcorrect behaviour rather than a broken API. `--report json|pretty|tsv|junit` is `run`'s own\n447\tformat flag and overrides `--output`.\n448\t\n449\t`--timeout` applies per operation, not per suite. An endpoint that never answers is one failed\n450\tline in the report with curl's status 28 in its reason, so the run still finishes and CI still\n451\tgets its output.\n452\t\n453\t`--report junit` writes a JUnit XML suite — one `<testcase>` per operation, named by\n454\toperationId, with a `<failure>` or `<skipped>` child carrying the reason — which is what makes\n455\t`run` land in CI without glue:\n456\t\n457\t```sh\n458\ttalaria run ./openapi.yaml --base-url \"$STAGING\" --report junit > report.xml\n459\t```\n460\t\n461\t`junit` is a `--report` value only: a suite of operations is the one thing there is to render\n462\tas a test report, so `--output junit` exits 2.\n463\t\n464\tFailing operations exit 0 unless you pass `--fail-on-error`, which makes them exit 4. A missing\n465\tcredential exits 5 either way — that operation was never tested, and the fix is exporting a\n466\tvariable, not reading a report. Every request goes into history with `\"source\": \"run\"`, under\n467\tits own 1000-entry cap, so a run over a large spec cannot bury the calls you made by hand.\n468\t\n469\t## Output\n470\t\n471\tEvery command takes `--output json|pretty|tsv`. With no flag, talaria prints `pretty` when\n472\tstdout is a terminal and `json` when it is piped — an explicit `--output` always wins.\n473\t\n474\tEvery JSON payload carries a top-level `\"schema\": \"talaria/v1\"` field. It is versioned so agent\n475\tprompts keep working; it only changes on a breaking change to the output shape. JSON output is\n476\tdeterministic — the same result renders byte-identically every time. `tsv` prints bare\n477\ttab-separated rows with no header line, for `cut` and `awk`.\n478\t\n479\t## Errors and exit codes\n480\t\n481\tFailures go to stderr as a single line of JSON in the same versioned envelope, saying what\n482\tfailed, why, and — where there is a fixed set of right answers — what would have been valid:\n483\t\n484\t```json\n485\t{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"unknown output format \\\"xml\\\": valid values are json, pretty, tsv\",\"valid_alternatives\":[\"json\",\"pretty\",\"tsv\"]}}\n486\t```\n487\t\n488\tExit codes are deterministic, so agents can branch on them:\n489\t\n490\t| Code | Meaning |\n491\t|---|---|\n492\t| 0 | Success. An HTTP 4xx/5xx is still a successful *observation* for `call`; use `--fail-on-error` to change that |\n493\t| 1 | The request could not be completed (network, curl failure) |\n494\t| 2 | Usage error — unknown operation, a command group named without its subcommand (`talaria auth`), missing required parameter, bad flag value |\n495\t| 3 | Spec parse/load error |\n496\t| 4 | Validation failure: the response violates the spec (only with `--fail-on-error` or `run`) |\n497\t| 5 | A required security scheme has no credential — distinct from a usage error so an agent can ask a human to set `$NAME` |\n498\t\n499\t## Building\n500\t\n501\tRequires Go 1.26+ and curl 7.70+ (curl is the execution engine; `--write-out '%{json}'` is\n502\trequired).\n503\t\n504\t```sh\n505\tgo build -o talaria ./cmd/talaria\n506\t./talaria version\n507\t```\n508\t\n509\tThe version string defaults to `dev` and is stamped at release time:\n510\t\n511\t```sh\n512\tgo build -ldflags \"-X main.version=v0.1.0\" -o talaria ./cmd/talaria\n513\t```\n514\t\n515\tRun the tests with `go test ./...`.\n516\t\n517\t`internal/canary` is the leak suite: it builds the binary, drives every auth mechanism —\n518\tbearer, basic, and an API key in a header, a query parameter and a cookie — through every\n519\tcommand and every output format, and greps every byte talaria emits or writes for the injected\n520\tsecret, raw, percent-encoded and base64. It is the check that gates a release, so a failure\n521\tthere is a redaction regression, not a flaky test. Run it alone with\n522\t`go test ./internal/canary/...`.\n523\t\n524\tCI runs the build, the lint and the whole suite on every push to `main` and every pull request\n525\t([.github/workflows/ci.yml](.github/workflows/ci.yml)), which is what makes the leak suite a\n526\tgate rather than something to remember. The workflow runs the commands recorded in\n527\t`.ralph/stack.json` and nothing else; `internal/ci` fails if the two drift apart or if the\n528\tpinned Go version stops matching `go.mod`.\n529\t\n530\t## Driving talaria from an agent\n531\t\n532\t[AGENT.md](AGENT.md) is the operating manual for the LLM on the other end: the\n533\tlist→search→describe→dry-run→call loop, the exit codes and what to do about each, and — the part\n534\tno other API client has to answer — how to name a credential you cannot see. Hand it to the model\n535\tas a system prompt or a skill file.\n536\t\n537\tIt is tested like code. `cmd/talaria/agentdoc_test.go` checks the doc's exit-code table against\n538\tthe `clierr` constants, every command it names against the registered command tree, and every\n539\t`TALARIA_*` variable against the ones talaria actually reads, so a manual that drifts from the\n540\tbinary fails the build. It also *runs* the examples: every fenced `talaria …` line is executed\n541\tagainst a fixture spec, under `--dry-run` where the command has it, and a usage error fails the\n542\tbuild — because a documented invocation the parser rejects is one an agent will copy.\n543\t\n544\t## The name\n545\t\n546\t*Talaria* — the winged sandals of Hermes. The tool isn't the messenger; the agent is. This is\n547\twhat it wears to move fast.\n548\t\n549\tSee [DESIGN.md](docs/design/DESIGN.md) for the architecture, threat model, and roadmap.\n550\t\n551\t## License\n552\t\n553\tMIT — see [LICENSE](LICENSE).\n554\t"
      }
    ]
  },
