          "subagent_type": "general-purpose",
          "run_in_background": true,
          "prompt": "You are the CONCURRENCY reviewer for the Go CLI project `talaria` at /home/casper/projects/talaria (branch `ralph/design`, base `main`).\n\n`main` contains only docs — the ENTIRE Go codebase on this branch is the diff. Get the file list with `git diff --name-only main...HEAD` and read source files directly.\n\nWhere concurrency actually lives in this codebase:\n- `internal/curl/exec.go` — spawns the curl subprocess, pipes stdin/stdout/stderr, handles context cancellation and signals, process groups (procgroup_unix.go / procgroup_other.go), temp-file cleanup, `sweep.go`.\n- `internal/curl/version.go` — a preflight subprocess.\n- `internal/corpus/store.go` + `lock_unix.go` / `lock_other.go` — file locking for concurrent history appends and trims; read-modify-write races between processes.\n- `cmd/talaria/run.go` — `talaria run` smoke mode; check whether it runs operations concurrently and whether shared state (report aggregation, corpus writes, counters) is synchronized.\n- `cmd/talaria/call.go`, `cmd/talaria/history.go` — signal handling, cleanup ordering.\n- `internal/spec/source.go` — remote spec fetch + local cache writes.\n\nChecklist:\n- Data races on shared state; unsynchronized map/slice access. RUN THE RACE DETECTOR: `go test -race ./...` and report anything it finds. Also try stress: `go test -race -count=5 ./internal/corpus/... ./internal/curl/... ./cmd/talaria/...`.\n- Goroutine leaks — is every spawned goroutine guaranteed to exit? Look for goroutines writing to unbuffered channels no one reads after an early return, and for pipe-copy goroutines not waited on before `cmd.Wait()`.\n- Subprocess lifecycle: is curl always reaped? Any path where Wait() is skipped, or where the process group is killed but the goroutine copying its output blocks forever? Deadlocks between writing curl's stdin (config document) and reading its stdout when the config is larger than the pipe buffer (64KB) — a large request body could deadlock.\n- Context cancellation honoured: timeouts (--timeout), signal handling (SIGINT/SIGTERM). Is the raw response capture file always removed on cancellation? Are cleanup handlers idempotent and race-free?\n- Errors swallowed instead of propagated (ignored `err` from Wait, Close, Remove, Sync, flock).\n- File-lock correctness: is the lock held across the full read-modify-write? Is it released on every path including panic/early return? Does `lock_other.go` (non-unix) silently no-op?\n- TOCTOU on temp files, cache files, and the history file rename.\n\nA PRIOR review round fixed: signal handling so a killed talaria cancels curl instead of orphaning it and does not leak the raw capture (commit 94a68c3), and serialised history appends so trim cannot destroy entries (commit e0cceae). VERIFY those fixes are actually correct — read `git show 94a68c3` and `git show e0cceae`. An incomplete fix is a CRIT.\n\nAlready known, do NOT re-report unless you find them to be CRIT: no fsync before the rename in corpus replace; CI does not run the race detector; `curl --version` preflight has no timeout; lock_other.go disables locking on non-unix.\n\nReport findings as a numbered list. For each: Title, Severity (CRIT/WARN/INFO), File:line, Description (concrete failure scenario: what inputs/state produce what wrong outcome), Suggested fix. Every finding needs a verified file:line. Prefer findings you can DEMONSTRATE with a test or the race detector over ones you reason about."
        },
