  "prompt": "You are the INTEGRATION reviewer for the Go CLI project `talaria` at /home/casper/projects/talaria (branch `ralph/design`, base `main`).\n\n`main` contains only docs — the ENTIRE Go codebase on this branch is the diff. Get the file list with `git diff --name-only main...HEAD` and read source files directly. Read `docs/design/DESIGN.md` for what the components are supposed to do.\n\ntalaria is a multi-component pipeline. Your job is to verify data actually FLOWS correctly across component boundaries — not that each component works in isolation. Do not assume; VERIFY by running the built binary end-to-end against a real local HTTP server you spin up (write a small Go or python test server in /tmp) and by reading the wiring code.\n\nThe chains to verify:\n1. spec load → convert (Swagger 2.0 → 3.x) → operation model → describe/list/search/uses. Does a converted 2.0 spec produce the same operation fields as a native 3.0 one (params, body schema, security schemes, response contracts)? Does security-scheme info survive conversion (see internal/spec/testdata/swagger2-apikey.json)?\n2. operation + config/auth + flags → internal/request build → internal/curl config document → real curl → response parse. Does what `--dry-run` prints actually match what gets sent on the wire? Test both: run a call against a local server that echoes the request it received, and compare against the dry-run output. Mismatches here are CRIT — the emitted curl is a documented product feature.\n3. response → internal/validate → the `validation` block in output → exit code 4 with `--fail-on-error`. Does redaction (internal/secret/response.go) mutate the body BEFORE validation sees it, producing spurious failures? Which order is it actually in?\n4. call → internal/corpus write → `history` list → `history show` → `history replay`. Does a replayed request reproduce the original (method, path, query, headers, body, media type)? What is lost in the round trip? Does replay re-resolve secrets symbolically or send garbage?\n5. `run` → per-operation test data (spec examples → --fixtures → internal/gen) → call → report (json/junit/pretty/tsv). Does the fixture matching by operationId actually work? Do validation errors reach the report?\n6. internal/canary/surfaces.go → canary_test.go: does the canary suite actually enumerate every output surface DESIGN.md §5a requires (json, pretty, tsv, dry-run, errors, junit, history store, corpus files, debug logs)? A surface it claims to cover but does not actually exercise is a CRIT — it is the release gate.\n7. Environment parity: does behaviour differ between `go test` and a real terminal? TTY detection for pretty-vs-json default, env vars, HOME/XDG paths for config, cache and history. Do tests stub something that hides a real-runtime bug (e.g. tests inject a fake curl but production uses system curl)?\n8. .github/workflows/ci.yml — does CI actually run what it claims (build, lint, test, canary suite)? Does the lint step actually fail on unformatted code?\n\nAlso check: internal/e2e/e2e_test.go and boundary_test.go — do they test the real loop or a mocked stand-in?\n\nA PRIOR review round fixed 9 CRIT issues; verify these end-to-end rather than trusting them: `curl -q` now passed (does it break anything that relied on curlrc?); base-URL userinfo rejected (does a legitimate URL still work?); redact.headers now applied to displayed request AND emitted curl (does the emitted curl remain runnable?); history entries now have stable ids (does `history show <id>` and `history replay <id>` accept the id AND is the old positional form handled sanely?).\n\nAlready known, do NOT re-report unless CRIT: internal/corpus imports internal/curl (blocks Phase 6 twin); repeated request headers comma-folded through the corpus; remote-spec cache never expires; `history replay` lacks the query-string credential warning; garbled \"no credential for security 1 scheme bearerAuth\" message in run.go.\n\nReport findings as a numbered list. For each: Title, Severity (CRIT/WARN/INFO), File:line, Description (the concrete broken chain: what produces what, and where it fails to land), Suggested fix. Every finding needs a verified file:line. Prioritise findings you DEMONSTRATED by running the binary.",
