    "stdout": ".ralph/ralph-20260802-195025.log:2773\n.ralph/ralph-20260802-154350.log:898\n---\n[19:50:25] PHASE 3/5 — BUILD\n[19:53:41] PHASE 4/5 — REVIEW\n[22:34:51] PHASE 5/5 — PULL REQUEST\n--- generic ---\n    \"stdout\": \"commit d1eecd0a082285c9fe5d948a4b304f82f974df6c\\nAuthor: Teeeep <casperjantepe@gmail.com>\\nDate:   Sun Aug 2 15:40:01 2026 +0000\\n\\n    docs: reposition as talaria — Postman for agents\\n    \\n    Positioning: an API client you can hand to an agent. The credential\\n    firewall moves from a design principle to the product's reason to exist.\\n    Postman, Insomnia, Bruno, Hurl and Apidog enter the landscape as the\\n    actual incumbents under this framing; Bruno (44k stars, May 2026) is the\\n    one to beat on developer sentiment, and none of them are trying to solve\\n    the credential problem.\\n    \\n    Adds history, which unifies 'what did I call' with the twin's corpus and\\n    gives the useful half of request chaining via replay. Adds exit code 5\\n    for missing credentials so agents can act on it. Documents the threats\\n    the firewall does not cover.\\n    \\n    Name resolved to talaria — Hermes's winged sandals. The tool is not the\\n    messenger; the agent is.\\n---PREV FINDINGS---\\n## Finding 1: curl reads `~/.curlrc` on every call — one writable file exfiltrates every resolved credential\\n- **Severity:** CRIT\\n- **File:** internal/curl/config.go:103\\n## Finding 2: A malformed `--header`/`--body` flag echoes the credential verbatim into the structured error\\n- **Severity:** CRIT\\n- **File:** internal/request/build.go:311\\n## Finding 3: Credentials in a URL's userinfo reach stdout, the emitted curl, and the permanent history file in cleartext\\n- **Severity:** CRIT\\n- **File:** internal/request/build.go:144\\n## Finding 4: `redact.headers` from the config file never reaches the displayed request or the emitted curl\\n- **Severity:** CRIT\\n- **File:** internal/request/build.go:80\\n## Finding 5: Built-in response-body redaction silently fails on array-shaped bodies; the secret is written permanently to history\\n- **Severity:** CRIT\\n- **File:** internal/secret/response.go:155\\n## Finding 6: Killing talaria orphans curl, leaks an unredacted response capture, and lets the request complete post-mortem\\n- **Severity:** CRIT\\n- **File:** internal/curl/exec.go:69\\n## Finding 7: `history` indices are positional and shift on every write, so `replay <n>` re-issues the wrong request\\n- **Severity:** CRIT\\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/history.go:271\\n## Finding 8: `talaria auth` and `talaria auth <typo>` exit 0 with help on stdout\\n- **Severity:** CRIT\\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/auth.go:30\\n## Finding 9: AGENT.md and README document `--header 'Name: value'`; the binary only accepts `name=value`, and a near-miss silently corrupts the header\\n- **Severity:** CRIT\\n- **File:** AGENT.md:54\\n## Finding 10: No `globoff` — a glob in a spec-supplied URL path turns one authenticated call into N\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/curl/config.go:132\\n## Finding 11: Request bodies are recorded with only the three RFC 6749 *response* token fields redacted\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/corpus/entry.go:232\\n## Finding 12: The built-in sensitive-name list misses common credential spellings, including `Authentication`\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/secret/redact.go:19\\n## Finding 13: `scrubber` misses name-matched sensitive literals, so curl's stderr is only scrubbed for structural secrets\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/curl/exec.go:265\\n## Finding 14: `scrubber` corrupts curl's error text when the credential is a short or common string\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/curl/exec.go:257\\n## Finding 15: The curl-timeout fix does not cover the preflight subprocess; `curl --version` can hang talaria forever\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/curl/version.go:42\\n## Finding 16: `replace` is not crash-safe: no fsync before the rename, so a crash mid-trim can destroy the whole history file\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/corpus/store.go:265\\n## Finding 17: CI never runs the race detector, so the concurrency tests guarding the corpus fix cannot catch a regression\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** .github/workflows/ci.yml:38\\n## Finding 18: `--report junit` drops the per-field validation errors `internal/validate` computed\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:553\\n## Finding 19: Response redaction and response validation collide, producing spurious validation failures and exit 4\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/call.go:390\\n## Finding 20: `internal/corpus` depends on `internal/curl`, which will block the Phase 6 twin from using the store\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/corpus/entry.go:24\\n## Finding 21: `history replay` issues query-string credentials without the §5a warning\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/history.go:209\\n## Finding 22: Garbled missing-credential message in `run`: \\\"no credential for security 1 scheme bearerAuth\\\"\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:336\\n## Finding 23: The remote-spec cache never expires and has no refresh escape hatch\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/spec/source.go:86\\n## Finding 24: The emitted curl and `request.body` are lossy for non-UTF-8 request bodies\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/curl/render.go:114\\n## Finding 25: The canary suite still omits the response-validation error path on a premise that is now stale\\n- **Severity:** WARN\\n- **File:** internal/canary/canary_test.go:423\\n## Finding 26: `lock_other.go` silently disables all locking on non-unix, reintroducing the trim data-loss bug\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** internal/corpus/lock_other.go:11\\n## Finding 27: `curl` inherits talaria's whole environment, contradicting the comment that says it does not\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** internal/curl/exec.go:40\\n## Finding 28: `run --report tsv` appends a prose summary line to otherwise machine-readable output\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:539\\n## Finding 29: README's status line claims a scaffolded command tree that does not exist\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** README.md:6\\n## Finding 30: DESIGN.md calls `search` \\\"fuzzy find\\\"; it is case-insensitive substring matching\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** docs/design/DESIGN.md:169\\n## Finding 31: An unrecognised `openapi:` major version loads as an empty spec and exits 0\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** internal/spec/load.go:1\\n## Finding 32: `exclusiveMaximum` is carried by the strict-3.0 fixture but never asserted\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** internal/validate/testdata/strict-3.0.yaml:40\\n## Finding 33: JUnit reports 0.000s for the operation that consumed the entire run\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:574\\n## Finding 34: Repeated request headers are comma-folded through the corpus, so replay sends one header where the original sent two\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** internal/corpus/entry.go:71\\n## Finding 35: DESIGN.md's exit-4 row is ambiguous about whether bare `run` should exit 4\\n- **Severity:** INFO\\n- **File:** docs/design/DESIGN.md:248\",\n        \"content\": \"297\\t### Auth\\n298\\t\\n299\\tThe spec declares security schemes; credentials come from outside and are named, never shown:\\n300\\t- Env vars by convention: `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER`, `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC`, `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_<SCHEME>`.\\n301\\t- Profiles (`~/.config/talaria/config.yaml`, mode 0600): named sets of base-url + auth +\\n302\\t  headers, selected with `--profile`. Secrets in profiles may reference env vars.\\n303\\t- `auth check` reports which schemes are satisfied and from which *source* — never the value —\\n304\\t  so an agent can diagnose a broken auth setup blind and tell the human exactly what to set.\\n305\\t- v1 scope: bearer, basic, API key (header/query/cookie). OAuth flows out of scope (bring your\\n306\\t  own token); Restish shows the cost of doing them properly.\\n307\\t\\n308\\t## 5a. The credential firewall\\n309\\t\\n310\\t**Invariant: no consumer of stdout or stderr ever observes a credential value.**\\n311\\t\\n312\\tThe setup is asymmetric by design. A human (or CI) places secrets in env vars or a profile file\\n313\\tonce. The agent only ever names them. This is what makes it safe to point an agent at a\\n314\\tproduction API doc.\\n315\\t\\n316\\t### How secrets reach curl: the config document on curl's stdin\\n317\\t\\n318\\t**Primary mechanism: `curl -K -`.** curl reads a config document from stdin when the filename\\n319\\tis `-`. Secret-bearing headers, credentials, and the request body go in that document;\\n320\\t**nothing sensitive is ever an argv element.** This is the documented way to keep credentials\\n321\\tout of the process list — `/proc/*/cmdline` is readable by any process on the host, including\\n322\\tones the agent spawns.\\n323\\t\\n324\\t**Resolving stdin contention.** `--body -` (body from stdin) and `-K -` (config from stdin)\\n325\\tappear to collide. They do not:\\n326\\t\\n327\\t> **The Go process owns the real stdin. curl's stdin is always a fresh pipe that Go writes.**\\n328\\t\\n329\\t`--body -` is read to EOF by the Go process from *its* stdin. curl is then spawned with a new\\n330\\tpipe on *its* stdin, carrying the config document — including the body as a `data` directive.\\n331\\tOne stdin consumer at each level. For large or binary bodies, fall back to a 0600 temp file\\n332\\treferenced as `data = \\\"@/path\\\"`, deleted immediately after exec. The temp file is the exception.\\n333\\t\\n334\\t### Leak channels and countermeasures\\n335\\t\\n336\\t| Leak channel | Countermeasure |\\n337\\t|---|---|\\n338\\t| Emitted/dry-run curl commands | Always symbolic: `-H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\\\"`. Runnable where the env var exists; useless to exfiltrate |\\n339\\t| `request.headers` in JSON output | Sensitive headers rendered `<redacted:env:NAME>`. Built-in list (`Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Proxy-Authorization`, `*api*key*`, `*token*`, `*secret*`) + user-extensible. Applies to pretty output too |\\n340\\t| curl process argv (`ps`, `/proc/*/cmdline`) | Never argv. Config document on curl's stdin (above) |\\n341\\t| Response headers/bodies (`Set-Cookie`, login endpoints returning `access_token`) | Redact `Set-Cookie` and configurable response-body JSON paths by default; document that auth-issuing endpoints are for humans, not agents |\\n342\\t| **History** | Same redaction as output, applied at write time. History is a permanent artifact — it is the highest-risk surface in the tool |\\n343\\t| Error paths (curl stderr, validation errors quoting the request) | Errors are built from the redacted representation, never the raw one. Test explicitly — error paths are where redaction bugs live |\\n344\\t| Recordings / corpus | Redaction at **write** time, not read time. Un-redacted recording is not an option |\\n345\\t| Spec cache, verbose/debug logs | Debug output goes through the same redacted representation. No `--verbose` that bypasses it |\\n346\\t| Query-string API keys (`?api_key=`) | Symbolic in emitted curl, `<redacted>` in output URL fields. These still leak into *server* logs — warn once on stderr |\\n347\\t\\n348\\t**Architecture consequence.** Exactly one component (`internal/curl`, at exec time) ever holds\\n349\\tresolved secret values, and only long enough to write the config document. Everything else —\\n350\\toutput, validation, history, corpus, errors — operates on a `Request` whose secret fields are\\n351\\t*structurally* references (`SecretRef{EnvVar: \\\"…\\\"}`), not strings. Leaking then requires\\n352\\tdeliberately resolving a ref, which is greppable and reviewable. Forgetting to scrub a string\\n353\\tis neither.\\n354\\t\\n355\\t**Twin synergy.** The twin needs **zero real credentials**. It enforces that *a* credential is\\n356\\tpresent (auth realism) but accepts placeholders — so the agent's entire develop-and-test loop\\n357\\truns secret-free, and real credentials exist only in a final human- or CI-gated run.\\n358\\t\\n359\\t**Testing requirement.** A CI suite injects canary secrets through every auth mechanism and\\n360\\tgreps every output surface — json, pretty, tsv, dry-run, errors, junit reports, history store,\\n361\\tcorpus files, debug logs — for them. Redaction regressions fail the build. **This suite is\\n362\\tPhase 2 work and gates every release thereafter.**\\n363\\t\\n364\\t### Threats explicitly not covered\\n365\\t\\n366\\tHonesty about the boundary's limits:\\n367\\t- **A response body containing a secret** is returned to the agent, because the tool cannot know\\n368\\t  a field is sensitive unless configured. Mitigated by default `Set-Cookie` redaction and\\n369\\t  configurable body paths; not solved.\\n370\\t- **An agent that can read env vars or the profile file directly** bypasses everything here.\\n371\\t  This tool secures the *API-calling* path, not the machine. Running the agent without those\\n372\\t  vars in its environment — and letting the binary read them from a file it alone opens — is\\n373\\t  the deployment that fully realises the boundary.\\n374\\t- **Server-side logs** may record query-string keys regardless of what this tool does.\\n375\\t\\n376\\t### Test data for `run` mode\\n377\\t\\n378\\tPriority order: spec `example`/`examples` → user fixture files (`--fixtures dir/`, matched by\\n379\\toperationId) → schema-generated data (`gen`). Examples-first keeps requests realistic.\\n380\\t\\n381\\t## 6. The digital twin\\n382\\t\\n383\\t**Goal: mimic the real API, not just return schema-valid noise.**\\n384\\t\\n385\\t**What is table stakes and what is not.** Replay-from-recordings and stateful CRUD already exist\\n386\\tin mature products — Specmatic records real traffic into spec-validated mocks; WireMock does\"\n          \"prompt\": \"You are the SPEC-COMPLIANCE reviewer for the Go CLI project `talaria` at /home/casper/projects/talaria (branch `ralph/design`, base `main`).\\n\\n`main` contains only docs — the ENTIRE Go codebase on this branch is the diff. Get the changed-file list with `git diff --name-only main...HEAD` and read the source files directly (the diff is 29k lines).\\n\\nThe specification is `docs/design/DESIGN.md`. Read it COMPLETELY first. Also read `README.md`, `AGENT.md`, `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`, and `docs/research/2026-08-02-design-review.md` (conventions files).\\n\\nSCOPE: The branch implements Phases 1-4 of DESIGN.md §7 (list, describe, search, uses, call --dry-run, real execution + credential firewall + history, response validation, `run` smoke mode). Phases 5-8 (recording proxy, twin serve, stateful twin, fault injection) are DELIBERATELY out of scope — there is a decision gate after Phase 4. Do NOT report missing twin functionality as a finding.\\n\\nCheck, against DESIGN.md:\\n- §4 CLI surface: does EVERY documented command, subcommand and flag exist with the documented name and semantics? Actually run the built binary (`go build -o /tmp/talaria ./cmd/talaria` then run it) to check `--help` output against the spec's flag list for `list`, `describe`, `search`, `uses`, `call`, `history`, `history show`, `history replay`, `run`, `auth check`, `version`.\\n- §4 Exit codes table: verify each of 0,1,2,3,4,5 is produced in the documented circumstance. Test them for real.\\n- §4 \\\"Output shape for call\\\": does the actual JSON match the sketched shape (`schema`, `request.curl`, `request.method`, `request.url`, `request.headers`, `response.status/headers/body/timing_ms`, `validation.status_documented/body_valid/errors`)? Field names matter — an agent's prompt breaks on drift.\\n- §3 principles: `--output json` on EVERY command; pretty-on-TTY / JSON-when-piped default; never prompts, never pages; errors to 