d semantics? Actually run the built binary (`go build -o /tmp/talaria ./cmd/talaria` then run it) to check `--help` output against the spec's flag list for `list`, `describe`, `search`, `uses`, `call`, `history`, `history show`, `history replay`, `run`, `auth check`, `version`.\\n- §4 Exit codes table: verify each of 0,1,2,3,4,5 is produced in the documented circumstance. Test them for real.\\n- §4 \\\"Output shape for call\\\": does the actual JSON match the sketched shape (`schema`, `request.curl`, `request.method`, `request.url`, `request.headers`, `response.status/headers/body/timing_ms`, `validation.status_documented/body_valid/errors`)? Field names matter — an agent's prompt breaks on drift.\\n- §3 principles: `--output json` on EVERY command; pretty-on-TTY / JSON-when-piped default; never prompts, never pages; errors to stderr as structured JSON naming valid alternatives; `\\\"schema\\\": \\\"talaria/v1\\\"` present everywhere.\\n- §5 architecture boundary rules: `operation`, `validate`, `gen` must NOT import `curl` or `twin`. Verify with `go list -deps` or grep of imports. `secret` resolves values for exactly one caller (internal/curl at exec time).\\n- §5 Auth: env var conventions TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER / TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC / TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_<SCHEME>; profiles at ~/.config/talaria/config.yaml mode 0600; v1 scope bearer/basic/apikey in header/query/cookie — is cookie apikey actually implemented?\\n- §5a: `curl -K -` config on stdin; the stdin-ownership rule for `--body -`; temp-file fallback for large/binary bodies at 0600 deleted after exec; the canary CI suite covering json/pretty/tsv/dry-run/errors/junit/history/corpus/debug.\\n- §5 \\\"Test data for run mode\\\": priority order spec example/examples → --fixtures dir matched by operationId → gen.\\n- Swagger 2.0 conversion fidelity (Phase 1 requirement), OpenAPI 3.0/3.1/3.2 support, JSON and YAML.\\n- §7 Phase 2: AGENT.md v1 as a first-class deliverable — does it accurately describe the shipped binary? Every example in README.md and AGENT.md should actually work; try running them.\\n- Drift: anything in the code that contradicts the doc without a recorded reason. Also anything in README/AGENT.md that contradicts the binary.\\n\\nA PRIOR review round already fixed 9 CRIT issues including: doc/binary mismatch on `--header` syntax (docs now say name=value), a command group with no subcommand now exits 2, history entries now have stable ids for replay. Verify those are consistent across ALL docs and ALL command groups (check `talaria history` and `talaria twin` if present, not just `talaria auth`).\\n\\nAlready-known, do NOT re-report unless now CRIT: DESIGN.md calls `search` \\\"fuzzy find\\\" but it is substring matching; README status line claims a scaffolded tree; DESIGN.md exit-4 row ambiguous for bare `run`; `run --report tsv` appends a prose summary line; JUnit drops per-field validation errors; JUnit reports 0.000s timing.\\n\\nReport findings as a numbered list. For each: Title, Severity (CRIT/WARN/INFO), File:line, Description, Suggested fix. CRIT = an unmet DESIGN.md requirement or behaviour that contradicts the doc in a way that would break an agent following AGENT.md. WARN = spec drift. Every finding needs a verified file:line. Verify by RUNNING the binary, not by reading alone.\",\n        \"content\": \"1\\t# talaria — Design Document\\n2\\t\\n3\\t*Status: draft v0.3 · repositioned 2026-08-02 · pre-implementation*\\n4\\t\\n5\\t> **Name resolved: `talaria`** — the winged sandals of Hermes. The tool is not the messenger;\\n6\\t> the agent is. This is what it wears to move fast. Fixes `cmd/talaria`, the binary on `$PATH`,\\n7\\t> `TALARIA_AUTH_*` env vars, and the `\\\"schema\\\": \\\"talaria/v1\\\"` output field.\\n8\\t\\n9\\t**Changes from v0.2:** repositioned around a single idea — *an API client you can hand to an\\n10\\tagent*. The credential firewall moves from a design principle to the product's reason to exist\\n11\\t(§1, §2, §5a). Postman / Insomnia / Bruno enter the landscape as the actual incumbents (§2).\\n12\\t`history` added, unifying \\\"what did I call\\\" with the twin's corpus (§4, §5, §7). Name settled.\\n13\\t\\n14\\t---\\n15\\t\\n16\\t## 1. Vision\\n17\\t\\n18\\t**Postman and Insomnia for agents.**\\n19\\t\\n20\\tPoint it at any API's OpenAPI/Swagger doc and an agent can explore, call, and test that API —\\n21\\t**without ever being given your credentials.**\\n22\\t\\n23\\tThat second clause is the product. Every existing API client — Postman, Insomnia, Bruno, curl\\n24\\titself — assumes the operator is a human who is entitled to see their own secrets. Hand an\\n25\\tagent a Postman collection with an environment, or a `.bru` file, or a shell with\\n26\\t`$STRIPE_KEY` exported, and the agent has your key. It is in the context window, in the\\n27\\ttranscript, in the logs, and on whatever server processed the request.\\n28\\t\\n29\\tThis tool is the trust boundary. The agent operates on credential *names*; the binary resolves\\n30\\t*values* at the last possible moment and never emits them. See §5a — it is the core of the\\n31\\tdesign, not a hardening pass.\\n32\\t\\n33\\tThree capabilities, from one spec, in one binary:\\n34\\t\\n35\\t1. **An explorable API client** — list, describe, search, and call operations, curl underneath.\\n36\\t2. **A spec-driven tester** — validate real responses against the contract; smoke-test whole APIs.\\n37\\t3. **A digital twin** — a local server that mimics the real API for safe, fast, offline testing.\\n38\\t\\n39\\t**One-line pitch:** *Postman for agents. Point it at any API doc; your agent works the API\\n40\\tand never sees your credentials.*\\n41\\t\\n42\\t### Why an agent needs a different client\\n43\\t\\n44\\tPostman's value to a human is persistence and organisation: saved requests, curated\\n45\\tcollections, shared workspaces. An agent needs none of that — it re-derives what it needs from\\n46\\tthe spec on every run. What an agent needs instead, and what no existing client provides:\\n47\\t\\n48\\t| Agent need | Why existing clients fail it |\\n49\\t|---|---|\\n50\\t| **Never see credentials** | Every client assumes a human operator who owns the secrets |\\n51\\t| **Progressive discovery** | A collection or spec must be loaded whole; a 2MB `swagger.json` is a context bomb |\\n52\\t| **No setup step** | Collections must be built and maintained before they are useful |\\n53\\t| **Deterministic, parseable results**",
